values
  ($q$cs-307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A profile of an inventor spends its opening paragraph describing a broken kitchen gadget she tinkered with as a child, then details her later career, and closes by returning to a redesigned version of that same gadget, now sold worldwide, without stating the connection directly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing paragraph functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It creates a deliberate parallel with the opening anecdote, implicitly connecting her early curiosity to her later success.","It introduces a new critique of her business practices.","It shifts the focus entirely away from her inventions to her personal life.","It contradicts the claim made in the opening paragraph."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing paragraph revisits the gadget imagery from the opening 'without stating the connection directly,' linking her origin story to her career, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on tides opens by describing a dramatic high tide that stranded boats on a beach, then explains the gravitational interaction between Earth and the moon that produces tides generally.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains the general phenomenon behind the specific event described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the description given in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated astronomical event.","It proves that tides never strand boats."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence accounts for the stranded boats by explaining the gravitational cause of tides generally, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay's final paragraph returns to a question about why a weaving pattern survived centuries of change, this time answering it directly after several paragraphs documenting the pattern's uses across generations.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final paragraph?$q$, $q$["It resolves the question raised at the start of the essay, now supported by the evidence presented.","It introduces a brand-new argument unrelated to the rest of the essay.","It undermines the evidence presented earlier in the essay.","It restates the essay's introduction without adding new information."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final paragraph directly answers the question from the introduction, now backed by the evidence built up through the essay — a resolving function.$q$),
  ($q$cs-310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A science article begins by asserting that deep-sea creatures cannot communicate without sound, then describes the discovery of fish species that flash coordinated light patterns to signal each other, using the finding to overturn the assumption stated at the outset.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the description of the fish in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a counterexample that overturns the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It confirms the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It qualifies the opening claim as true only for creatures living below a certain depth.","It summarizes the article's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states directly that the finding is used 'to overturn the assumption stated at the outset' entirely, not to narrow its depth range, making choice A the accurate description.$q$),
  ($q$cs-311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A museum essay on ceramic glazes opens by describing the glassy sheen visible on a finished bowl, then explains that the sheen results from minerals in the glaze melting and fusing to the clay at specific kiln temperatures.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It reveals the chemical process behind the visual effect described first.","It disputes the claim that the bowl has a glassy sheen.","It argues that the sheen is purely decorative with no chemical basis.","It shifts the essay's focus to an unrelated ceramic tradition."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains the chemistry behind the sheen described first, adding explanation rather than disputing or ignoring it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article opens by describing a single orchid's unusually shaped bloom, then broadens to examine how that shape evolved to attract one specific pollinator species, and finally discusses what the orchid's decline suggests about its pollinator's population.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a specific example to broader evidence to a wider implication.","It presents two competing theories and evaluates each in turn.","It begins with a general claim and ends with a specific anecdote.","It compares two unrelated flower species throughout the article."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text follows a clear progression: one orchid, then its evolved relationship with a pollinator, then a broader implication, moving from specific to general.$q$),
  ($q$cs-313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on early railroads spends its first two paragraphs celebrating the speed with which they moved goods across a continent, then devotes its final paragraph to noting how those same railroads displaced communities whose land lay along the new routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final paragraph in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It complicates the earlier celebration by introducing a less positive consequence of the same development.","It repeats the praise offered in the first two paragraphs without adding anything new.","It shifts the topic entirely away from railroads and displaced communities.","It proves that railroads had no effect on the communities involved."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After celebrating the railroads' speed, the final paragraph introduces a displacing effect of the same development, complicating rather than repeating the earlier praise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A history of navigation devotes its opening section to star-based wayfinding used by early seafarers, its middle section to the mechanical sextants of recent centuries, and its final section to the satellite systems that largely replaced both.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the development of a practice chronologically from ancient methods to modern technology.","It presents a single case study and analyzes it from multiple perspectives.","It begins with modern systems and works backward to ancient methods.","It argues that ancient methods were more accurate than modern systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three sections move in chronological order from ancient star-based methods to sextants to satellite systems, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on bookbinding opens with a description of thread being pulled tight through folded pages, then explains the tension principles that keep a hand-sewn binding from loosening over decades.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains the mechanical reasoning behind the technique described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the description given in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated bookbinding tradition.","It proves that hand-sewn bindings always loosen quickly."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains why the thread tension works, accounting for the technique the first sentence describes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A report on public libraries opens with a single branch's rising foot traffic after it added free tutoring sessions, then presents citywide circulation data showing similar increases at other branches that added community programming.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the citywide data in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It generalizes a single branch's result into a citywide pattern that strengthens the report's claim.","It contradicts the claim made by the single branch's example.","It defines technical terms related to library administration.","It criticizes branches that did not add new programming."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The citywide data extends the single branch's result to a broader pattern, strengthening rather than contradicting the report's claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on sign language interpreting acknowledges the common view that interpreting is a simple word-for-word substitution, then spends the remainder of the piece detailing the split-second grammatical restructuring interpreters perform, using this detail to complicate the initial view.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a simplified view of interpreting with evidence of its real complexity","To argue that interpreting requires no specialized skill","To argue that word-for-word substitution is in fact the most accurate way to interpret","To defend one interpreter against professional criticism"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay starts with the word-for-word view, then uses detail about split-second grammatical restructuring to complicate, not confirm, that view, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A biology chapter opens with a time-lapse description of a tadpole's tail disappearing, then uses that image throughout the chapter to illustrate the broader principle of programmed cell death.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening description in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a vivid, concrete image that anchors an abstract biological principle discussed later.","It proves that all amphibians undergo identical metamorphosis.","It argues against studying programmed cell death.","It states the chapter's conclusion before any explanation is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using a vivid image to introduce and later illustrate an abstract principle is a common structural device, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on furniture restoration opens by describing a chair's wobbling leg, then explains how a loosened glue joint, invisible from the outside, was the actual cause of the wobble.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It identifies the underlying cause of the problem described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the description given in the first sentence.","It introduces an unrelated piece of furniture.","It proves the chair's wobble could not be fixed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reveals the hidden cause of the wobble described first, functioning as explanation rather than contradiction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The op-ed on wind energy acknowledges that turbines can disrupt local bird migration before spending the remaining paragraphs describing turbine placement studies that minimize this disruption without reducing power output.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Conceding a real drawback before showing how it can be mitigated without sacrificing the main benefit","Ignoring the drawback entirely to avoid weakening the argument","Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument","Avoiding any clear thesis so that readers can draw their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The op-ed concedes the migration drawback, then shows how placement studies mitigate it, a concession-and-mitigation strategy matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A profile of a composer begins with an extended account of a public failure at her first major premiere, an event she says reshaped how she approached revision. The piece then details her later career, and closes by returning to a triumphant premiere decades later, noting the echo between the two nights without stating it directly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing paragraph functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It creates a deliberate parallel with the opening anecdote, implicitly connecting early failure to later success.","It introduces a new critique of her later compositions.","It introduces a second composer's triumphant premiere for comparison.","It contradicts the claim made in the opening paragraph."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing paragraph revisits her own premiere imagery from the opening 'without stating it directly,' linking her early failure to her later triumph, not introducing a different composer, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Sleep researcher A found that moderate caffeine intake improved reaction times in sleep-deprived adults.

Text 2: Sleep researcher B found no improvement in reaction times from the same dose of caffeine, but tested only well-rested adults, a group less likely to show caffeine's benefits in the first place.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's results do not directly contradict Researcher A's findings, since the two studies tested different populations.","Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.","Both researchers studied grip strength rather than reaction time.","Researcher A found that caffeine had no effect on reaction time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because Researcher B tested well-rested adults rather than sleep-deprived ones, B's null result does not directly contradict A's finding in a different population.$q$),
  ($q$cs-323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Astronomer A argues a distant exoplanet could host liquid water because its orbit places it within its star's habitable zone.

Text 2: Astronomer B argues the same planet is unlikely to host liquid water because its thin atmosphere, measured via spectroscopy, would let any surface water evaporate into space.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Astronomer B would likely respond to Astronomer A's claim?$q$, $q$["B would argue that the planet's thin atmosphere undermines the habitability suggested by its orbital position.","B would agree that the planet almost certainly hosts liquid water.","B would claim that the planet's orbit lies outside the habitable zone.","B would argue that spectroscopy cannot measure planetary atmospheres."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Astronomer B's evidence concerns atmospheric thinness, which would counter A's habitable-zone argument by allowing water to evaporate despite the favorable orbit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that the stirrup's adoption revolutionized medieval warfare by allowing mounted knights to deliver far more powerful lance strikes.

Text 2: Historian B argues that the stirrup's military impact has been overstated, noting that heavily armored cavalry had already proven effective in battles recorded before the stirrup's widespread use.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A credits a single technological change, while Historian B points to pre-existing effectiveness that predates it.","Both historians agree the stirrup had no military significance.","Historian B relies exclusively on accounts of lance strikes.","Historian A argues that cavalry was ineffective even after the stirrup's adoption."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A credits the stirrup itself, while Historian B points to cavalry effectiveness that existed before the stirrup — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Animal behaviorist A found dogs reliably follow a human pointing gesture to locate hidden food.

Text 2: Animal behaviorist B found wolves raised in similar conditions rarely follow the same gesture, suggesting the skill may be unique to domestication.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two behaviorists?$q$, $q$["Behaviorist B's finding highlights a difference between dogs and their wild relatives that supports a domestication-based explanation.","Both behaviorists studied the exact same species.","Behaviorist A found that wolves outperform dogs at following gestures.","Behaviorist B's results exactly match Behaviorist A's results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Behaviorist B's finding that wolves rarely follow the gesture, unlike dogs, supports the idea that domestication explains the skill, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Workplace researcher A found open-office employees reported lower productivity amid ambient chatter.

Text 2: Workplace researcher B found that employees given noise-canceling headphones in the same offices reported productivity comparable to private-office workers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Researcher B's finding relates to Researcher A's finding?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the noise itself, rather than the open layout alone, may explain the productivity drop Researcher A observed.","It proves that open offices always outperform private offices.","It was conducted in an entirely different type of workplace.","It shows that ambient chatter improved productivity in Researcher A's study."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Removing noise through headphones restored productivity, suggesting noise, not the open layout itself, may drive the effect Researcher A observed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Paleobotanist A argues that a fossilized leaf pattern indicates the plant grew in a seasonally dry climate, based on comparisons with modern drought-adapted leaves.

Text 2: Paleobotanist B argues the same leaf pattern more likely reflects insect damage rather than climate adaptation, citing similar marks found on leaves from consistently wet fossil sites.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two paleobotanists' explanations?$q$, $q$["Paleobotanist A attributes the pattern to climate, while Paleobotanist B attributes it to insect damage.","Both paleobotanists agree the leaf grew in a consistently wet climate.","Paleobotanist B relies exclusively on modern drought-adapted leaves.","Paleobotanist A argues that insects caused the leaf pattern."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Paleobotanist A attributes the leaf pattern to climate, while Paleobotanist B attributes the same pattern to insect damage — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Conservator A argues that damaged paintings should be restored to their original appearance as closely as possible, using historical records to guide repainting.

Text 2: Conservator B argues that visible gaps should be left where original paint is missing, so viewers can distinguish restoration from the artist's original work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two conservators' views?$q$, $q$["Conservator B's approach prioritizes transparency about restoration over the seamless appearance Conservator A favors.","Both conservators agree that damaged areas should always be left blank.","Conservator A argues against using historical records at all.","Conservator B recommends repainting missing areas invisibly."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Conservator B's visible-gap approach favors transparency about what is original, in contrast to Conservator A's preference for a seamless restored appearance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Marine researcher A found that a particular coral species survived an unusual heat wave better than nearby species.

Text 2: Marine researcher B found that the same coral species died at the same rate as neighboring species during a different, more prolonged heat wave the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's finding suggests the coral's apparent resilience in Researcher A's study may not hold under longer heat exposure.","Researcher B's findings exactly confirm Researcher A's results.","Both researchers studied the coral's reaction to cold water.","Researcher A found the coral died faster than neighboring species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The coral's resilience seen in Researcher A's shorter heat wave did not hold during Researcher B's more prolonged one, qualifying the earlier finding.$q$),
  ($q$cs-330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Engineer A argues autonomous vehicles are already safer than human drivers on highways, citing lower crash rates per mile in highway testing.

Text 2: Engineer B argues the comparison is misleading, since autonomous vehicles are tested mainly in favorable weather and traffic conditions that human drivers must also navigate in far harsher circumstances.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Engineer B would likely respond to Engineer A's claim?$q$, $q$["B would argue that the favorable testing conditions make the crash-rate comparison unfair to human drivers.","B would agree the comparison is entirely fair and accurate.","B would claim autonomous vehicles have never been tested on highways.","B would argue that human drivers perform worse in all conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Engineer B's objection is that testing conditions favor autonomous vehicles, making the crash-rate comparison to human drivers in all conditions unfair.$q$),
  ($q$cs-331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Labor economist A argues that a recent minimum wage increase in a mid-sized city caused measurable job losses in the restaurant sector within six months.

Text 2: Labor economist B argues that the same city's restaurant employment data show no significant change once seasonal hiring patterns are accounted for.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two economists' explanations?$q$, $q$["Economist A attributes a change in employment to the wage increase, while Economist B attributes the apparent change to seasonal patterns.","Both economists agree the wage increase caused no change in employment.","Economist B argues the wage increase caused significant job losses.","Economist A relies exclusively on seasonal hiring data."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist A credits the wage increase for job losses, while Economist B credits seasonal hiring patterns for the apparent change — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues Norse sailors navigated primarily using sun-compass devices, pointing to artifacts resembling sundials found in ship burials.

Text 2: Historian B argues the artifacts are more likely decorative or calendrical objects, noting none show the wear patterns expected from frequent navigational use.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A interprets the artifacts as navigational tools, while Historian B interprets them as serving a different, non-navigational purpose.","Both historians agree the artifacts were never placed aboard ships.","Historian B argues the artifacts show heavy navigational wear.","Historian A claims the artifacts are purely decorative."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A sees the artifacts as navigational tools, while Historian B sees them as decorative or calendrical — a difference in the artifacts' purpose, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Ornithologist A found young birds raised without hearing adult song develop abnormal, simplified songs as adults.

Text 2: Ornithologist B found that young birds raised in isolation but later exposed briefly to recordings of adult song developed nearly normal songs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two ornithologists?$q$, $q$["Ornithologist B's finding suggests brief exposure to adult song may be enough to prevent the abnormal development Ornithologist A observed.","Both ornithologists found that isolation has no effect on song development.","Ornithologist A found that recordings have no effect on song development.","Ornithologist B's findings exactly replicate Ornithologist A's results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Brief exposure to recordings prevented the abnormal song development seen in full isolation, suggesting that exposure alone may be sufficient.$q$),
  ($q$cs-334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Sleep researcher A found teenagers who used phones within an hour of bedtime took significantly longer to fall asleep.

Text 2: Sleep researcher B found no such delay among teenagers using e-readers with blue-light filters enabled during the same window before bedtime.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's finding suggests that light exposure, rather than device use itself, may explain the delay Researcher A observed.","Researcher B's findings prove that phones have no effect on sleep.","Both researchers studied adults rather than teenagers.","Researcher A found that e-readers caused the longest delays."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the filtered e-reader avoided the sleep delay seen with phones, the light emitted, not device use in general, may best explain Researcher A's result.$q$),
  ($q$cs-335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Astrophysicist A argues most stellar-mass black holes form from the direct collapse of massive stars without a preceding supernova explosion, citing stars that vanished from telescope surveys without any observed flash.

Text 2: Astrophysicist B argues that a faint, easily missed supernova likely did occur in these cases, citing faint afterglows detected in similar events after more sensitive follow-up observations.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Astrophysicist B would likely respond to Astrophysicist A's claim?$q$, $q$["B would argue that more sensitive observation would likely reveal a faint supernova that direct-collapse surveys missed.","B would agree that no supernova ever precedes black hole formation.","B would claim that telescope surveys are incapable of detecting any stellar event.","B would argue that direct collapse is impossible under any circumstances."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Astrophysicist B's evidence of faint afterglows detected through follow-up observation directly counters A's claim that no supernova occurred in these cases.$q$),
  ($q$cs-336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Curator A argues textiles should be displayed under very low light to prevent fading, even if visitors find the dim galleries hard to view.

Text 2: Curator B argues brief, timed increases in lighting when a visitor approaches a display preserve the textile nearly as well while greatly improving visibility.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two curators' approaches?$q$, $q$["Curator B proposes an alternative that aims to balance preservation with the visibility Curator A's approach sacrifices.","Both curators agree textiles should be displayed under constant bright light.","Curator A argues that lighting has no effect on textile preservation.","Curator B recommends removing all lighting restrictions entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Curator B's timed-lighting proposal seeks nearly the same preservation as Curator A's constant low light while improving the visibility Curator A sacrifices.$q$),
  ($q$cs-337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Botanist A found that a certain shrub's seeds sprout far more successfully after passing through a bird's digestive tract.

Text 2: Botanist B found that seeds collected directly from the same shrub's fruit, without passing through any animal, sprouted at nearly the same rate when soaked in water first.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two botanists?$q$, $q$["Botanist B's finding suggests that soaking, not digestion specifically, may account for the improved sprouting Botanist A observed.","Both botanists found that soaking has no effect on sprouting.","Botanist A found that digestion reduced sprouting rates.","Botanist B's findings exactly replicate Botanist A's results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because soaked, undigested seeds sprouted nearly as well as digested ones, soaking rather than digestion itself may explain the improvement Botanist A observed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Security researcher A argues open-source software is generally more secure because its publicly visible code allows many independent experts to find and fix flaws.

Text 2: Security researcher B argues visibility cuts both ways, since the same public code lets malicious actors study it for weaknesses just as easily as defenders do.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' views?$q$, $q$["Researcher B reframes the transparency Researcher A credits as a factor that can also help attackers, not only defenders.","Researcher B agrees entirely that open visibility only benefits defenders.","Both researchers argue that proprietary software cannot be studied at all.","Researcher A argues that visibility helps only malicious actors."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B accepts that code visibility matters but points out it can aid attackers too, reframing rather than simply agreeing with Researcher A's defender-only framing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that rising literacy rates in a region preceded and enabled the spread of printed pamphlets, since local records show reading schools established decades before presses arrived.

Text 2: Historian B argues that printed pamphlets themselves drove the rise in literacy, since demand for reading material motivated families to seek instruction only after pamphlets became available locally.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' explanations?$q$, $q$["Historian A treats literacy as the cause of pamphlet demand, while Historian B treats pamphlets as the cause of rising literacy.","Both historians agree literacy and printing arrived in the same year.","Historian B argues that reading schools predated any printed material.","Historian A claims that pamphlets caused literacy to rise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A places literacy before pamphlets as the cause, while Historian B places pamphlets before literacy as the cause — the reversed causal order in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Ecologist A argues urban foxes have grown measurably bolder around humans over several generations, citing reduced flight distances recorded in city parks.

Text 2: Ecologist B argues the measured boldness reflects which foxes researchers can easily observe, since bolder foxes are simply more likely to be seen and recorded than genuinely shy ones.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Ecologist B would likely respond to Ecologist A's claim?$q$, $q$["B would argue that a sampling bias toward visible foxes, not real behavioral change, may explain A's measurement.","B would agree that all urban foxes have become equally bold.","B would claim that flight distance cannot be measured in city parks.","B would argue that shy foxes are overrepresented in A's data."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ecologist B's objection is that observation bias toward bolder, more visible foxes could explain the measured boldness, rather than genuine behavioral change across generations.$q$),
  ($q$cs-341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Historian A credits a single researcher's persistent individual experiments for a major vaccine breakthrough.

Text 2: Historian B credits a broader team of collaborators whose contributions, documented in lab notebooks, were central to the same breakthrough.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasizes an individual's role, while Historian B emphasizes a collaborative team's role.","Both historians agree no individual contributed to the breakthrough.","Historian B argues that lab notebooks contain no useful records.","Historian A credits a team rather than an individual."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A attributes the breakthrough to one researcher, while Historian B attributes it to a broader collaborative team — the contrast described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Education researcher A found students in a fully online course scored lower on final exams than students in an in-person version of the same course.

Text 2: Education researcher B found that online students who participated in optional weekly video discussions scored about the same as in-person students.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's finding suggests that participation in discussions, not the online format itself, may account for the gap Researcher A observed.","Researcher B's findings prove that online courses are always superior.","Both researchers studied exclusively in-person courses.","Researcher A found that online students scored higher overall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because discussion-participating online students closed the gap, participation rather than the online format itself may explain the score difference Researcher A observed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Geomorphologist A argues a river's meandering pattern is shaped primarily by the underlying bedrock's resistance to erosion, citing straighter channels where bedrock is harder.

Text 2: Geomorphologist B argues sediment deposited by the river itself shapes the meanders more than bedrock does, citing meanders that shifted position over decades in a floodplain with no bedrock close to the surface.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two geomorphologists' explanations?$q$, $q$["Geomorphologist A points to bedrock resistance, while Geomorphologist B points to sediment deposition as the primary shaping factor.","Both geomorphologists agree the river's channel never changes shape.","Geomorphologist B relies exclusively on measurements of bedrock hardness.","Geomorphologist A argues that sediment is irrelevant to channel shape in all rivers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Geomorphologist A attributes meander shape to bedrock, while Geomorphologist B attributes it to sediment deposition — the contrast described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Typographer A argues serif fonts are more legible in long printed passages because small strokes guide the eye along each line.

Text 2: Typographer B argues legibility differences between serif and sans-serif fonts disappear once text is enlarged enough, citing reading-speed tests that showed no difference at larger sizes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two typographers' claims?$q$, $q$["Typographer B's finding suggests that A's legibility advantage may depend on text size rather than font style alone.","Typographer B's findings exactly confirm Typographer A's claim at every size.","Both typographers argue that font style never affects legibility.","Typographer A argues that sans-serif fonts are always more legible."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the legibility difference vanished at larger sizes in Typographer B's tests, A's claimed advantage may depend on text size rather than holding universally.$q$),
  ($q$cs-345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Botanist A found that a plant under insect attack releases a scent that causes nearby plants of the same species to produce defensive chemicals.

Text 2: Botanist B found that plants of a different, unrelated species growing nearby did not increase defensive chemical production in response to the same scent.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two findings?$q$, $q$["Botanist B's finding suggests the scent signal observed by Botanist A may be most effective among plants of the same species.","Both botanists found the scent signal works equally across all species.","Botanist A found that unrelated species responded most strongly to the scent.","Botanist B's findings exactly replicate Botanist A's results in every species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since unrelated species failed to respond while same-species plants did, the scent signal Botanist A observed may be limited to plants of the same species.$q$),
  ($q$cs-346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Materials scientist A argues a newly tested battery material could double the driving range of electric vehicles, citing lab results showing twice the energy density of current materials.

Text 2: Materials scientist B argues the lab results are unlikely to translate to real vehicles, noting the material degraded sharply after only a few hundred charge cycles in separate durability tests.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Materials scientist B would likely respond to Materials scientist A's claim?$q$, $q$["B would argue that the material's rapid degradation undermines its practical benefit despite its high energy density.","B would agree that the material will double driving range in real vehicles.","B would claim that energy density has no bearing on driving range.","B would argue that durability testing is irrelevant to battery performance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Materials scientist B's durability findings show the material degrades quickly, which would undercut the practical range benefit A claims from its energy density alone.$q$),
  ($q$cs-347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Archaeologist A argues that bronze-working spread across a region through direct trade contact between neighboring communities, citing nearly identical alloy ratios found at sites along known trade routes.

Text 2: Archaeologist B argues that communities independently discovered similar bronze-working methods, citing the wide availability of the same ore deposits across the entire region regardless of trade routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two archaeologists' views?$q$, $q$["Archaeologist A attributes the spread to trade contact, while Archaeologist B attributes it to independent discovery enabled by shared resources.","Both archaeologists agree no ore deposits existed in the region.","Archaeologist B relies exclusively on alloy ratio measurements.","Archaeologist A argues that bronze-working was discovered independently everywhere."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Archaeologist A credits trade contact for the spread of bronze-working, while Archaeologist B credits independent discovery from shared ore deposits — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The textbook's explanation of thermodynamics was unusually ______: even readers with no physics background could follow the chain of reasoning from one law to the next.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["lucid","cryptic","technical","lengthy"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Readers with no background being able to follow the reasoning describes a clear, or lucid, explanation, not a cryptic or overly technical one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Knowing the market could turn volatile, the retiree made the ______ choice to keep six months of expenses in a low-risk savings account before investing the rest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prudent","reckless","impulsive","generous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting aside expenses as a safeguard before investing is a cautious, sensible decision, which 'prudent' captures; 'reckless' and 'impulsive' are opposites.$q$),
  ($q$cs-350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$After a summer of drought, the farmer's harvest was ______, yielding barely enough grain to last the family through winter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["meager","abundant","varied","delayed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A drought that leaves 'barely enough grain' describes a scant, or meager, harvest, the opposite of abundant.$q$),
  ($q$cs-351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Despite the layoffs reported elsewhere in the industry, the small firm's mood remained unexpectedly ______, lifted by a string of new client contracts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["buoyant","somber","indifferent","cautious"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New contracts lifting the mood despite industry layoffs describes a hopeful, upbeat, or buoyant mood, not a somber one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Transcribing the handwritten ledgers one entry at a time proved ______, and most interns quit before finishing even a single box.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tedious","lucrative","collaborative","rewarding"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Work so dull that most interns quit before finishing one box is tedious, not lucrative or rewarding.$q$),
  ($q$cs-353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Engineers found that the new coating reduced friction by a ______ amount, so small that test drivers could not detect any difference in handling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["negligible","substantial","dangerous","predictable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An amount 'so small' that drivers couldn't notice it is negligible, the opposite of substantial.$q$),
  ($q$cs-354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$After months of practicing together, the rowing team finally moved as a ______ unit, every stroke landing in the water at exactly the same instant.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cohesive","disjointed","competitive","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Strokes landing 'at exactly the same instant' describe a tightly unified, or cohesive, team, not a disjointed one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Compared to the ornate buildings surrounding it, the new library's ______ facade of plain gray concrete drew little attention from passersby.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["understated","elaborate","colorful","historic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A 'plain gray' facade that draws 'little attention' next to ornate buildings is understated, the opposite of elaborate or colorful.$q$),
  ($q$cs-356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The merger agreement's ______ language, full of nested clauses and cross-references, required three attorneys a full week to untangle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["convoluted","straightforward","brief","informal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Language that is 'full of nested clauses' and takes a week to 'untangle' is convoluted, not straightforward or brief.$q$),
  ($q$cs-357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The young sailor's ______ plan to cross the strait alone in a boat built from salvaged wood struck seasoned captains as reckless bravado.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["audacious","timid","methodical","customary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A plan that seasoned captains see as 'reckless bravado' is boldly daring, or audacious, not timid or customary.$q$),
  ($q$cs-358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics dismissed the painter's early canvases as ______, noting how closely each composition echoed the brushwork of her more famous teacher.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["derivative","innovative","abstract","unfinished"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Work that closely echoes another artist's style is derivative, or imitative, the opposite of innovative.$q$),
  ($q$cs-359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than guessing at the cause of the outage, the technician proceeded in a ______ manner, checking each component in sequence until the faulty wire was found.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["methodical","erratic","hurried","careless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Checking components 'in sequence' rather than guessing describes an orderly, step-by-step, or methodical approach.$q$),
  ($q$cs-360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Boxes were stacked in a ______ fashion throughout the garage, with fragile dishes wedged beneath heavy tools and nothing labeled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["haphazard","systematic","sparse","orderly"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Fragile dishes wedged under heavy tools with nothing labeled describes a disorganized, random, or haphazard arrangement, not a systematic one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The committee's final statement sounded oddly ______, pairing a paragraph celebrating unity with a paragraph listing years of unresolved disputes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["discordant","harmonious","concise","formal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pairing celebration of unity with a list of unresolved disputes creates a clashing, or discordant, effect, not a harmonious one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though his feedback was often accurate, the new manager's ______ delivery—blunt criticism given in front of the whole team—left morale lower each week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["abrasive","diplomatic","hesitant","generous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Blunt public criticism that lowers morale describes a harsh, grating, or abrasive delivery, the opposite of diplomatic.$q$),
  ($q$cs-363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Negotiating between two rival factions who refused even to sit at the same table, the mediator remained ______, careful never to let either side feel blamed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diplomatic","partisan","indifferent","aggressive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being 'careful never to let either side feel blamed' describes tactful, even-handed, or diplomatic conduct, not partisan or aggressive behavior.$q$),
  ($q$cs-364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$For most recent graduates, rent in the city center has become ______, forcing many to commute from towns an hour away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prohibitive","negligible","stable","subsidized"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rent high enough to force graduates to commute from distant towns is prohibitively expensive, not negligible or subsidized.$q$),
  ($q$cs-365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$On stage the violinist was ______, punctuating every solo with sweeping bow strokes and dramatic pauses that the sheet music never called for.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flamboyant","reserved","mechanical","nervous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sweeping, theatrical gestures not called for by the music describe a showy, flamboyant performer, the opposite of reserved or mechanical.$q$),
  ($q$cs-366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than pursuing the ideal but costly solution, the city council took a ______ approach, patching the most dangerous potholes first with the budget available.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["pragmatic","idealistic","reckless","ceremonial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choosing a practical fix within budget rather than the costly ideal describes a pragmatic approach, the opposite of idealistic.$q$),
  ($q$cs-367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The star's brightness dimmed and brightened on no predictable schedule, a ______ pattern that kept astronomers debating its cause for years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["perplexing","familiar","gradual","seasonal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A pattern with 'no predictable schedule' that sparks years of debate is puzzling, or perplexing, not familiar or seasonal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though her name appeared in no official minutes, the translator had become so ______ to the negotiations that delegates privately agreed no session should proceed without her.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indispensable","peripheral","controversial","influential"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Delegates insisting 'no session should proceed without her' signals that she had become irreplaceable, which 'indispensable' names; 'influential' only means she had a significant effect, not that negotiations literally could not continue in her absence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$What the newcomers dreaded as punishing, the veteran hikers found oddly ______: the thin, cold air at the summit seemed to sharpen their thinking rather than exhaust it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["invigorating","debilitating","unremarkable","pleasant"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clue is a specific energizing effect — thinking 'sharpened' rather than dulled — which 'invigorating' names; 'pleasant' only means agreeable and doesn't capture that the thin air actively heightened their mental sharpness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The editor's cuts to the manuscript were so ______ that the author, rereading the shortened draft, could not identify a single scene she actually missed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["judicious","careless","extensive","thorough"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The author's inability to miss anything shows the cuts were wisely chosen, not just comprehensive — 'thorough' would describe cuts that covered a lot of material, which could still remove something the author missed, unlike the good judgment 'judicious' implies.$q$),
  ($q$cs-371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Scholars have spent decades on the inscription's final line, which remains ______ enough that no two translations agree on its meaning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cryptic","redundant","archaic","literal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Disagreement among translators points to an obscure, puzzling meaning, which 'cryptic' names directly; 'archaic' only describes the language as old-fashioned and wouldn't by itself explain why scholars can't agree on what it means.$q$),
  ($q$cs-372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Nothing in the firm's decade of filings escaped the auditor's ______ attention, not even a rounding error buried three spreadsheets deep.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["scrupulous","cursory","diligent","belated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Catching an error 'buried three spreadsheets deep' shows nothing escaped notice, a level of exacting precision 'scrupulous' captures; 'diligent' describes hard, persistent work but doesn't guarantee that same exhaustive attention to the smallest detail.$q$),
  ($q$cs-373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Insurers quoted a premium so ______ for the fragile sculpture's transport that the museum chose to cancel the loan rather than pay it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["exorbitant","nominal","standard","steep"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Canceling the loan over the price implies it was judged unreasonably excessive, which 'exorbitant' conveys; 'steep' only means high and could still describe a price the museum found justifiable, just expensive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Honeybee scouts that find a rich flower patch perform a 'waggle dance' back at the hive. The dance's angle and duration encode the direction and distance to the food source for other bees to follow.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?$q$, $q$["It explains the mechanism behind the behavior introduced in the first sentence.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely new topic unrelated to bees.","It summarizes the entire text before any detail is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains how the dance encodes direction and distance, elaborating on the mechanism behind the behavior named in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A short article begins by describing how sailors once relied on bonfires atop cliffs to avoid rocky shoals. It then explains how the Fresnel lens revolutionized lighthouse design in the nineteenth century. It closes by noting that satellite navigation has made many lighthouses obsolete today.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the history of a technology from an early method to a major improvement to its current decline.","It compares two competing theories about the origins of lighthouses.","It argues that lighthouses should be rebuilt using historical methods.","It lists safety regulations for modern maritime navigation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves chronologically from bonfires, to the Fresnel lens improvement, to the decline caused by satellites, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A food blogger's post opens with a story about her grandmother's kitchen, then transitions into a detailed recipe for the soup that kitchen was known for.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening story about the grandmother's kitchen?$q$, $q$["It provides a personal, sentimental frame that motivates the recipe that follows.","It proves that the recipe originated in a professional restaurant.","It lists the exact ingredients required for the soup.","It criticizes modern cooking techniques."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The anecdote sets a personal, sentimental tone that leads into and motivates the recipe, rather than listing ingredients or criticizing anything.$q$),
  ($q$cs-377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A business profile first describes the company's humble beginning in a garage. The next paragraph details its rapid expansion after a single viral product launch. The final paragraph discusses the leadership struggles that followed that growth.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second paragraph?$q$, $q$["To mark the turning point between the company's modest start and its later struggles","To criticize the company's original founders","To describe a struggle that occurred before the company existed","To provide statistics unrelated to the company's growth"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second paragraph's account of rapid expansion sits between the modest beginning and the later struggles, functioning as the pivot connecting them.$q$),
  ($q$cs-378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Glass was once so costly that only the wealthy could afford windows made of it. Over centuries, cheaper manufacturing methods made glass common in ordinary homes. Today glass is so inexpensive that it is often used once and thrown away.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces how an object's status changed as its cost declined over time.","It explains the chemical composition of glass.","It compares glass to other building materials used today.","It argues that glass should no longer be manufactured."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from glass as a luxury, to a common household material, to a disposable one, tracking its changing status as cost fell.$q$),
  ($q$cs-379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Before she became known for her mathematical proofs, the mathematician spent a decade working as a seamstress to support her studies.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of this sentence in a biography of the mathematician?$q$, $q$["It highlights an unexpected contrast between her early circumstances and her later achievements.","It explains the mathematical content of her proofs.","It argues that seamstresses cannot become mathematicians.","It criticizes her decision to pursue mathematics."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Naming an unrelated decade-long job 'before' her fame sets up a contrast between her modest early circumstances and her later achievements.$q$),
  ($q$cs-380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Scientists long assumed that the volcano's dormant period meant its magma chamber had cooled significantly. New seismic readings, however, show the chamber remains nearly as hot as it was before the last eruption, suggesting dormancy says little about the chamber's temperature.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?$q$, $q$["It presents new evidence that undermines the assumption described in the first sentence.","It restates the first sentence without adding new information.","It describes an unrelated volcanic eruption from history.","It proposes a method for predicting future eruptions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The 'however' signals a reversal: seismic readings showing the chamber is still hot directly undermines the cooling assumption stated first.$q$),
  ($q$cs-381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on urban parks opens with a statistic about declining green space in cities, pivots to interviews with three city planners about competing visions for new parks, and ends by proposing a compromise that borrows from each planner's vision.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the essay?$q$, $q$["It moves from a problem, through multiple perspectives on that problem, to a proposed synthesis.","It presents a single planner's argument and refutes it point by point.","It narrates a chronological history of one city's park system.","It compares the cost of parks in different countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay proceeds from a stated problem, to three differing perspectives, to a synthesis combining them, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The report claims that remote work increases productivity for most employees. It then cites a study in which output per hour rose eight percent after a software company allowed employees to work from home three days a week.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence?$q$, $q$["It supplies specific evidence for the general claim made in the first sentence.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a new claim unrelated to productivity.","It questions the reliability of the first sentence's claim."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The specific statistic about output rising eight percent supports, rather than contradicts, the general productivity claim made in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Advocates often describe the new vaccine as a complete solution to the outbreak. The vaccine, however, appears to reduce transmission mainly among adults under fifty, with weaker effects observed so far in older populations.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It qualifies the sweeping claim made in the first sentence with a more limited finding.","It fully confirms the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a different disease unrelated to the vaccine.","It argues that the vaccine should be discontinued entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Noting that the effect varies by age group narrows, or qualifies, the sweeping claim of a 'complete solution' made in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Critics of the new subway line concede that it has shortened commute times for thousands of riders. They argue, however, that its construction displaced several small businesses that have not been able to relocate nearby.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It acknowledges a benefit before raising a significant drawback.","It lists several unrelated benefits of the subway line.","It narrates the day-to-day construction of the subway line.","It argues that the subway line should never have been proposed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text concedes shortened commutes as a benefit, then pivots with 'however' to raise the displacement of businesses as a drawback.$q$),
  ($q$cs-385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Before analyzing the bridge's failure, the engineer's report first defines 'fatigue cracking' as the gradual weakening of metal from repeated stress rather than a single overload. Only after establishing this definition does the report examine the bridge's specific cracks.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence?$q$, $q$["It defines a key term that the rest of the report will depend on.","It summarizes the report's final conclusion.","It argues that the bridge's failure was intentional.","It criticizes the engineers who built the bridge."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence establishes a definition of 'fatigue cracking' before the report applies that term to the bridge's specific cracks.$q$),
  ($q$cs-386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A psychology article opens with the story of a single patient who overcame a specific phobia through gradual exposure. The article then broadens its focus to describe how gradual exposure therapy has been studied across thousands of patients with many different phobias.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the opening anecdote and the rest of the article?$q$, $q$["The anecdote introduces a specific case that the article then generalizes into a broader pattern.","The anecdote contradicts the broader pattern described later.","The anecdote proves that exposure therapy fails in most cases.","The anecdote is unrelated to the broader discussion that follows."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The single-patient story functions as a specific case that the rest of the article broadens into a general pattern across thousands of patients.$q$),
  ($q$cs-387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many assume that older buildings are always less energy-efficient than new ones. One nineteenth-century stone farmhouse, however, retains heat so effectively through its thick walls that its owners use less fuel each winter than neighbors in houses built a decade ago.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It offers a specific counterexample to the assumption stated in the first sentence.","It provides statistical proof that all old buildings are efficient.","It explains how stone is quarried for construction.","It argues that new buildings should be banned."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The single farmhouse that outperforms newer houses serves as a specific counterexample to the general assumption stated first, not a universal proof.$q$),
  ($q$cs-388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A historian's essay seems at first to defend the king's reputation, cataloguing his reforms to taxation and law. Only in its final paragraph does the essay reveal that those reforms were implemented by the king's advisors over his repeated objections, recasting everything praised before as evidence against him.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the final paragraph functions within the essay as a whole?$q$, $q$["It reframes the preceding praise by attributing its basis to someone other than the king.","It simply repeats the praise offered earlier in the essay.","It reveals that the king personally championed the reforms despite his advisors' objections.","It abandons the topic of reforms entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By revealing the advisors implemented the reforms over the king's own objections, not the reverse, the final paragraph reframes the earlier praise as actually undermining his reputation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The review spends three paragraphs praising the novel's intricate plotting, careful pacing, and vivid setting. Its final line, almost in passing, admits that the reviewer skimmed the last hundred pages.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the effect of the final line on the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It undercuts the credibility of the detailed praise that came before it.","It confirms that the reviewer read every page carefully.","It reveals that the reviewer skimmed the opening chapters rather than the ending.","It summarizes the plot points discussed earlier in greater detail."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Admitting that the reviewer skimmed the last hundred pages, not the opening, casts doubt on the reliability of the detailed praise given in the preceding paragraphs.$q$),
  ($q$cs-390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A policy brief raises the objection that stricter emissions limits could raise costs for small manufacturers. It then presents data showing that, among manufacturers studied, the limits raised costs only for those that had delayed upgrading equipment for over a decade, while early adopters saw costs fall.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the data presented in the second sentence?$q$, $q$["It narrows the force of the objection raised in the first sentence to a specific subset of cases.","It fully confirms the objection raised in the first sentence applies to all manufacturers.","It shows that costs rose for every manufacturer regardless of when they upgraded equipment.","It argues that emissions limits should be abandoned entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Showing that costs rose only for manufacturers that delayed upgrades for over a decade, not for every manufacturer regardless of timing, limits the objection to a specific subset rather than confirming it broadly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A critique of a proposed dam begins by listing the engineers' projections for the electricity it would generate. It then shifts to decades-old records of similar dams whose output fell well short of projections once silt buildup reduced reservoir capacity. The critique closes by questioning whether the new dam's projections account for this pattern.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the critique?$q$, $q$["It presents a forecast, complicates it with historical precedent, then questions the forecast's assumptions.","It praises the engineers' projections without qualification.","It narrates the dam's construction process from start to finish.","It closes by endorsing the engineers' original electricity projections as accurate."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The critique moves from the stated forecast, to historical records complicating it, to a closing question about whether the forecast accounts for that pattern — not an endorsement of it — matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The memoir's central chapters recount the author's years as a translator at international summits, rendering speeches between leaders who often could not otherwise understand one another. One brief aside notes that the author sometimes softened insults exchanged between delegates, altering the historical record those delegates believed they had created.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the aside about softening insults?$q$, $q$["It complicates the chapters' portrayal of the author as a neutral conduit between delegates.","It proves that the author never made translation errors.","It summarizes the memoir's account of the author's childhood.","It argues that translators should be eliminated from summits entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Revealing that the author altered what was actually said complicates the earlier impression of a neutral conduit simply rendering speeches between leaders.$q$),
  ($q$cs-393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Researcher A found that students who studied right before sleeping recalled vocabulary lists better the next day than students who studied in the morning.

Text 2: Researcher B found the same memory benefit when students napped briefly after studying in the morning, suggesting sleep itself, not time of day, drives the effect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' findings?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's finding suggests that sleep, rather than studying at night specifically, may explain the benefit Researcher A observed.","Researcher B's finding contradicts Researcher A's results entirely.","Both researchers found that naps have no effect on memory.","Researcher A's finding applies only to students who never sleep."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since a morning nap produced the same benefit, sleep itself, rather than studying at night, may explain the recall advantage Researcher A found.$q$),
  ($q$cs-394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Critic A praised the new bakery's sourdough for its thick, crackling crust.

Text 2: Critic B also praised the sourdough's crust, adding that the tangy interior made it the best loaf in the neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two critics' reviews?$q$, $q$["Critic B agrees with Critic A's assessment and adds further praise.","Critic B disagrees with Critic A about the quality of the crust.","Critic A praised the interior while Critic B praised only the crust.","The two critics reviewed different bakeries entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Critic B echoes Critic A's praise for the crust and adds additional praise for the interior, so the two reviews agree and build on each other.$q$),
  ($q$cs-395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Gardener A waters tomato plants every morning, believing consistent moisture produces the sweetest fruit.

Text 2: Gardener B waters tomato plants only when the soil is visibly dry, arguing that occasional stress makes the fruit sweeter.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two gardeners' approaches?$q$, $q$["Gardener A favors a consistent watering schedule, while Gardener B favors watering only when the soil dries out.","Both gardeners agree that tomatoes should never be watered.","Gardener B waters more frequently than Gardener A.","Gardener A believes tomatoes require no sunlight."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Gardener A waters on a consistent daily schedule, while Gardener B waters only when soil dries out, the contrast stated in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Coach A has the swim team practice for three hours every day, believing more repetition builds faster times.

Text 2: Coach B limits practice to ninety minutes, arguing that shorter, more focused sessions prevent injury and improve times just as much.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two coaches' philosophies?$q$, $q$["Coach A emphasizes longer practices, while Coach B emphasizes shorter, more focused sessions.","Both coaches agree that practice length does not matter.","Coach B has the team practice longer than Coach A.","Coach A believes injuries are more important than speed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Coach A favors three-hour practices for more repetition, while Coach B favors shorter ninety-minute sessions, the difference choice A describes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Historian A dates the village's founding to the early 1600s, citing a land deed stored in a regional archive.

Text 2: Historian B dates the founding several decades earlier, citing pottery shards found at the site that match styles from the late 1500s.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two historians' claims?$q$, $q$["The two historians rely on different kinds of evidence to propose different founding dates for the village.","Both historians agree on the exact founding date of the village.","Historian A relies on pottery shards for the founding date.","Historian B argues the village was never founded."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A cites a land deed for an early-1600s date, while Historian B cites pottery shards for an earlier date, so each relies on different evidence for a different date.$q$),
  ($q$cs-398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Economist A studied a city that raised its minimum wage and found no measurable increase in unemployment over the following two years.

Text 2: Economist B studied a different city with a similar wage increase and found a small but clear rise in unemployment among workers under twenty, concentrated in retail jobs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two economists' findings?$q$, $q$["Economist B's findings suggest the effect of minimum wage increases may vary by location and worker group rather than being uniform.","Economist B's findings exactly replicate Economist A's results.","Both economists found large unemployment increases across every industry.","Economist A studied the same city and workers as Economist B."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the two studies found different results in different cities and worker groups, the effect may vary rather than being uniform, as choice A states.$q$),
  ($q$cs-399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Linguist A argues that a regional dialect is dying out because younger residents increasingly use standard pronunciations in everyday speech.

Text 2: Linguist B argues the dialect is merely shifting rather than dying, pointing to recordings showing younger residents still use many of its distinctive words, just with standard pronunciation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Linguist B's view relates to Linguist A's?$q$, $q$["Linguist B reinterprets the evidence Linguist A cites as a change in the dialect rather than its disappearance.","Linguist B agrees entirely that the dialect is disappearing.","Linguist A argues the dialect's vocabulary is spreading to new regions.","Both linguists agree the dialect has already vanished completely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Linguist B takes the same pronunciation shift Linguist A cites as evidence of death and reinterprets it as evidence of ongoing change instead.$q$),
  ($q$cs-400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A study of office workers found that those who drank coffee before noon reported better focus throughout the afternoon.

Text 2: A study of night-shift workers found that coffee consumed before their equivalent 'morning' shift start made no measurable difference in reported focus.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the second study's findings relate to the first study's findings?$q$, $q$["The second study suggests the focus benefit found in the first study may depend on factors beyond simply drinking coffee before a shift begins.","The second study confirms that coffee improves focus for all workers regardless of schedule.","Both studies found that coffee consistently reduces focus.","The first study examined night-shift workers exclusively."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since the same coffee timing failed to help night-shift workers, the benefit found in office workers may depend on factors beyond coffee timing alone.$q$),
  ($q$cs-401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Architect A argues that mass timber construction should replace steel in most mid-size buildings, citing its much smaller carbon footprint during manufacturing.

Text 2: Architect B argues that steel remains preferable for buildings in high-wildfire-risk areas, citing timber's greater vulnerability to fire despite modern fire-retardant treatments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two architects' positions?$q$, $q$["Architect B's concern about fire risk suggests Architect A's recommendation may not hold in every setting.","Architect B agrees that timber is preferable in all settings.","Both architects agree that steel should never be used in construction.","Architect A argues that fire risk is irrelevant to building design."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Architect B's fire-risk concern identifies a setting where Architect A's general recommendation for timber may not apply, as choice A states.$q$),
  ($q$cs-402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Critic A praises the director's use of long, uninterrupted takes for making tense scenes feel more immediate and real.

Text 2: Critic B argues the same long takes often drag on past the point of tension, leaving some scenes feeling padded rather than immersive.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two critics' assessments?$q$, $q$["The critics agree on the director's technique but disagree about its overall effect on the viewer.","The critics disagree about which technique the director actually used.","Critic B argues the director never uses long takes.","Both critics agree the long takes ruin every scene in the film."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both critics identify the same long-take technique, but Critic A finds it immersive while Critic B finds it padded, a disagreement about effect, not technique.$q$),
  ($q$cs-403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Paleontologist A argues that a species of dinosaur hunted in coordinated packs, citing multiple skeletons found together near a single large prey animal.

Text 2: Paleontologist B argues the skeletons more likely gathered individually around the carcass after it died of other causes, noting no bite marks from the smaller dinosaurs appear on the prey's bones.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two paleontologists' interpretations?$q$, $q$["Paleontologist A interprets the fossil grouping as evidence of coordinated hunting, while Paleontologist B interprets it as scavenging after death.","Both paleontologists agree the dinosaurs hunted alone.","Paleontologist B found bite marks confirming active hunting.","Paleontologist A argues the prey animal was never found."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Paleontologist A reads the grouped skeletons as coordinated hunting, while Paleontologist B reads the same grouping as later scavenging, the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A city council member claims that adding protected bike lanes on Main Street will reduce car traffic congestion within a year.

Text 2: A transportation researcher responds that similar projects in comparable cities reduced congestion only where nearby public transit could absorb the resulting drop in parking, not simply from adding the lanes alone.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the transportation researcher most likely respond to the council member's claim?$q$, $q$["By cautioning that the reduction may depend on supporting transit options, not the bike lanes alone.","By fully endorsing the claim without any reservations.","By arguing that bike lanes always increase congestion.","By insisting that public transit should be eliminated entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The researcher's evidence that comparable cities needed strong transit to see the benefit implies caution about the council member's unqualified claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Meteorologist A's forecasting model predicted the storm would weaken significantly before making landfall, based on patterns from the past decade.

Text 2: Meteorologist B notes that the storm instead strengthened over unusually warm ocean water that wasn't present in the decade of data the model was trained on.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Meteorologist B's observation relates to Meteorologist A's prediction?$q$, $q$["It identifies a condition absent from the model's training data that may explain why the prediction failed.","It confirms that the model's prediction was accurate.","It argues that ocean temperature never affects storm strength.","It shows that the storm weakened exactly as predicted."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Meteorologist B points to warm water absent from the model's training data as a likely reason the storm defied the model's weakening prediction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Philosopher A argues that a person's identity persists over time because of continuous psychological connections, like overlapping memories linking each day to the next, even if no single memory spans an entire life.

Text 2: Philosopher B argues that this chain of overlapping connections cannot by itself secure identity, since two people could in principle share such a chain between them without either being the same person as the other.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Philosopher B's argument functions in relation to Philosopher A's?$q$, $q$["It presents a scenario meant to show that A's criterion is not sufficient to guarantee identity.","It confirms that A's criterion is both necessary and sufficient for identity.","It argues that memories never overlap between consecutive days.","It restates A's position without raising any objection."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By imagining two people sharing such a chain, Philosopher B shows that overlapping psychological connections alone do not guarantee identity, undercutting A's sufficiency claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that a region's shift from subsistence farming to cash crops in the nineteenth century was driven primarily by new rail lines that made distant markets reachable for the first time.

Text 2: Historian B argues the shift began years before the rail lines were completed, pointing to tax records showing cash-crop acreage already rising in response to earlier changes in land ownership law.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Historian B's evidence complicates Historian A's argument?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the shift was already underway for reasons other than rail access, undermining rail lines as the primary cause.","It confirms that rail lines were the sole cause of the shift.","It shows that cash-crop farming never occurred before the rail lines were built.","It argues that land ownership law had no effect on farming practices."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tax records showing the shift predated the rail lines suggest another cause was already driving it, which weakens rail access as the primary explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Immunologist A argues that a vaccine candidate's strong antibody response in trial participants predicts durable long-term protection, based on past vaccines where high antibody levels correlated with years of protection.

Text 2: Immunologist B cautions that for the virus in question, antibody levels have been observed to fade within months even in people who later proved resistant to reinfection, suggesting antibody levels alone may not predict protection for this virus.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between Immunologist B's caution and Immunologist A's argument?$q$, $q$["It challenges the reliability of the measure Immunologist A relies on, at least for this particular virus.","It confirms that antibody levels predict protection for every virus.","It argues that no vaccine can ever provide protection.","It shows that Immunologist A's trial participants were never tested."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By showing antibody levels fade even in people who remain protected, Immunologist B challenges whether antibody levels reliably predict protection for this virus.$q$),
  ($q$cs-409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Art historian A attributes an unsigned portrait to a well-known painter's workshop, citing brushwork in the fabric folds that closely matches the master's documented technique.

Text 2: Art historian B questions the attribution, noting that pigment analysis shows the portrait uses a lead-based white that fell out of common use in the master's workshop a decade before the portrait's estimated date.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Art historian B's finding relates to Art historian A's attribution?$q$, $q$["It raises a chronological inconsistency that calls the stylistic attribution into question.","It fully supports the attribution based on brushwork alone.","It confirms the portrait's exact date of creation.","It argues that pigment analysis is irrelevant to dating paintings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pigment's fall from use before the portrait's estimated date creates a timing inconsistency that undercuts Art historian A's brushwork-based attribution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Because the trail was so ______, hikers had to watch every step on the loose gravel and exposed roots.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["familiar","crowded","scenic","treacherous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hikers watching every step on loose gravel and roots implies the trail is dangerous, so 'treacherous' fits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The chef was known for her ______ approach to sourcing: she personally visited local farms each morning to choose the best produce.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indifferent","careless","hurried","meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Personally visiting farms to choose the best produce shows a meticulous approach.$q$),
  ($q$cs-412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Although the first draft of the report was lengthy, the editor's revisions made the final version far more ______, removing repetition and unnecessary detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["concise","confusing","controversial","elaborate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Removing repetition and unnecessary detail makes the report concise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The new software is so ______ that even people with no technical training can learn to use it in a few minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expensive","outdated","complicated","intuitive"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Learnable in minutes by non-experts means the software is intuitive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The botanist described the desert flower as ______: it survives for only a few days after rare rains before vanishing until the next storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ubiquitous","perennial","formidable","ephemeral"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Surviving only a few days describes something short-lived, or ephemeral.$q$),
  ($q$cs-415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Historians have long regarded the diary as ______ evidence of daily life in the colony, since the writer recorded events as they happened and had no reason to embellish them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["irrelevant","fabricated","reliable","dubious"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Contemporaneous recording without motive to embellish supports describing the diary as reliable.$q$),
  ($q$cs-416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The mayor's response to the criticism was deliberately ______; rather than addressing the specific complaints, she offered only vague assurances.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["forthright","evasive","sympathetic","emphatic"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Offering only vague assurances instead of addressing complaints is evasive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Researchers were surprised that the seemingly ______ change in the experiment's temperature, just one degree, produced a dramatic shift in the results.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["drastic","deliberate","unprecedented","negligible"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A one-degree change that leads to a surprisingly large effect must have seemed negligible.$q$),
  ($q$cs-418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The architect's design is notable for its ______: every element, from the placement of windows to the choice of materials, serves the building's central purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["extravagance","coherence","ambiguity","fragility"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Every element serving one purpose describes coherence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$In her memoir, the diplomat describes the negotiations as ______ because both delegations refused to compromise, and talks stalled for months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["intractable","fruitful","routine","amicable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Stalled talks with no compromise are intractable.$q$),
  ($q$cs-420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Critics praised the novelist's ______ prose, noting that she conveyed complex emotions in just a few carefully chosen words.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["economical","careless","ornate","verbose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Conveying a lot in few words is economical.$q$),
  ($q$cs-421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The committee's decision to postpone the vote was ______, intended to give members more time to review the data rather than to block the proposal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["arbitrary","provisional","permanent","hostile"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A postponement meant to allow more review is temporary, i.e., provisional.$q$),
  ($q$cs-422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The ecologist's findings ______ the long-standing assumption that predator removal always benefits prey species; in the study, prey populations actually declined after the predators were removed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["undermine","illustrate","extend","corroborate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The prey populations declined, contradicting the assumption, so the findings undermine it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Although the senator's speech was ostensibly about fiscal policy, its ______ message, conveyed through carefully chosen anecdotes, was a critique of her opponent's character.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["explicit","implicit","irrelevant","innocuous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A message conveyed through anecdotes rather than stated outright is implicit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The archaeologists were cautious in their interpretation, noting that the scant remains were ______ and could support more than one reading of how the settlement was used.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["conclusive","equivocal","abundant","pristine"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Evidence that supports more than one reading is equivocal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Far from being a ______ observer, the journalist actively shaped the story she covered by organizing interviews and urging participants to speak publicly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detached","skilled","prominent","committed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Far from' signals the opposite of active involvement, which is a detached observer.$q$),
  ($q$cs-426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The economist's argument is ______: it rests on the premise that consumers behave rationally, a premise that her own data contradict.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-undermining","straightforward","airtight","unoriginal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument whose premise is contradicted by its own data undermines itself.$q$),
  ($q$cs-427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The poet's early work is marked by a ______ tone, steeped in nostalgia for a lost rural childhood, in contrast to the sharp irony of her later poems.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["wistful","boisterous","clinical","sardonic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Nostalgic longing for the past is wistful, in contrast with later irony.$q$),
  ($q$cs-428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The company's profits remained ______ throughout the year, hovering between $2.1 million and $2.2 million each quarter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stable","plummeting","unpredictable","volatile"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hovering within a narrow range indicates stability.$q$),
  ($q$cs-429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Visitors to the observatory are ______ to arrive early, since the number of places for the evening tour is limited.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reluctant","unable","advised","forbidden"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Limited places make early arrival advisable.$q$),
  ($q$cs-430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The author's tone in the essay is ______ rather than angry; she lays out the flaws in the policy calmly and methodically.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","indignant","frenzied","strident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Calm and methodical describes a measured tone.$q$),
  ($q$cs-431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Scholars debate whether the manuscript is a ______ account of the battle or a later invention; its details match no surviving contemporary record.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["genuine","derivative","fictitious","provisional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The 'or a later invention' contrast makes 'genuine' the logical counterpart, supported by the uncertainty about its details.$q$),
  ($q$cs-432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's performance was so ______ that the audience remained silent for several seconds after the final note before erupting into applause.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disjointed","moving","perfunctory","tedious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A deeply affecting performance explains the stunned silence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the two theories appear to conflict, a closer reading reveals that they are ______ rather than contradictory, each describing a different aspect of the same process.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["identical","complementary","incompatible","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each describing a different aspect of one process makes them complementary.$q$),
  ($q$cs-434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The old bridge was in such poor condition that engineers declared it ______ and closed it to all traffic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unnecessary","unsafe","historic","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Closing a bridge in poor condition to all traffic shows it was declared unsafe.$q$),
  ($q$cs-435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Honeybees communicate the location of food through a 'waggle dance.' The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates direction in relation to the sun, and the length of the waggle indicates distance.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It introduces a new topic unrelated to the first sentence.","It gives a historical background for the discovery of the dance.","It challenges the claim made in the first sentence.","It explains how the dance conveys information about the food's location."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first sentence introduces the dance; the second details how its angle and length encode direction and distance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice. In fact, the Empire State Building is struck by lightning about 25 times a year.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a common belief and then offers a fact that contradicts it.","It describes a process step by step.","It compares two historical events.","It offers a hypothesis and then confirms it."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first sentence states a belief; the second counters it with a contradicting fact.$q$),
  ($q$cs-437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Deep-sea hydrothermal vents were once thought to be barren. Then, in 1977, researchers aboard a submersible discovered dense communities of tube worms, clams, and bacteria thriving around them, fueled not by sunlight but by chemicals in the vent fluid.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain how submersibles are built","To argue that sunlight is unnecessary for most life","To describe a discovery that overturned an earlier assumption","To compare different kinds of sea creatures"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts an earlier belief (vents are barren) with the 1977 discovery.$q$),
  ($q$cs-438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Economist Linh Tran has proposed that cities adopt congestion pricing. Under her model, drivers entering the downtown core during peak hours would pay a fee, with the revenue funding public transit. She argues that the policy would reduce traffic while expanding alternatives to driving.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the last sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It states the benefits that Tran claims her proposal would bring.","It describes how the fee is calculated.","It identifies a flaw in Tran's proposal.","It provides a counterexample to Tran's model."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The last sentence gives Tran's argument for the policy's benefits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In 1854, physician John Snow mapped cholera deaths in London and noticed that they clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. At his urging, officials removed the pump's handle, and the outbreak subsided. Snow's map is now regarded as a foundational example of epidemiology.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It contrasts two competing medical approaches.","It proposes a theory and then lists objections.","It recounts an investigation, describes its outcome, and notes its lasting significance.","It defines a term and then gives several examples."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text narrates Snow's investigation, the result, and its legacy.$q$),
  ($q$cs-440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The poem's opening stanza describes an empty harbor at dawn, the boats gone and the water still. By the final stanza, the speaker has begun to address the harbor directly, as though it could answer. This shift from description to address transforms the setting from a backdrop into a kind of companion.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the last sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It interprets the effect of a change in the poem's approach.","It restates the poem's first stanza.","It questions the poem's reliability.","It provides biographical information about the poet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final sentence analyzes what the shift from description to address accomplishes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Astronomers once assumed that planets form only around single stars. The discovery of Kepler-16b, a planet orbiting two stars, showed that this assumption was too narrow. The finding has since prompted researchers to look for planets in binary systems that they had previously ignored.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that single stars cannot host planets","To criticize a particular research method","To explain how a discovery changed the direction of research","To describe the physical features of Kepler-16b"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text shows a discovery overturning an assumption and redirecting research.$q$),
  ($q$cs-442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Some linguists contend that children learn language mainly by imitation. Yet children routinely produce forms they have never heard, such as 'goed' for 'went,' errors that reveal an internal grammar rule being applied. Such errors, these researchers suggest, are better evidence of rule-learning than of imitation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It offers an observation that complicates the view presented in the first sentence.","It shifts the discussion to a different language.","It supports the view with an additional example.","It provides a definition of an unfamiliar term."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Children's novel errors cannot be explained by imitation, complicating the first sentence's view.$q$),
  ($q$cs-443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Textile historians often credit the Industrial Revolution with transforming cloth production. But this view overlooks earlier developments: by the 1500s, Flemish weavers had already organized a system in which merchants supplied raw wool to rural households and collected finished cloth. The factory, in this light, was less a break than the culmination of a long evolution.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It lists causes of a historical event in chronological order.","It narrates the biography of an individual weaver.","It compares two manufacturing techniques and recommends one.","It presents a prevailing view, introduces overlooked evidence, and offers a revised interpretation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text states a common view, adds earlier developments, and concludes with a reinterpretation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The study of fossilized pollen can reveal the plants that grew in an area thousands of years ago. Pollen grains are remarkably durable, and their shapes differ by species, allowing scientists to identify the plants that produced them. Layers of pollen in lake sediment thus act as a record of changing vegetation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It states a conclusion that follows from the preceding information.","It introduces a limitation of the method.","It contradicts a claim made earlier.","It provides a counterexample."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Thus' signals a conclusion drawn from the durability and identifiability described before.$q$),
  ($q$cs-445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The octopus has three hearts. Two pump blood through the gills, and the third circulates it to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It disputes the first sentence.","It explains what the three hearts do.","It introduces an unrelated animal.","It gives a historical example."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence details the roles of the three hearts mentioned in the first.$q$),
  ($q$cs-446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The idea that early humans subsisted mostly on meat, popularized in mid-century accounts, has been challenged by analyses of tooth wear and residue on stone tools, which suggest a diet rich in tubers and seeds. Proponents of the older view counter that such plant evidence preserves far better than animal evidence, and so may overstate its role.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It defines a concept and illustrates it with a single case.","It describes a claim, a challenge to it, and a response to that challenge.","It lists three causes of a single phenomenon.","It proposes a new method and then demonstrates it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The passage moves from an older claim to evidence challenging it to a rebuttal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$After years of declining readership, the local newspaper launched a free weekly edition delivered to every household. Within two years, advertising revenue had doubled. The editor attributes the turnaround to the paper's renewed visibility.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that all newspapers should be free","To compare two newspapers","To criticize the decline of print media","To describe a strategy and its reported results"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text outlines the paper's strategy, its outcome, and the editor's explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In her essay, writer Maya Soto recalls watching her grandmother mend a single sweater over decades, replacing sleeves, then the collar, then the hem, until little of the original remained. 'Was it still the same sweater?' she asks. The question, Soto suggests, applies as readily to a person revisited across a lifetime as to a garment.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the question in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an argument about clothing manufacturing.","It undermines the point made in the first sentence.","It uses a concrete image to raise a broader question about identity.","It expresses Soto's frustration with her grandmother's habits."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sweater question serves as a metaphor for how a person changes over time.$q$),
  ($q$cs-449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The Amazon River carries more water than any other river on Earth. It discharges roughly one-fifth of all the freshwater that flows into the oceans.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It gives a statistic that illustrates the claim in the first sentence.","It defines a technical term.","It contradicts the claim in the first sentence.","It introduces a counterargument."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence quantifies the Amazon's enormous output.$q$),
  ($q$cs-450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Though the study found no significant difference in test scores between students taught with the new curriculum and those taught with the old, its authors caution against concluding that the curricula are equally effective. The study followed students for only a single semester, they note, and benefits of the new approach, if any, may emerge only over a longer period.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It disputes the validity of the test scores.","It shows that the study's result was surprising.","It proposes an alternative curriculum.","It explains why the authors hesitate to draw a firm conclusion from the results."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence gives the study's limitation as the reason for caution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Historian Ana Ruiz argues that the printing press spread literacy rapidly across Europe in the 1500s, pointing to the sharp increase in books published.

Text 2
Historian Ben Okoye contends that increases in publishing do not necessarily reflect literacy gains, since many books were bought by the small number of people who could already read.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okoye most likely respond to Ruiz's argument?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the press was not invented in the 1500s","By claiming that publishing declined in the 1500s","By noting that more books being published does not by itself show that more people could read","By agreeing that literacy rose sharply"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Okoye's point is that rising book output may simply reflect existing readers buying more.$q$),
  ($q$cs-452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Nutrition researcher Dr. Lee claims that breakfast improves students' concentration, citing a study in which students who ate breakfast scored higher on morning attention tasks.

Text 2
Researcher Dr. Patel notes that the students in Lee's study chose whether to eat breakfast and argues that students from more stable households may be both likelier to eat breakfast and likelier to concentrate.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?$q$, $q$["Text 2 repeats the finding in Text 1 with new data.","Text 2 offers an alternative explanation for the finding presented in Text 1.","Text 2 describes a different study unrelated to Text 1.","Text 2 confirms that breakfast causes better concentration."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Patel proposes a confounding factor (household stability) as another explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Musicologist A argues that Mozart's Requiem was largely completed by Mozart himself before his death, with his student Süssmayr merely tidying the score.

Text 2
Musicologist B argues that surviving sketches show Mozart left only fragments of the later movements, and that Süssmayr composed substantial portions on his own.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Musicologist B most likely respond to Musicologist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By agreeing that Süssmayr only tidied the score","By pointing to manuscript evidence indicating that much of the later music was Süssmayr's own work","By claiming that Mozart never wrote any part of the Requiem","By arguing that the Requiem was never completed"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$B cites surviving sketches showing Mozart left only fragments, contradicting A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Urban planner Rosa Delgado believes that adding bike lanes always reduces downtown traffic congestion by encouraging more people to cycle.

Text 2
Traffic analyst Sam Ito found that in two cities, bike lanes that replaced car lanes were followed by increased congestion, though he acknowledges that lanes built without removing car lanes did not have this effect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Ito's findings relate to Delgado's belief?$q$, $q$["They prove that bike lanes never matter.","They confirm her claim in all cases.","They indicate that her claim does not hold in every circumstance.","They are irrelevant to her claim."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ito's counterexamples show that 'always' is too strong a claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Philosopher Dana Cho argues that moral progress is driven mainly by great individuals whose ideas change society.

Text 2
Sociologist Eli Brandt argues that changes in material conditions, such as economic growth, make new moral views possible, so that individuals merely articulate shifts already underway.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the difference in the authors' views?$q$, $q$["Cho focuses on economics, while Brandt focuses on philosophy.","Both authors believe moral change is impossible.","Cho and Brandt disagree about whether morality exists.","Cho emphasizes the role of individuals, while Brandt emphasizes underlying social conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Cho credits influential individuals; Brandt credits material conditions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1
A bakery owner says a new oven will increase her output.

Text 2
Her accountant says the oven will increase output but that its cost means the bakery will not profit for two years.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the accountant would likely view the owner's claim?$q$, $q$["As entirely false","As exaggerated about the oven's cost","As accurate but incomplete, since it overlooks the oven's cost","As unrelated to the bakery's finances"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The accountant agrees output rises but adds the financial caveat.$q$),
  ($q$cs-457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Biologist Carmen Ortiz proposes that a species of frog is declining mainly because of a fungal infection, based on infection rates found in dying populations.

Text 2
Biologist Greg Hall reports that in several healthy populations the fungus is also common, and suggests that habitat loss, which has affected all the declining populations, is a more likely cause.$q$, $q$How would Hall most likely respond to Ortiz's proposal?$q$, $q$["By asserting that the frogs have not declined","By confirming that the fungus is the sole cause","By denying that the fungus exists","By arguing that the fungus's presence in healthy populations weakens the case that it is the main cause"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hall points out the fungus occurs where frogs are healthy, undermining the causal claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
An art critic argues that abstract painting abandons the artist's responsibility to depict the world and is therefore a lesser achievement than representational art.

Text 2
An art historian responds that the criterion of depiction is itself a convention, one that photography made obsolete as a measure of painterly value.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the historian's response to the critic?$q$, $q$["It challenges the standard the critic uses to judge art.","It offers a biography of the critic.","It agrees that abstraction is a lesser form.","It restates the critic's claim in new words."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The historian questions the criterion (depiction) that grounds the critic's evaluation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Meteorologist Priya Nair says that long-range weather forecasts beyond ten days are essentially worthless because of the chaotic nature of the atmosphere.

Text 2
Climatologist Tom Weber says that while day-specific forecasts beyond ten days are unreliable, seasonal outlooks that predict whether a period will be warmer or wetter than average can be useful.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Weber most likely view Nair's claim?$q$, $q$["As completely correct","As too broad, since some long-range predictions have value","As irrelevant to forecasting","As understating the reliability of day-specific forecasts"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Weber agrees about day-specific forecasts but notes seasonal outlooks are useful.$q$),
  ($q$cs-460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1
Ms. Ward says the school should start later to give students more sleep.

Text 2
Mr. Cole agrees that students need more sleep but says a later start would hurt after-school sports.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Cole's view relates to Ward's?$q$, $q$["He accepts her premise but raises a concern about her proposal.","He restates her view without change.","He proposes an unrelated policy.","He rejects her premise outright."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cole agrees on sleep but notes a cost of the proposal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Legal scholar Hale argues that the right to privacy should be understood to cover personal data held by corporations, since such data reveal intimate details of individuals' lives.

Text 2
Legal scholar Mori argues that privacy protections traditionally constrain the government, and that extending them to corporations would blur an important distinction between public power and private choice.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes Mori's likely response to Hale's argument?$q$, $q$["By claiming that privacy has never been protected","By cautioning that extending privacy rights to corporate data collection would conflate two different relationships","By arguing that personal data are never intimate","By agreeing that corporations should be bound exactly as governments are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mori worries that applying government-focused protections to corporations would blur an important distinction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Ecologist Amira Shah argues that reintroducing wolves to a national park has restored the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the elk population and allowing willows to regrow along streams.

Text 2
Ecologist Paul Dunn agrees that willows have regrown but notes that a concurrent period of heavy rainfall may also have contributed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes Dunn's attitude toward Shah's claim?$q$, $q$["Indifferent","Fully supportive","Partly accepting, while proposing another possible contributor","Fully dismissive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Dunn accepts willow regrowth but suggests rainfall as an additional factor.$q$),
  ($q$cs-463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Author Lin Zhao writes that reading fiction increases empathy because readers practice imagining others' thoughts.

Text 2
Psychologist Omar Reyes reports that studies of fiction and empathy have produced mixed results, with some showing gains and others none.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Reyes most likely characterize Zhao's claim?$q$, $q$["As more certain than the research warrants","As unrelated to empathy","As conclusively proven","As contradicting all research"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reyes says the evidence is mixed, so Zhao's confident claim goes beyond it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1
A park ranger says the trail is open year-round.

Text 2
A second ranger says the trail is open year-round except when snow covers the upper section.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the second ranger's statement?$q$, $q$["It contradicts the first ranger entirely.","It repeats the first statement exactly.","It describes a different trail.","It adds a qualification to the first ranger's statement."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second ranger adds an exception to 'open year-round'.$q$),
  ($q$cs-465$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1
Historian Karim argues that the empire fell mainly because of sustained military defeats along its borders.

