Historian Voss argues that the military defeats were symptoms rather than causes, since a decline in tax revenue had already weakened the army's ability to field troops.$q$, $q$Which statement best characterizes Voss's view of Karim's argument?$q$, $q$["Karim identifies real events but misreads their place in the chain of causes.","Karim's argument is correct as stated.","Karim overlooks that the empire never fell.","Karim fabricated the military defeats."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Voss accepts the defeats occurred but argues they resulted from fiscal decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-466$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Instead of presenting a single clear thesis, the essay ______ among several loosely connected ideas, leaving readers unsure of its point.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["converges","marches","meanders","plunges"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Moving among loosely connected ideas without a clear point is meandering.$q$),
  ($q$cs-467$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$After weeks of heavy rain, the ground was so ______ that the hikers' boots sank into the mud with every step.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["saturated","frozen","parched","brittle"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Boots sinking into mud indicates the ground was soaked, or saturated.$q$),
  ($q$cs-468$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The senator's critics accused her of ______, noting that she had publicly supported the bill for years and then voted against it without explanation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconsistency","candor","persistence","humility"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reversing a long-held position without explanation is inconsistency.$q$),
  ($q$cs-469$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The sculptor's work is ______ in its attention to detail; even the veins on the leaves of her bronze trees are carefully rendered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["abstract","minimal","slapdash","painstaking"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Careful rendering of tiny details is painstaking.$q$),
  ($q$cs-470$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Although the committee's report was officially neutral, its selective use of statistics ______ a preference for the first proposal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["retracted","concealed","contradicted","betrayed"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Selective statistics that reveal a preference betray it despite the claimed neutrality.$q$),
  ($q$cs-471$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The village's isolation ______ its traditions: with few outside influences, customs remained largely unchanged for generations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["threatened","complicated","erased","preserved"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Few outside influences meant customs stayed unchanged, so isolation "preserved" them.$q$),
  ($q$cs-472$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many people believe that goldfish have memories lasting only three seconds. In fact, experiments have shown that goldfish can learn to associate a particular sound with feeding time and remember the association for months. The myth likely persists because it is easy to repeat and because goldfish seem to swim aimlessly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text?$q$, $q$["It provides a definition of a key term used earlier in the text.","It introduces a new experiment that supports the claim in the first sentence.","It offers a possible explanation for why a mistaken belief endures.","It concludes that goldfish are more intelligent than other fish."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the text refutes a myth, the last sentence suggests why the myth persists.$q$),
  ($q$cs-473$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Although often credited to a single inventor, the modern bicycle evolved through contributions from many people. Early "running machines" had no pedals; later designs added pedals to the front wheel; and the "safety bicycle" of the 1880s introduced the chain drive and equal-sized wheels.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It challenges a simplified account and then outlines successive stages of development.","It argues that bicycles are less efficient than earlier machines.","It praises one inventor above all others.","It compares bicycles with other forms of transportation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text questions the "single inventor" idea and lists stages showing gradual evolution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-474$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Because the manuscript was written in an obscure script, scholars found it nearly ______ until a key to the writing system was discovered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["illustrated","indecipherable","valuable","fragile"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Needing a key to understand it implies the text could not be read: "indecipherable."$q$),
  ($q$cs-475$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: The decline of the tide pools is due mainly to pollution.

Text 2: Pollution is a factor, though data from the sites suggest that harvesting by visitors has had a greater effect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By suggesting that another cause has had a larger impact than the one Text 1 emphasizes","By arguing that visitors should be encouraged to harvest","By agreeing that pollution is the only cause","By denying that the tide pools have declined"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 grants pollution a role but says harvesting matters more.$q$),
  ($q$cs-476$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Scientists were ______ by the discovery; none had predicted that the species could survive at such depths.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reassured","unconcerned","astonished","bored"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because no one had predicted the finding, scientists were greatly surprised: "astonished."$q$),
  ($q$cs-477$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Museums should pay artists a fee whenever they loan works for exhibitions.

Text 2: Payment for loans is reasonable but could discourage smaller museums from borrowing, limiting public access to art.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By rejecting the idea of any compensation for artists","By claiming that large museums need no loans","By supporting the idea in principle while noting a possible drawback","By arguing that artists should never loan their works"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2 calls the idea reasonable but raises a concern about smaller museums.$q$),
  ($q$cs-478$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the evidence was ______, the prosecutor knew that a jury would need more than a hunch to convict, so she kept searching for corroboration.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["overwhelming","fabricated","circumstantial","irrelevant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Needing more than a hunch and seeking corroboration implies the evidence only indirectly pointed to guilt: "circumstantial."$q$),
  ($q$cs-479$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$In the early 1900s, city planners treated streets primarily as channels for traffic, widening them to move cars quickly. Today, many planners prioritize pedestrians, adding narrow lanes, benches, and trees. The shift reflects a growing recognition that streets can serve as public spaces, not merely routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It criticizes pedestrian-friendly design as impractical for modern cities.","It contrasts an earlier approach to street design with a current one and explains what motivates the change.","It describes a single street and traces how its appearance has changed.","It argues that traffic planners have ignored pedestrians entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text contrasts early-1900s planning with today's planning and ends by explaining the shift in thinking.$q$),
  ($q$cs-480$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Because chimpanzees use tools, they must share human-like cognition.

Text 2: Tool use alone is not strong evidence of human-like cognition; some insects also use tools through instinct.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By claiming that insects are more intelligent than chimpanzees","By arguing that the evidence cited does not establish the conclusion","By denying that chimpanzees use tools","By agreeing that all tool users have human-like minds"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 says tool use by itself doesn't establish cognition, noting insects use tools instinctively.$q$),
  ($q$cs-481$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Cities should convert vacant lots into community gardens, because gardens strengthen neighborhood ties and provide fresh produce.

Text 2: Community gardens are valuable, but converting every vacant lot overlooks the fact that some lots are better suited to housing, which cities urgently need.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By arguing that gardens provide no benefit to neighborhoods","By proposing that cities stop building housing altogether","By claiming that cities have no vacant lots available","By agreeing that gardens have benefits while arguing that some lots should serve other purposes"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Text 2 grants that gardens are valuable but argues against converting every lot, since housing may be a better use for some.$q$),
  ($q$cs-482$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The restaurant's rise has been ______: although it opened only a year ago, it already has a waiting list of several weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stagnant","meteoric","gradual","declining"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A fast and dramatic rise to popularity is "meteoric."$q$),
  ($q$cs-483$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Dr. Chen's team had expected the new alloy to be brittle at low temperatures. To their surprise, it grew stronger as the temperature dropped to −200°C. The researchers are now investigating the atomic structure that produces this unusual behavior.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It reveals a result that contradicts the expectation stated in the first sentence.","It defines a technical term from the first sentence.","It proposes a new research method.","It confirms the expectation stated in the first sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"To their surprise" signals that the finding contradicted the team's expectation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-484$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Schools should start later to benefit teenage sleep.

