  ($q$cs-485$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The film's ______ pacing left audiences restless; scenes dragged on long after their point had been made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["brisk","frantic","plodding","dazzling"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Scenes that drag on describe slow, "plodding" pacing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-486$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Rather than ______ the criticism, the director welcomed it, revising the film's ending in response.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismissing","requesting","adopting","summarizing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Rather than" signals a contrast with welcoming the criticism, so the missing word means to reject or brush aside: "dismissing."$q$),
  ($q$cs-487$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Imagine a library in which every book is blank except for a single sentence on its spine. This is roughly how early search engines saw the web: they indexed titles and a few keywords rather than the full text of pages. Modern search engines read entire pages, which allows them to answer questions the older systems could not.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text?$q$, $q$["It describes an actual library that was converted into a search engine.","It presents a problem that the text then leaves unsolved.","It offers an analogy that helps readers picture how early search engines worked.","It argues that libraries are obsolete."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first sentence is a hypothetical image that the second sentence explicitly connects to early search engines.$q$),
  ($q$cs-488$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$While some praised the new stadium as a bold civic investment, others viewed it as ______ spending the city could ill afford.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["extravagant","modest","temporary","essential"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Critics saw the stadium as an expense the city couldn't afford, so they considered it excessive: "extravagant."$q$),
  ($q$cs-489$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Electric cars are better for the environment than gasoline cars.

Text 2: The benefits of electric cars depend on how the electricity is generated; where it comes from coal, the advantages shrink considerably.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By claiming that gasoline cars are cleaner","By stating that the claim holds only under certain conditions","By arguing that electricity is never generated from coal","By agreeing that electric cars are always better"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 makes the benefit conditional on the power source.$q$),
  ($q$cs-490$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Translation always loses something essential from the original work.

Text 2: Translation changes a work, but it can also give it new life in another language, so loss is only part of the story.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By asserting that readers cannot appreciate translated works","By claiming that original works are not worth translating","By acknowledging that translation changes a work while emphasizing its positive effects","By arguing that translation never alters a work"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2 admits change but highlights gains, resisting the "always loses" framing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-491$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$For years, the city's water department insisted that the aging pipes posed no danger. Last month, however, tests found lead levels above federal limits in dozens of homes. The department has now promised to replace every lead pipe within a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares water departments in several cities.","It argues that federal limits for lead are too strict.","It reports a previously held position, introduces evidence that contradicts it, and describes a response.","It describes how pipes are manufactured and installed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text moves from the old position to contradicting test results to the department's promised action.$q$),
  ($q$cs-492$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A common critique of standardized testing is that it narrows the curriculum. Supporters respond that tests provide consistent data for identifying schools that need help. The disagreement may be less about the tests themselves than about how their results are used.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It argues that standardized tests should be abolished.","It presents data showing that testing improves achievement.","It describes the history of standardized testing.","It presents a criticism, a response to it, and an observation that reframes the disagreement."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The structure is critique, rebuttal, and then a reframing of what the dispute is really about.$q$),
  ($q$cs-493$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The dancer's performance was ______: every movement was precise, yet it never felt mechanical.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["careless","polished","tentative","exhausting"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Precise movements that still feel natural describe a refined performance: "polished."$q$),
  ($q$cs-494$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A four-day school week saves districts money without harming student achievement.

Text 2: The cost savings are real, but the achievement data come from only a handful of districts, so the claim of no harm is premature.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By proposing a five-day week in every district","By conceding the savings while questioning whether the evidence supports the claim about achievement","By insisting that achievement has clearly improved","By denying that four-day weeks save any money"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 accepts the savings but says the evidence about achievement is too limited.$q$),
  ($q$cs-495$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Her argument is ______: each claim follows logically from the one before, leaving little room for objection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ornamental","scattered","coherent","contradictory"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Claims that follow logically from one another make an argument "coherent."$q$),
  ($q$cs-496$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: The repetition in the poem conveys the speaker's despair.

