  ($q$cs-511$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Reading on paper improves comprehension compared with reading on screens.

Text 2: A recent study found no comprehension difference when screen readers were allowed to scroll freely and annotate.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By agreeing that screens always hinder comprehension","By pointing to evidence that the difference may disappear under certain conditions","By recommending that schools ban paper textbooks","By arguing that comprehension cannot be measured"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 cites a study in which the paper advantage vanished when readers could scroll and annotate.$q$),
  ($q$cs-512$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Some historians argue that the printing press, by making books cheap, caused the Protestant Reformation. Others counter that the Reformation depended less on technology than on political rivalries among European rulers. Both camps agree, however, that printed pamphlets spread reformers' ideas faster than any earlier medium.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the printing press from its invention to the present.","It presents two competing interpretations of an event and identifies a point on which they agree.","It argues that the printing press had no influence on European history.","It criticizes historians for disagreeing about the Reformation."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sentence one and two give competing views; sentence three ("Both camps agree") notes common ground.$q$),
  ($q$cs-513$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Dozens of languages are spoken in the Caucasus region, many of them found nowhere else. Linguists attribute this diversity to the area's rugged mountains, which kept communities isolated from one another for centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It contrasts the Caucasus with another region.","It questions the accuracy of the claim in the first sentence.","It offers an explanation for the phenomenon described in the first sentence.","It provides a historical timeline of the region's languages."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence gives the geographic reason for the linguistic diversity noted in the first.$q$),
  ($q$cs-514$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In the opening chapter, the author lingers on a deserted train station, noting its broken clocks and peeling posters. This imagery establishes a mood of abandonment that persists through the novel's first half, even as the characters' fortunes begin to change.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It contrasts the train station with a different setting.","It explains the effect of the description mentioned in the first sentence.","It introduces a character who appears later in the novel.","It argues that the opening chapter is the weakest part of the novel."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence says what the described imagery accomplishes: it sets a lasting mood.$q$),
  ($q$cs-515$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The explorer's account of the journey was so ______ that historians have struggled to separate fact from embellishment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unremarkable","concise","reliable","exaggerated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Historians struggle to separate fact from embellishment because the account contains a great deal of overstatement: "exaggerated."$q$),
  ($q$cs-516$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Early in her career, the senator was known for her ______ pursuit of legislation, often staying at the Capitol past midnight to negotiate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["halfhearted","tireless","reluctant","casual"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Working past midnight shows great persistence: "tireless."$q$),
  ($q$cs-517$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The artist's later work is notable for its ______: a few simple lines convey an entire landscape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["secrecy","excess","economy","complexity"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Conveying a landscape with only a few lines shows restraint and efficiency of means, i.e., "economy."$q$),
  ($q$cs-518$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The mayor's remarks were deliberately ______, offering neither support nor opposition to the proposal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["noncommittal","enthusiastic","emphatic","hostile"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Offering neither support nor opposition describes remarks that avoid taking a position: "noncommittal."$q$),
  ($q$cs-519$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: The narrator of the novel can be trusted, because his account is consistent with those of the other characters.

Text 2: Consistency with other characters' accounts is weak evidence, since those characters know the story only through the narrator's retelling.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the consistency Text 1 cites does not actually confirm the narrator's reliability","By claiming that the novel has no narrator","By agreeing that the narrator is fully trustworthy","By arguing that the other characters are unreliable narrators themselves"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 undermines the evidence by noting that the other accounts depend on the narrator.$q$),
  ($q$cs-520$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The hikers' ______ preparation paid off: when the storm struck, they had extra food, dry clothing, and a reliable map.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["minimal","thorough","hasty","reluctant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Having extra supplies for every contingency shows careful, complete preparation: "thorough."$q$),
  ($q$cs-521$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Researchers were careful not to ______ their findings, noting that the study involved only twenty participants.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["repeat","delay","conceal","overstate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Pointing out the small sample shows caution against claiming too much: "overstate."$q$),
  ($q$cs-522$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Linguists consider the dialect ______, since it has fewer than fifty remaining speakers and is rarely taught to children.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ancient","endangered","standardized","thriving"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Few speakers and no transmission to children indicate the dialect is at risk of disappearing: "endangered."$q$),
  ($q$cs-523$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$For decades, biologists assumed that plants respond to their environment only through slow growth. Recent experiments, however, show that some plants can detect the vibrations of a caterpillar's chewing and release defensive chemicals within minutes. These findings suggest that plant responses are faster and more sophisticated than previously believed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a long-held assumption, presents findings that complicate it, and states a resulting conclusion.","It compares two competing explanations and declares one of them more convincing.","It proposes a hypothesis and then lists several experiments that failed to support it.","It narrates a single researcher's career from early experiments to later recognition."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first sentence gives an old assumption, the second gives new evidence ("however") that complicates it, and the third states what the findings suggest.$q$),
  ($q$cs-524$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The octopus's arms contain more neurons than its central brain, which allows each arm to explore and react independently. A severed arm, for instance, will continue to grasp at objects for some time. This distributed arrangement helps explain how an octopus can manipulate several objects at once.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the argument of the entire text.","It raises an objection to the claim made in the first sentence.","It defines the term \"neurons.\"","It gives a specific example of the independence of the octopus's arms described in the first sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"For instance" signals that the second sentence offers an example of the arms' independence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-525$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The scientist cautioned that the early results, while ______, should not be treated as conclusive until they were replicated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["promising","disappointing","discredited","irrelevant"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cautioning against treating them as conclusive suggests the results look hopeful but need confirmation: "promising."$q$),
