  ($q$cs-617$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The festival's organizers were ______ in their attention to sustainability, banning plastic and composting all waste.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["scrupulous","negligent","indifferent","sporadic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Strict adherence to sustainability practices is scrupulous.$q$),
  ($q$cs-618$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The explorer's account, once dismissed as ______, was later vindicated when satellite images revealed the lost city he had described.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fanciful","accurate","routine","tedious"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Dismissal followed by vindication suggests the account had been seen as fanciful.$q$),
  ($q$cs-619$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Far from being ______ by the criticism, the author used it to sharpen her argument in the revised edition.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["amused","energized","informed","deterred"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Far from' sets up a contrast with using criticism productively, so 'deterred.'$q$),
  ($q$cs-620$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The merger was ______ by regulators, who feared it would reduce competition and raise prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["overlooked","hastened","applauded","scrutinized"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Regulators' fears imply close examination, or scrutiny.$q$),
  ($q$cs-621$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The poet's use of imagery is ______; a single image often carries several layers of meaning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["plain","sparse","arbitrary","resonant"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Images carrying multiple meanings are resonant.$q$),
  ($q$cs-622$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The new theory, though ______ in its elegance, fails to account for several well-documented observations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unfamiliar","seductive","unattractive","complicated"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'Though' contrasts elegance with failure to explain data: seductive but flawed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-623$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The mountain village is so ______ that the nearest hospital is a six-hour drive away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["remote","crowded","modern","prosperous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A distant hospital indicates remoteness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-624$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A passage describes a bakery's busy morning rush and ends with a single sentence: 'By ten, the shelves were bare and the baker finally sat down.'$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the last sentence?$q$, $q$["It compares the bakery with a rival.","It introduces a new character.","It marks the end of the rush and shows its effect on the baker.","It defines the word 'rush.'"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence concludes the scene by showing the result of the busy morning.$q$),
  ($q$cs-625$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The author begins with a brief story about getting lost on a hike. She then explains three basic navigation skills every hiker should learn.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening story?$q$, $q$["It argues against hiking.","It lists the three navigation skills.","It provides a map of the trail.","It introduces the topic by showing why the skills matter."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The anecdote motivates the skills the author goes on to explain.$q$),
  ($q$cs-626$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A scientist writes: 'Most people picture the deep ocean as empty. In fact, it teems with life that produces its own light.'$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence?$q$, $q$["It corrects a common misconception stated in the first.","It provides a definition of 'deep ocean.'","It repeats the first sentence in different words.","It introduces an unrelated topic."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence counters the belief described in the first.$q$),
  ($q$cs-627$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The author of an essay on typewriters notes that they have become popular again among young writers, and then suggests that the appeal lies in the absence of distractions such as notifications.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the second part of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare typewriters to modern printers.","To describe how typewriters work.","To offer an explanation for a trend described earlier.","To argue that typewriters should be banned."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The suggestion explains why typewriters are popular again.$q$),
  ($q$cs-628$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A passage describes a lighthouse keeper's solitary routine in precise, unemotional detail. The final line reads: 'He had not spoken aloud in eleven days.'$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the effect of the final line?$q$, $q$["It highlights the keeper's isolation through a stark fact.","It reveals the lighthouse's location.","It introduces a dispute with another character.","It shows the keeper enjoying his routine."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The blunt detail underscores how isolated the keeper is.$q$),
  ($q$cs-629$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A biologist begins by describing a puzzling observation: a certain frog species is thriving in a polluted pond. She then proposes two possible explanations and describes an experiment to test them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a puzzle, proposes explanations, and outlines a way to test them.","It contrasts two unrelated species.","It argues for a single conclusion without evidence.","It traces a historical timeline."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from observation to hypotheses to a test.$q$),
  ($q$cs-630$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In the first paragraph, a critic summarizes the plot of a controversial film. In the second, she argues that the film's critics have confused the characters' views with the filmmaker's own.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second paragraph?$q$, $q$["It criticizes the film's acting.","It summarizes the film's ending.","It compares the film with a novel.","It defends the film against a particular misreading."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second paragraph argues that critics have misread the film.$q$),
  ($q$cs-631$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A chapter on the history of refrigeration first explains how households once relied on ice delivered by horse-drawn wagons. It then argues that electric refrigerators, by removing that dependence, changed not only diets but also the layout of cities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence?$q$, $q$["It defines the term 'refrigerator.'","It rejects the idea that diets changed.","It establishes the earlier practice that the later development replaced.","It argues that ice delivery should return."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first sentence sets up the baseline the author then contrasts with electric refrigeration.$q$),
  ($q$cs-632$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The author notes that critics have praised the memoir for its honesty. She then observes that the memoir omits entire years of the author's life, and suggests that its candor is therefore selective.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence?$q$, $q$["It complicates the praise described in the first sentence.","It summarizes the memoir's plot.","It introduces an unrelated memoir.","It repeats the critics' praise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The omission complicates the claim that the memoir is fully honest.$q$),
  ($q$cs-633$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$In an essay about maps, the author begins: 'Every map lies.' She goes on to explain that flattening a sphere onto paper necessarily distorts either shape or area.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening statement?$q$, $q$["It offers a provocative claim that the rest of the text explains.","It asks readers to draw their own map.","It states a fact that the author later denies.","It describes a specific map in detail."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bold statement is clarified by the explanation of map distortion.$q$),
  ($q$cs-634$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A text describes how a village celebrates the first snowfall each year with a communal meal. It concludes that the tradition helps neighbors feel connected.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A seasonal tradition helps strengthen a sense of community.","Meals should be shared only in winter.","Snow is rare in the region.","The village is cold all year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text focuses on the tradition's connecting effect.$q$),
  ($q$cs-635$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A scientist describes an experiment in which plants exposed to classical music grew slightly taller than those in silence. She cautions that the difference was small and may not be meaningful.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the scientist's caution?$q$, $q$["It rejects the experiment's design entirely.","It tempers the significance of the experiment's result.","It confirms that music causes plants to grow.","It introduces a new experiment."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The caution limits how much to read into the small difference.$q$),
  ($q$cs-636$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The author contrasts the quiet of a library in the morning with its bustle after school. She writes that the same room 'seems to hold two different lives.'$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence?$q$, $q$["It describes the library's collection.","It argues for longer library hours.","It introduces a new topic.","It sums up the contrast with a figurative image."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The figurative statement encapsulates the contrast the author drew.$q$),
  ($q$cs-637$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A scholar argues that early photographs of Native communities, though often treated as neutral records, reflected the photographers' choices about what to include and what to omit.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the technology used in early cameras.","To catalog the communities depicted.","To challenge the assumption that the photographs are neutral documents.","To argue that photographs should be destroyed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The scholar questions the neutrality of the photographs.$q$),
  ($q$cs-638$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The author of a book review opens by quoting a famous line from the book, then argues that the line, often cited as the book's thesis, is actually contradicted by the book's later chapters.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening quotation?$q$, $q$["It introduces a commonly cited idea that the review then challenges.","It provides biographical details about the author.","It illustrates a point about grammar.","It summarizes the review's main conclusion."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The quotation sets up the popular reading the reviewer disputes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-639$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Nina believes the town should build a new playground in the park.

Text 2: Omar believes the town should repair the existing playground instead of building a new one.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two views?$q$, $q$["They both oppose any playground.","They agree on the same plan.","They discuss unrelated topics.","They propose different ways of addressing playground needs."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Nina favors building new; Omar favors repairing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-640$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: A guidebook states that the old fort is open to visitors every day.

Text 2: A recent notice states that the fort is closed on Mondays for repairs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the notice relates to the guidebook?$q$, $q$["It confirms the guidebook exactly.","It describes a different fort.","It argues the fort should be demolished.","It indicates that the guidebook's information is no longer fully accurate."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The notice adds an exception that contradicts 'every day.'$q$),
  ($q$cs-641$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Dr. Silva argues that students retain more when they take notes by hand.

Text 2: Dr. Kim argues that typed notes are just as effective, provided students review them regularly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Dr. Kim would most likely respond to Dr. Silva?$q$, $q$["By arguing students should not review notes.","By arguing that the key factor is review, not the note-taking medium.","By saying notes are irrelevant to learning.","By agreeing that handwriting is always superior."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Kim emphasizes review over medium, qualifying Silva's claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-642$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A naturalist argues that wolves should be reintroduced to the region to restore ecological balance.

Text 2: A rancher argues that wolf reintroduction threatens livestock and livelihoods.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the difference between the two writers' concerns?$q$, $q$["Neither mentions wolves.","The naturalist emphasizes livestock, while the rancher emphasizes ecosystems.","The naturalist emphasizes ecological benefits, while the rancher emphasizes economic risks.","Both emphasize economic benefits."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$One focuses on ecology; the other on economic harm.$q$),
  ($q$cs-643$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Critic A believes the new stadium will revitalize downtown.

Text 2: Critic B believes the stadium will drain public funds that could improve schools.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["It confirms the revitalization claim.","It restates Text 1.","It raises a cost not considered in Text 1.","It describes the stadium's design."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2 introduces the opportunity cost of public funds.$q$),
  ($q$cs-644$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: A botanist states that the orchid blooms only in the spring.

Text 2: A gardener reports seeing the same orchid in bloom in autumn after an unusually warm summer.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["It provides an observation that complicates Text 1's claim.","It describes how to grow orchids.","It argues that orchids are not plants.","It confirms Text 1's claim."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The autumn bloom complicates the claim that it blooms only in spring.$q$),
  ($q$cs-645$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Philosopher Adler argues that moral rules should be judged solely by their consequences.

Text 2: Philosopher Bhatt argues that some acts, such as betraying a promise, are wrong regardless of their consequences.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the central disagreement between Adler and Bhatt?$q$, $q$["Whether consequences can be measured.","Whether the morality of an act depends entirely on its outcomes.","Whether promises can be kept.","Whether philosophers should study ethics."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adler is a consequentialist; Bhatt holds that some acts are wrong in themselves.$q$),
  ($q$cs-646$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Linguist Park claims that children acquire grammar mainly through innate mental structures.

Text 2: Linguist Reyes argues that exposure to language and social interaction are the primary drivers, with innate abilities playing a supporting role.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes Reyes's position relative to Park's?$q$, $q$["Reyes denies that children learn language.","Reyes gives more weight to experience than to innate structures.","Reyes argues that grammar does not exist.","Reyes agrees that innate structures are the main factor."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reyes reverses the emphasis: experience primary, innate abilities supporting.$q$),
  ($q$cs-647$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: An art historian claims that the painting is a forgery because its pigments were unavailable in the artist's lifetime.

Text 2: A chemist notes that the pigment in question was recently found in earlier workshops, suggesting it was in limited use decades earlier than assumed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the chemist's finding relates to the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["It strengthens the forgery claim.","It weakens a key piece of evidence for the forgery claim.","It proves the painting is authentic.","It is unrelated to the pigment's date."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The finding undermines the premise that the pigment was unavailable at the time.$q$),
  ($q$cs-648$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A city planner argues that bike lanes reduce traffic by encouraging cycling.

Text 2: A study of three cities found that bike lanes increased cycling but did not measurably change car traffic.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the study relates to the planner's argument?$q$, $q$["It fully confirms the planner's argument.","It supports part of the argument while casting doubt on the claimed effect on traffic.","It is irrelevant to the argument.","It argues cycling decreased."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The study confirms increased cycling but not reduced traffic.$q$),
  ($q$cs-649$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Sam thinks the school should start later so students can sleep more.

Text 2: Dana thinks the school should keep its current start time because later starts would interfere with after-school activities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Dana's view differs from Sam's?$q$, $q$["Dana wants school to start earlier.","Dana thinks students sleep too much.","Dana prioritizes after-school activities, while Sam prioritizes sleep.","Sam and Dana want the same schedule."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$They weigh different priorities: sleep versus activities.$q$),
  ($q$cs-650$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: A report says that remote work has increased employee satisfaction.

Text 2: A manager argues that satisfaction is high but collaboration has suffered.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the manager's view relates to the report?$q$, $q$["It argues satisfaction fell.","It accepts the report's finding but identifies a trade-off.","It restates the report.","It rejects the report's finding."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The manager concedes satisfaction rose but adds a cost.$q$),
  ($q$cs-651$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: An author argues that classic novels should remain required reading.

Text 2: Another author argues that students should choose their own books to foster a love of reading.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the disagreement?$q$, $q$["Whether libraries should be open.","Whether reading assignments should be fixed or chosen by students.","Whether novels exist.","Whether reading is a useful skill."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$They disagree about required versus self-selected reading.$q$),
  ($q$cs-652$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A geologist claims the crater was formed by a meteorite impact, citing shocked quartz found at the site.

Text 2: Another geologist argues that shocked quartz can also be produced by volcanic eruptions, so it is not conclusive evidence of impact.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["It argues the crater is not a crater.","It questions whether Text 1's evidence uniquely supports its conclusion.","It restates Text 1's evidence.","It supplies additional evidence for an impact."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 argues the cited evidence is consistent with another cause.$q$),
  ($q$cs-653$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A sociologist argues that social media has weakened community ties.

Text 2: A researcher finds that social media users report more frequent contact with distant friends, though less with neighbors.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the researcher's finding relates to the sociologist's claim?$q$, $q$["It argues neighbors are more important.","It complicates the claim by showing that connection shifted rather than simply declined.","It shows social media has no effect.","It confirms that all community ties weakened."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Contact with distant friends rose even as neighbor contact fell, complicating a simple decline story.$q$),
  ($q$cs-654$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A nutritionist says the study proves that eating nuts reduces heart disease.

Text 2: A reviewer points out that the study was observational and cannot establish that nuts caused the reduction.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the reviewer's main point?$q$, $q$["Heart disease cannot be measured.","The study's sample was too large.","Nuts are unhealthy.","The study's design does not support the causal conclusion drawn."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Observational studies show association, not causation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-655$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The tiny village was ______, with only a few dozen houses and one shop.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["modern","small","enormous","famous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A few dozen houses and one shop describe a small village.$q$),
  ($q$cs-656$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The critic's review was ______, listing both the film's strengths and its flaws without favoring either.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismissive","scathing","balanced","glowing"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Weighing strengths and flaws equally is balanced.$q$),
  ($q$cs-657$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The council's ______ approach, reversing course whenever public opinion shifted, made long-term planning impossible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cautious","systematic","steadfast","vacillating"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Reversing with every shift in opinion is vacillating.$q$),
  ($q$cs-658$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Her ______ prose, free of jargon and unnecessary qualification, made the dense subject accessible to general readers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["guarded","opaque","lucid","florid"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Free of jargon and making a dense subject accessible describes lucid prose.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For much of the twentieth century, ecologists treated dead trees as waste—debris to be cleared so that healthy forests could grow. <u>Yet a standing dead tree, or snag, may host more life than a living one.</u> Woodpeckers excavate nest cavities in its softening wood; later, owls, bats, and flying squirrels move into the abandoned holes. Fungi and beetles break down the trunk, returning nutrients to the soil. Forest managers in many regions now deliberately leave snags in place, recognizing that removing them can impoverish the very ecosystems they hoped to protect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the evidence that led ecologists in the twentieth century to treat dead trees as waste.","It presents a hypothesis about snags that the text goes on to question by describing the ways in which dead trees can endanger the health of the living trees around them.","It identifies a practice of modern forest managers that the text later argues is ineffective.","It introduces a claim that challenges the view described in the previous sentence and that the rest of the text supports with examples."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence ("Yet a standing dead tree... may host more life") contradicts the earlier view of dead trees as waste. The following sentences give examples of the life snags support, and the final sentence describes how managers now act on this idea.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1957, the economist Hideo Tanabe predicted that Japan's rapid industrial growth would eventually strain its supply of fresh water. Planners largely ignored him; the country's rivers were abundant, and rainfall was reliable. By the 1970s, however, groundwater pumping in several industrial cities had caused the land to sink by as much as four meters, cracking foundations and worsening floods. Only then did the government impose strict limits on pumping and invest in recycling industrial water—measures that, in many cases, Tanabe had proposed two decades earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Japan's rapid industrial growth after 1957 was unsustainable and should have been slowed","To explain why pumping groundwater from beneath industrial cities causes the land to sink and how sinking land can make flooding in those cities worse","To show that a warning initially dismissed proved accurate and that the measures eventually taken echoed the original proposal","To compare the economic policies of Japan in the 1950s with those it adopted in the 1970s"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text traces Tanabe's ignored prediction, the problems that later confirmed it, and the eventual adoption of measures he had proposed. The other choices focus on secondary details or make claims the text does not.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most accounts of the printing press focus on the books it produced. Historian Elena Varga argues that its most transformative product was something more ephemeral: the pamphlet. Cheap, quick to print, and easy to pass from hand to hand, pamphlets allowed ordinary people to follow—and join—debates that had once been confined to scholars and clergy. During the religious conflicts of the sixteenth century, Varga notes, pamphlets circulated in such numbers that authorities found it impossible to suppress them, and public opinion became, for the first time, a force that rulers had to reckon with.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes the invention of the printing press and then traces its effect on the production of books in sixteenth-century Europe.","It presents a historian's argument that an overlooked product of the printing press had a major social effect, then explains the reasoning behind that argument.","It compares the influence of books and pamphlets and concludes that both were equally important to religious debates.","It explains why authorities in the sixteenth century tried and failed to suppress pamphlets, and it then argues that books were less important than pamphlets in that era."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text first notes the usual focus on books, then introduces Varga's claim that pamphlets were more transformative, and explains why (cheapness, wide circulation, the rise of public opinion).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The bar-tailed godwit, a shorebird weighing about 300 grams, makes one of the most extraordinary journeys in the animal kingdom: a nonstop flight of more than 11,000 kilometers from Alaska to New Zealand. <u>Before departing, a godwit nearly doubles its body weight, storing fat to fuel the trip, while organs it won't need in flight, such as its gizzard, shrink dramatically.</u> During the flight, which lasts about nine days, the bird neither eats nor drinks, and it is thought to sleep only by resting one half of its brain at a time.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains some of the physical changes that make the godwit's journey possible.","It introduces a scientific debate about how the godwit navigates over open ocean.","It provides evidence that the godwit's flight is longer than that of any other bird.","To compare the everyday meaning of the word \"theory\" with its meaning in science and to argue that the scientific meaning should replace the everyday one"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence describes how the godwit prepares physically—storing fat and shrinking unneeded organs—which helps explain how such a long flight is possible. It doesn't address navigation or post-flight health, and it offers no comparison with other birds.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Composer Marisol Ibarra was born in Havana and moved to New York in her twenties, intending to stay only briefly. Instead, she became the first music director of a newly founded Harlem dance company. Her own compositions blend Cuban son and Afro-Cuban rhythms with techniques drawn from European modernism, yet she has long resisted labels that would confine her to a single tradition. <u>Asked once whether she considered herself a Cuban composer or an American one, she replied that she considered herself simply a composer.</u> Her orchestral works are now performed by ensembles on four continents.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Ibarra chose to remain in New York rather than return to Havana.","It suggests that Ibarra's music draws more heavily on European modernism than on Cuban music.","The text describes a problem facing a fishing village, explains the villagers' view of its cause, and then shows how a scientist's research helped the village solve it.","It illustrates Ibarra's reluctance to be identified with any single musical or national tradition."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence follows directly from the statement that Ibarra "has long resisted labels that would confine her to a single tradition," and it illustrates that resistance through her own answer.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that a scientific theory is a guess that has not yet been proven. In science, however, a theory is something quite different: a well-tested explanation that accounts for a wide range of observations and has survived repeated attempts to disprove it. The theory of plate tectonics, for instance, explains the distribution of earthquakes, the shapes of the continents, and the ages of rocks on the ocean floor. Calling it "just a theory" misunderstands the term; in science, "theory" represents one of the highest levels of confidence an explanation can achieve.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that the theory of plate tectonics should be considered a scientific law rather than a theory","To describe the process by which scientists test and eventually prove a theory","To correct a common misunderstanding of a term by explaining its scientific meaning and illustrating it with an example","To compare the everyday meaning of \"theory\" with its meaning in other fields such as law and philosophy"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text begins with a common assumption about "theory," corrects it by explaining the scientific meaning, and gives plate tectonics as an example.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the early 1900s, the residents of a Norwegian fishing village noticed that their catches of cod rose and fell sharply from one period of years to the next. Local fishers attributed the swings to luck or weather. A marine biologist working in the region suspected something else. By measuring the age of thousands of fish using the growth rings in their scales, he found that a single exceptionally successful spawning year could dominate the catch for several years afterward, as that generation of fish grew large enough to be caught. The swings, in other words, reflected the uneven survival of young fish, not chance.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a scientist's method, explains why fishers rejected it, and then describes how the method was eventually accepted.","The text describes a pattern, notes a popular explanation for it, and then presents a scientist's alternative explanation along with the evidence for it.","To describe how dolphins use echolocation to navigate and communicate, and to explain how a study showed that echolocation requires a large brain","The text describes a problem facing a fishing village and then explains how a scientist's research helped solve it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the pattern in the catches, mentions the fishers' explanation (luck or weather), and then explains the biologist's alternative explanation and the evidence for it (fish ages from scale rings).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The architect Lina Bo Bardi designed the São Paulo Museum of Art so that its main gallery would appear to float: the entire building is suspended between four massive red pillars, leaving a public plaza open beneath it. Inside, she rejected the conventional practice of hanging paintings on walls. Instead, she mounted each work on a sheet of clear glass set in a concrete block, so that visitors could wander among the paintings as if through a forest. <u>Bo Bardi believed that museums too often intimidated ordinary people and that art should be encountered as part of everyday life.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a rationale for the unconventional design choices described earlier in the text.","It describes a criticism that other architects made of Bo Bardi's design.","It explains why the São Paulo Museum of Art is supported by four red pillars.","It suggests that Bo Bardi's design was more popular with visitors than with art critics."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence explains the beliefs behind Bo Bardi's choices—the open plaza and the glass mounts that let visitors wander—so it gives the reasoning for the design described before it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some scientists have proposed that the large brains of dolphins evolved to support complex social relationships, since dolphins live in groups whose membership shifts constantly. Others have argued that dolphins' brains are large mainly because echolocation—the processing of returning sound waves to "see" underwater—demands enormous computing power. A study comparing dozens of whale and dolphin species found that brain size was more closely linked to the size and complexity of social groups than to reliance on echolocation, though its authors acknowledged that the two factors may have reinforced each other.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a technological change, presents early evidence about its effects, and then argues that the change permanently harmed film as an art form.","To describe how dolphins use echolocation to navigate and communicate within groups","To explain why the social groups of dolphins shift constantly in membership","To present two hypotheses about a trait and describe a study whose results favored one while not ruling out the other"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents the social and echolocation hypotheses, then describes a study that found social group size more closely linked to brain size, while acknowledging that both factors may have played a role.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel O Pioneers! Alexandra Bergson has inherited her father's struggling farm on the Nebraska prairie.<br><br>For the first time, perhaps, since that land emerged from the waters of geologic ages, a human face was set toward it with love and yearning. It seemed beautiful to her, rich and strong and glorious. Her eyes drank in the breadth of it, until her tears blinded her. <u>Then the Genius of the Divide, the great, free spirit which breathes across it, must have bent lower than it ever bent to a human will before.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It indicates that Alexandra is overwhelmed by the harshness of the prairie and doubts her ability to farm it.","It shifts the focus from Alexandra's feelings to a description of the prairie's weather and landscape.","It suggests that the land itself responds to Alexandra's devotion, emphasizing the depth of her connection to it.","It reveals that Alexandra's neighbors regard her love for the land as foolish and naive."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After describing Alexandra's love for the land, the narrator imagines the spirit of the land "bend[ing]" to her will, suggesting a mutual relationship and emphasizing how deeply she is connected to the land.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the first sound films appeared in the late 1920s, many critics predicted the decline of film as an art form. Silent cinema, they argued, had developed a sophisticated visual language—expressive faces, inventive camera angles, montage—that dialogue would make unnecessary. Early sound films seemed to confirm these fears: bulky microphones forced actors to stand still, and cameras were confined to soundproof booths. Within a few years, however, technical advances freed both cameras and actors, and directors began using sound creatively, layering music, dialogue, and noise to create effects silent films could not achieve.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a technological change and then argues that the change permanently harmed film as an art form.","The text describes a prediction, presents early evidence that seemed to support it, and then explains developments that undercut it.","The text compares silent and sound films and concludes that silent films were more artistically sophisticated.","It introduces a method of estimating the snow leopard population that researchers continue to rely on despite its limitations."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text presents critics' prediction, notes that early sound films seemed to confirm it, and then explains how technical advances and creative use of sound undermined it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Snow leopards are notoriously difficult to study. They live at altitudes above 3,000 meters, range over enormous territories, and are so well camouflaged against rocky slopes that researchers sometimes pass within meters of one without noticing. <u>For decades, estimates of the global population rested largely on guesswork.</u> In recent years, however, scientists have combined camera traps, genetic analysis of droppings, and satellite collars to produce far more reliable counts, which suggest that between 4,000 and 6,500 snow leopards remain in the wild.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a limitation in earlier knowledge that the text goes on to say has been partly overcome.","To argue that Felon should be read primarily as a work of political argument rather than as poetry, since it raises questions about society","It provides evidence that the snow leopard population has declined over several decades.","It introduces a method that scientists still use to estimate the number of snow leopards."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence notes that population estimates used to be unreliable; the next sentence describes new methods that have produced more reliable counts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In her poetry collection Felon, Reginald Dwayne Betts draws on his own experience of incarceration. Rather than presenting prison as a distant, abstract institution, the poems focus on concrete details: the sound of a cell door, the texture of a letter from home, the paperwork required to seek a job after release. Some critics have described the collection as political, and in a sense it is; it raises questions about how society treats people who have served their sentences. But Betts largely avoids direct argument, allowing the accumulated details to make his case for him.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It provides the main conclusion of the text, summarizing what the evidence in the preceding sentences reveals about how Teotihuacan was governed.","To summarize the events of the poet's life that are described in Felon","To compare Felon with other poetry collections that deal with the experience of incarceration","To describe the approach a poet takes in a collection and explain how that approach shapes the collection's political dimension"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes Betts's focus on concrete details and explains that, while the collection has a political dimension, it makes its case through details rather than direct argument.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient city of Teotihuacan, in what is now central Mexico, was one of the largest cities in the world around 500 CE, with perhaps 100,000 residents. Unlike many ancient cities, it appears to have had no single ruler celebrated in monuments or inscriptions. Archaeologists have found no royal tombs and no images of individual kings. <u>Some researchers have proposed that Teotihuacan was governed collectively, perhaps by a council representing the city's neighborhoods.</u> Others caution that the evidence is ambiguous: rulers may simply have chosen not to depict themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides the conclusion of the text, summarizing the evidence presented in the preceding sentences.","It introduces a new piece of evidence that contradicts the earlier claim that Teotihuacan had no royal tombs.","It offers one interpretation of the evidence described earlier, which the following sentence notes is not the only possible interpretation.","It explains why Teotihuacan grew to be one of the largest cities in the world around 500 CE."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence proposes collective governance as an explanation for the lack of royal monuments. The next sentence notes that the evidence could also be explained differently.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Mosquitoes find people partly by detecting the carbon dioxide we exhale, but carbon dioxide alone does not explain why some people seem to attract far more bites than others. Neuroscientist Leslie Vosshall and her colleagues spent three years testing the skin odors of volunteers, who wore nylon stockings on their arms to collect their scent. The researchers placed the stockings in pairs in a chamber and released mosquitoes to see which they preferred. One volunteer, nicknamed "Subject 33," was consistently far more attractive than everyone else. Chemical analysis revealed that the most attractive volunteers produced unusually high levels of certain fatty acids on their skin.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that carbon dioxide is the main factor mosquitoes use to locate people","To describe a study that helped explain why some people attract more mosquitoes than others","To explain how mosquitoes use their sense of smell to find water for laying eggs","To describe the challenges researchers faced in recruiting volunteers for a long-term study"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text introduces the puzzle of why some people attract more mosquitoes, then describes Vosshall's study and its finding about fatty acids.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Inés, who is sixteen, has walked out to the orchard behind her grandmother's house on the first warm afternoon of spring.<br><br>She lay on her back beneath the oldest of the pear trees and let the hum of the bees settle over her like a second skin. The light came down through the blossoms in pieces, gold and white and gold again, and the breeze moved the branches as if the whole tree were breathing. She watched a single bee, heavy with pollen, sink into the heart of a flower, and the flower seemed to bend to meet it. Something she had no words for opened inside her then, as wide and bright as the sky beyond the branches.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It portrays a moment in which a character experiences an intense awakening while observing the natural world.","It contrasts a character's peaceful surroundings with her growing sense of unease about the future.","It explains why researchers initially doubted the age of the evidence found at Monte Verde and how they eventually confirmed that age.","It establishes a conflict between a character and the family members who disapprove of her daydreaming."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text vividly describes Inés lying under a pear tree, watching a bee enter a blossom, and feeling "something she had no words for" open inside her—a moment of awakening. There is no sense of unease, no scientific purpose, and no conflict with others.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For years, archaeologists believed that the first Americans arrived from Asia about 13,000 years ago, walking through an ice-free corridor that opened between two massive ice sheets in what is now Canada. Then, in the 1970s, excavations at Monte Verde in southern Chile uncovered evidence of human habitation dating to at least 14,500 years ago. <u>The finding posed a puzzle: the ice-free corridor had not yet opened at that time.</u> Many researchers now believe that the first Americans traveled along the Pacific coast, perhaps by boat, rather than through the interior.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that supports the original theory about how the first Americans arrived.","It explains why researchers initially doubted the age of the evidence found at Monte Verde.","It introduces a new archaeological site that challenges the findings at Monte Verde.","It identifies a problem raised by a discovery, which the following sentence offers a possible way to resolve."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence explains why the Monte Verde finding is puzzling (people arrived before the corridor existed), and the next sentence proposes the coastal route as a solution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Critics have often described the paintings of Agnes Martin as minimalist: most consist of little more than faint pencil grids or pale horizontal bands on a square canvas. Martin herself rejected the label. Minimalism, as she understood it, aimed to strip art of emotion and reference, presenting objects simply as objects. Her own work, she insisted, was about feelings—innocence, happiness, the experience of beauty—that she believed were best evoked by the simplest possible means. The spareness of her canvases, in other words, served a purpose quite different from the one critics assumed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that minimalist art is more emotionally expressive than critics have recognized","To describe the techniques an artist used to create pencil grids and horizontal bands","To explain why an artist rejected a label commonly applied to her work","It presents the main conclusion of the text, which the rest of the text then supports with additional examples of patients with similar injuries."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that critics called Martin's work minimalist but that she rejected this label because her spare paintings were intended to evoke feelings, unlike minimalism's aims.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1848, a railroad construction foreman named Phineas Gage survived an accident in which an iron rod passed completely through his skull, destroying much of the left frontal lobe of his brain. He could walk, speak, and remember, but according to his physician, his personality changed: once reliable and well liked, he became impulsive and profane. <u>Gage's case became one of the most frequently cited in the history of neuroscience, offering early evidence that specific regions of the brain are associated with personality and behavior.</u> Recent historians, however, note that the most dramatic accounts of his transformation were written years after his death and may have been exaggerated.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the medical treatment Gage received after his accident.","It explains the historical significance of the case described earlier, before the text introduces a reason to question some accounts of it.","It argues that Gage's personality change was caused by factors other than his brain injury.","To explain why large chocolate companies have pledged to pay premiums for cacao certified as sustainably grown, and to evaluate those pledges"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence describes why Gage's case mattered to neuroscience. The final sentence then notes that some accounts of his change may be exaggerated.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most of the world's chocolate comes from cacao grown on small family farms in West Africa, where farmers typically earn a tiny fraction of the price consumers pay for a chocolate bar. Several large companies have pledged to improve farmers' incomes, often by paying small premiums for cacao certified as sustainably grown. Economist Ama Owusu-Ansah argues that such premiums, while welcome, do little to address the underlying problem: farmers have almost no bargaining power, since they sell to intermediaries who set prices. Lasting change, he contends, would require farmers to organize into cooperatives large enough to negotiate directly with buyers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To present an economist's view that a common approach to a problem is insufficient and to describe the alternative he proposes","To describe the process by which cacao is grown, harvested, and sold to chocolate companies","To argue that consumers should pay more for chocolate in order to raise farmers' incomes","To explain why large companies have pledged to pay premiums for sustainably grown cacao"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the problem, notes companies' premiums, then presents Owusu-Ansah's argument that premiums are insufficient and that cooperatives are needed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Urban planners have long assumed that wider roads reduce traffic. Transportation researcher Delia Marsh argues that the opposite is often true. When a road is widened, driving along it becomes faster, at least at first, and people who had been avoiding the route—or taking buses, or not making the trip at all—begin to drive it. <u>Within a few years, Marsh found, traffic on widened roads in the cities she studied typically returned to its original level of congestion, now with more cars.</u> Her conclusion: building more lanes rarely solves congestion and may simply produce more driving.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes an exception to the pattern the researcher observed in most cities.","It explains why people choose to drive rather than take public transportation.","It introduces a counterargument to the researcher's view, which the text then refutes by explaining how wider roads attract new drivers.","It presents a finding that supports the researcher's argument against a common assumption."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence reports Marsh's finding that widened roads returned to congestion with more cars—evidence for her argument that widening roads doesn't reduce traffic, contrary to planners' assumption.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Teodor, an elderly clockmaker, is speaking to his apprentice.<br><br>"You think the work is in the hands," he said, setting down the tiny brass gear he had been polishing for most of the afternoon. "Everyone thinks so at the beginning. They want to be fast. They want to see a clock running by the end of the week." He held the gear up to the window so that the light caught each of its teeth. "But a clock is mostly patience. The hands only do what the patience allows."$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show that a character regrets having spent his life making clocks instead of pursuing another career","To describe in technical detail how the gears of a clock are made and polished","To convey a character's view that careful, unhurried attention matters more than speed or skill alone","To reveal a character's frustration that his apprentice is unwilling to learn"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Teodor contrasts the beginner's desire for speed with his belief that "a clock is mostly patience," conveying his view that patient attention matters more than quick handiwork.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The kakapo, a flightless parrot from New Zealand, breeds only in years when the rimu tree produces an unusually large crop of fruit—which happens just once every two to four years. Conservationists once considered this a problem to be overcome, and they tried feeding the parrots supplementary food to encourage breeding every year. <u>The birds ignored the extra food and continued to wait for the rimu.</u> Researchers now believe the parrots may be responding to a specific nutrient in the fruit, and they are studying whether supplements containing it could succeed where general feeding failed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that kakapo populations are declining because the rimu tree produces large crops of fruit only once every few years.","It reports the outcome of an effort described in the previous sentence, setting up the new approach described next.","It explains why the rimu tree produces large crops of fruit only every few years.","It suggests that conservationists should stop trying to influence kakapo breeding altogether."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence shows that supplementary feeding failed; the next sentence explains researchers' new idea (a specific nutrient) prompted by that failure.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians have often portrayed the medieval guilds of Europe as rigid organizations that stifled innovation, protecting established craftsmen by limiting competition. Economic historian Hana Novák offers a more mixed assessment. Guilds did restrict who could practice a trade, she acknowledges, and some resisted new techniques that threatened their members' livelihoods. But they also trained apprentices systematically, enforced quality standards that made goods more reliable, and pooled resources to help members through hard times. In many cases, Novák argues, these functions made guilds engines of skill and stability rather than mere barriers to progress.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To present a historian's argument that a common view of an institution is incomplete","To explain how medieval guilds trained apprentices and enforced quality standards, and why these practices limited competition","To argue that guilds were the main cause of economic growth in medieval Europe","To describe why medieval craftsmen resisted new techniques"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the usual negative view of guilds and then presents Novák's more balanced assessment, which acknowledges the drawbacks but adds positive functions—showing that the common view is incomplete.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Desert ants of the genus Cataglyphis forage far from their nests across featureless sand, yet they return home in a nearly straight line. How do they know the way? Experiments have shown that the ants count their steps. When researchers attached tiny stilts to the legs of ants that had just reached a food source, the ants overshot their nests on the return trip; when researchers shortened the ants' legs, the ants stopped short. <u>The ants, it seems, keep track of distance by something like a pedometer, combined with a sense of direction from the pattern of polarized light in the sky.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a hypothesis that the experiment described earlier failed to support.","It introduces a question that the rest of the text attempts to answer.","It offers an alternative explanation that contradicts the results of the experiment.","It states a conclusion drawn from the experiment described earlier."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence draws the conclusion (ants track distance by counting steps, plus a sense of direction) suggested by the stilt experiment.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the late nineteenth century, many American cities were plagued by horse manure: tens of thousands of horses pulled carriages, wagons, and streetcars, and the waste piled up faster than it could be removed. Some commentators predicted that cities would become uninhabitable. The problem was not solved by better sanitation but by the automobile, which within a few decades replaced the horse almost entirely. The episode is sometimes cited as a warning against assuming that present trends will continue indefinitely; a seemingly intractable problem was resolved not by confronting it directly but by a technological change that made it irrelevant.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a historical problem and then argues that the solution created new problems that were even worse.","The text compares two approaches to solving a problem and concludes that the more direct approach was more effective.","The text describes a historical problem, explains how it was ultimately resolved, and then draws a broader lesson from the episode.","The text describes predictions made about a problem and then explains why those predictions came true."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes the manure problem, notes that the automobile resolved it, and then explains the broader lesson (present trends may not continue).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The violinist Hyun-ji Park has recorded all six of Bach's sonatas and partitas for solo violin twice, once at age twenty-four and again at fifty. The two recordings could hardly be more different. The first is brilliant and fast, emphasizing the music's technical difficulty. The second is slower and more spacious; Park lingers over harmonies and lets silences stretch. <u>In an interview, Park said that in her youth she wanted to prove she could play the music, while now she simply wants to listen to it as she plays.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that critics generally prefer the first recording to the second.","It offers the performer's own explanation for the contrast between the two recordings described earlier.","It describes a technical challenge that Park overcame between the two recordings.","It indicates that Park now regrets the faster, more brilliant approach she took when she made her first recording of the music."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After describing the differences between the two recordings, the text gives Park's own account of why they differ—a change in her goals as a performer.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most people assume that a fossil is formed when an animal's bones turn to stone. In fact, fossilization can happen in several ways. Bones may be gradually replaced, molecule by molecule, with minerals from groundwater—the process most people imagine. But an animal can also leave an impression in mud that hardens, preserving the shape of its body without any of the original material. Insects trapped in tree resin can be preserved almost intact in amber. And in some rare cases, such as the woolly mammoths found in Siberian permafrost, freezing preserves even skin and hair.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It corrects a common assumption by describing several different ways that fossils can form.","It argues that fossils preserved in amber are more scientifically valuable than other kinds.","It explains why most animals never become fossils after they die.","It describes the discovery of woolly mammoths in Siberian permafrost."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text begins with a common but incomplete assumption and then describes multiple processes of fossilization, broadening the reader's understanding.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The jazz pianist Thelonious Monk was often accused by early critics of playing "wrong" notes; his chords clashed, his rhythms stumbled, and his melodies leapt in unexpected directions. Many musicians, however, recognized that Monk's dissonances were deliberate and carefully placed. <u>Musicians who learned his compositions often reported that the hardest part was resisting the urge to "correct" them.</u> Today, Monk is regarded as one of the most original composers in jazz, and his pieces are standard repertoire for musicians around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an example of the criticism that early reviewers directed at Monk's playing, which the rest of the text goes on to dispute.","It explains why Monk's compositions became standard repertoire for jazz musicians.","It suggests that musicians who worked with Monk found his music impossible to learn.","It supports the claim that Monk's unusual choices were intentional by describing musicians' experiences with his music."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text claims musicians recognized Monk's dissonances as deliberate; the underlined sentence supports this by noting that musicians learning his pieces had to resist "correcting" them—implying the oddities were intentional.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In many traditional societies, elders have been the primary source of practical knowledge: how to find water in a drought, which plants are safe to eat, how to predict a storm. Anthropologist Yusuf Adeyemi argues that rapid environmental change may be eroding this authority. When climate patterns shift faster than they did in the past, he notes, knowledge accumulated over a lifetime may become unreliable—rains no longer arrive when they once did, and familiar plants fail. In several communities Adeyemi studied, young people increasingly turned to weather apps and agricultural extension agents rather than to their elders for advice.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that weather apps are more reliable than traditional knowledge for predicting rainfall","To describe the kinds of practical knowledge that elders in traditional societies possess and explain why that knowledge is always reliable","To present an anthropologist's argument about how environmental change may be affecting the role of elders in traditional societies","To explain why young people in traditional societies are moving to cities"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that elders traditionally held practical knowledge, then presents Adeyemi's argument that rapid environmental change may be undermining their authority.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The giant panda's diet presents a puzzle. Pandas eat almost nothing but bamboo, consuming up to 12 kilograms a day. Yet their digestive systems are those of a carnivore: short, simple guts with none of the specialized chambers that help cows and other grazers break down tough plant material. Pandas digest only about 17 percent of the bamboo they eat. <u>They compensate by eating constantly—up to fourteen hours a day—and by moving as little as possible to conserve energy.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that pandas are evolving a more efficient digestive system.","It explains how pandas manage to survive despite the difficulty described in the preceding sentences.","It describes a behavior that makes pandas vulnerable to predators.","It introduces a new puzzle about the panda's diet—why pandas eat bamboo at all—that the rest of the text does not resolve."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The preceding sentences describe a problem (a carnivore's gut that digests little bamboo); the underlined sentence explains how pandas cope (eating constantly, conserving energy).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Ruth has returned to the farmhouse where she grew up, which has been empty for several years.<br><br>The kitchen was smaller than she remembered, as kitchens from childhood always are. The table was still there, pushed against the wall, its oilcloth faded from red to the color of dried roses. She ran her hand along its edge and found the notch her brother had carved the summer he was nine, the summer he had been punished for it, the summer before everything changed. She stood for a long time with her finger in the groove, as if it might tell her something she had forgotten.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To depict a character's encounter with a familiar place that stirs memories of the past","To explain why a character's family decided to leave their farmhouse","To describe a conflict between a character and her brother over the family's property","To show that a character is disappointed by how poorly the farmhouse has been maintained"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ruth returns to the farmhouse, notices familiar objects, and lingers over the notch her brother carved, recalling the past—the text focuses on memory triggered by place.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Mira Patel set out to understand why some neighborhoods recover quickly from disasters while others, with similar resources, struggle for years. She expected that income would be the strongest predictor of recovery. Instead, after studying communities affected by floods in three countries, she found that the strongest predictor was what she called "social infrastructure": the density of relationships among neighbors, built through shared spaces such as parks, libraries, and places of worship. Neighborhoods with strong social ties shared information, pooled resources, and checked on vulnerable residents, often well before official help arrived.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a researcher's question and then explains why her research was unable to answer it.","The text presents two competing theories about disaster recovery and argues that both are incorrect.","The text describes how neighborhoods recover from disasters and then recommends specific policies for governments.","The text describes a researcher's question, her initial expectation, and the different finding that her research produced."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text poses Patel's question, states her expectation (income), and then reports her unexpected finding (social infrastructure) and how it worked.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Glass appears to be a solid, but it lacks the ordered internal structure of most solids; its molecules are arranged randomly, like those of a liquid. This fact gave rise to a popular claim that old window panes are thicker at the bottom because glass flows slowly downward over centuries. <u>Physicists have shown, however, that at room temperature glass would take far longer than the age of the universe to flow noticeably.</u> The uneven thickness of old windows is now attributed to the way glass was made at the time: early techniques produced panes of uneven thickness, and glaziers often installed them with the thicker edge at the bottom.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence for the claim that glass is a liquid rather than a solid.","It explains why glass molecules are arranged randomly.","It refutes a popular claim described in the previous sentence.","It describes a technique that early glassmakers used to make panes of uniform thickness."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence shows that glass cannot flow noticeably at room temperature, disproving the claim that old panes thickened at the bottom by flowing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the ruins of Angkor in Cambodia were thought to consist of a cluster of temples surrounded by dense jungle. In 2012, archaeologists used lidar—laser scanning from aircraft that can see through forest canopy—to map the area. The scans revealed a vast network of roads, canals, and residential neighborhoods hidden beneath the trees, showing that the temples had been the center of an enormous urban landscape. The findings have changed estimates of Angkor's population and raised new questions about why such a large city was eventually abandoned.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Angkor was abandoned because its population grew too large to support","To describe how a new technology revealed that an ancient site was larger and more complex than previously believed","To explain how lidar technology works and why it is useful to archaeologists studying sites that are hidden by dense forest canopy","To compare Angkor with other ancient cities discovered using lidar"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the earlier view of Angkor and how lidar revealed a much larger urban landscape, changing understanding of the site.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1960s, psychologist Walter Mischel offered young children a choice: one marshmallow now, or two if they could wait fifteen minutes. Follow-up studies found that children who waited tended to have better outcomes years later, and the experiment became famous as evidence that self-control predicts success. <u>A larger study in 2018, however, found that once children's family background and early cognitive abilities were taken into account, the ability to wait predicted much less.</u> Some researchers now suggest that waiting may reflect a child's circumstances—such as whether promises from adults are usually kept—as much as an inborn trait.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a finding that complicates the earlier interpretation of the experiment.","It provides additional evidence that a child's ability to wait strongly predicts later success, regardless of family background.","It describes the original design of the marshmallow experiment.","It explains why the original experiment became famous."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence reports that the predictive power of waiting shrinks after accounting for background, challenging the earlier interpretation; the next sentence offers an alternative explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sea ice in the Arctic reflects most of the sunlight that strikes it back into space. Open ocean water, by contrast, is dark and absorbs most of the sunlight that reaches it. As the climate warms and sea ice melts, more open water is exposed, which absorbs more heat, which in turn melts more ice. Climate scientists call this a positive feedback loop, and it is one reason the Arctic is warming roughly four times faster than the global average.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It argues that efforts to reduce global warming should focus primarily on the Arctic.","It compares the effects of sea ice and open ocean on global weather patterns.","It describes how scientists measure the amount of sunlight that sea ice reflects and compare it with the amount absorbed by open water.","It explains a mechanism that helps account for the unusually rapid warming of a particular region."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text explains the ice-albedo feedback loop and connects it to the Arctic's rapid warming.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Rice farmers in parts of Bangladesh have begun raising ducks in their flooded paddies. The ducks eat insects and weeds that would otherwise damage the rice, reducing the need for pesticides and herbicides. Their droppings fertilize the fields. And as they paddle through the water, they stir up the soil, which helps oxygen reach the rice plants' roots. The farmers, meanwhile, gain a second source of income from selling ducks and eggs. <u>Not every farm can adopt the system, however; it requires fields that stay flooded for much of the growing season and access to markets for the ducks.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an additional benefit of raising ducks in rice paddies.","It explains why the ducks eat insects and weeds rather than rice plants.","It qualifies the benefits described earlier by noting conditions that limit the system's use.","It argues that the duck-rice system, despite its benefits, is less profitable than conventional rice farming in most of Bangladesh."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After listing many benefits, the underlined sentence notes limitations—the system requires certain conditions—qualifying the earlier positive account.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short. In fact, he was about 1.69 meters tall, roughly average for a Frenchman of his time. The myth may have several sources. French and English units of measurement differed, and his height recorded in French inches was misread by English speakers as a smaller figure. British cartoonists, eager to mock an enemy, routinely drew him as a tiny, tantrum-prone figure. And he was often seen surrounded by his Imperial Guard, whose members were selected in part for their height.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Napoleon's height affected his military decisions","To debunk a popular belief and suggest reasons why the belief arose","To compare the systems of measurement used in France and England","To describe the role of political cartoons in British society"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text states the myth, corrects it with Napoleon's actual height, and then explains possible sources of the misconception.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1840s, the Hungarian physician Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women who gave birth in a hospital ward staffed by doctors died of fever far more often than those in a ward staffed by midwives. He observed that the doctors often came directly from performing autopsies. Suspecting that they carried some kind of contamination on their hands, he required them to wash with a chlorine solution. Deaths in the doctors' ward fell dramatically. <u>Yet many of his colleagues rejected his conclusions, offended by the suggestion that physicians themselves were spreading disease.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a reaction to the physician's findings that contrasts with the success those findings had demonstrated.","It presents additional evidence supporting the physician's conclusion that doctors were carrying contamination from autopsies.","It explains how the physician developed the chlorine solution he used.","It suggests that the physician's findings were later shown to be mistaken."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes Semmelweis's successful intervention; the underlined sentence, introduced with "Yet," contrasts this success with his colleagues' rejection.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Beginning in the 1970s, the city of Curitiba in Brazil developed a bus system that, by the 1990s, functioned much like a subway: buses ran in dedicated lanes, passengers paid before boarding at raised tube-shaped stations, and doors opened at platform level, allowing rapid boarding. The system cost a small fraction of what a subway would have cost and carried more passengers per day than many subways. It became a model for "bus rapid transit" systems in cities from Bogotá to Jakarta, many of which could not afford the expense of building underground rail.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that subways are no longer necessary in large cities","To compare the bus rapid transit systems of Curitiba, Bogotá, and Jakarta and determine which carries the most passengers","To explain why passengers prefer buses to subways","To describe an innovative transit system and its influence on other cities"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes Curitiba's bus system and notes that it became a model for other cities.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For much of the nineteenth century, doctors believed that disease was spread by miasma—bad air from decaying matter. The theory was wrong, but it led to some beneficial reforms. Cities built sewers to remove waste and drained swamps to eliminate foul odors, and these measures did reduce the spread of diseases such as cholera and malaria—though for reasons the reformers did not understand. Only later did germ theory explain that the true causes were microorganisms carried in contaminated water and by mosquitoes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that germ theory was developed by scientists in direct response to the failures of the reforms based on miasma theory","To describe how nineteenth-century cities built sewer systems","To show that a mistaken theory nonetheless prompted actions that had beneficial effects","To compare the spread of cholera with the spread of malaria"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that although miasma theory was wrong, it led to sewers and drainage that reduced disease, for reasons later explained by germ theory.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Amara has just arrived in a new city after leaving her hometown.<br><br>Everything was louder here. The trains screamed into the station, the vendors shouted over one another, and even the pigeons seemed to quarrel. Amara stood on the platform with her single suitcase and let the noise wash over her. At home, she had always been told to speak softly. <u>Here, she thought, no one would ever hear her unless she learned to raise her voice.</u> The idea frightened her, and then, to her surprise, it didn't.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the character decided to leave her hometown, where she had always been told to speak softly, for the city.","It reveals a character's realization about how she may have to change, setting up her final shift in feeling.","It describes the specific sounds of the city that the character finds most unpleasant.","It suggests that the character plans to return home as soon as possible."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence shows Amara recognizing that she must adapt (raise her voice); the final sentence shows her fear turning into something else.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economists have long debated whether raising the minimum wage reduces employment. Traditional theory predicts that it should: if labor becomes more expensive, employers will hire fewer workers. In a widely cited study, two economists compared fast-food restaurants in New Jersey, which had just raised its minimum wage, with restaurants in neighboring Pennsylvania, which had not. They found no decline in employment in New Jersey relative to Pennsylvania. The study did not end the debate, but it prompted many economists to reconsider the assumption that higher wages always cost jobs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text introduces a debate, presents a theoretical prediction, and then describes a study whose results challenged that prediction.","The text describes a study and then explains why its methods were flawed.","The text compares economic conditions in two states and recommends policies for both.","The text presents two theories about the minimum wage and then argues that a study proved one of them to be correct in all cases."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text introduces the minimum-wage debate, states the traditional prediction, describes the New Jersey–Pennsylvania study, and notes how it challenged the prediction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most trees in temperate forests drop their leaves in autumn, but the reason is not simply that the leaves are no longer useful. As days shorten and temperatures fall, a tree begins breaking down the green chlorophyll in its leaves and reabsorbing valuable nutrients such as nitrogen. <u>The yellow and orange pigments that were present in the leaves all along, but masked by the chlorophyll, become visible.</u> Finally, the tree seals off each leaf with a layer of cells at the base of its stem, and the leaf falls.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It identifies the main reason that trees drop their leaves in autumn, which is that the leaves' pigments are no longer useful.","It describes a step that occurs after the leaves have fallen from the tree.","It presents an exception to the process that most trees follow in autumn.","It explains a visible change that results from the process described in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence explains why leaves turn yellow and orange: breaking down chlorophyll (described previously) reveals pigments that were masked.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Library of Alexandria is often described as having been destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. Historians now believe its decline was more gradual. Funding from the Ptolemaic rulers dwindled; scholars were expelled during political purges in the second century BCE; a fire set during Julius Caesar's siege in 48 BCE may have damaged part of the collection; and later conflicts and neglect further diminished it. By the time the last remnants disappeared, the library had long since ceased to be the great center of learning it once was.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Julius Caesar's siege of Alexandria in 48 BCE was primarily responsible for destroying the library's collection","To describe the kinds of texts that were kept in the Library of Alexandria","To challenge a popular account of an event by describing a more complex historical process","To explain why the Ptolemaic rulers founded the Library of Alexandria"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts the popular story of a single fire with historians' view of a gradual decline caused by multiple factors.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The violin maker Yuki Tanabe spent three years trying to reproduce the varnish used by Italian craftsmen in the eighteenth century. Chemical analysis of old instruments had identified its main ingredients, but every batch she made looked dull and lifeless. The breakthrough came when she read an old letter describing how varnished instruments were left to dry for months in direct sunlight. <u>When Tanabe did the same, the varnish developed the deep, glowing color she had been seeking.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why chemical analysis failed to identify the varnish's main ingredients.","It describes the result of applying an idea from the letter mentioned in the previous sentence.","It reveals that the violin maker eventually abandoned her effort to reproduce the varnish.","It introduces a second problem that the violin maker encountered after her breakthrough."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The previous sentence describes the letter's detail about drying instruments in sunlight; the underlined sentence describes the successful result when Tanabe tried this.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Seed banks store frozen seeds to protect crop diversity, but some important plants, such as cacao, coconut, and avocado, produce seeds that cannot survive freezing or drying. For these species, scientists must use other methods. Some maintain living collections in field gene banks—orchards of hundreds of varieties planted side by side—though these are vulnerable to disease and storms. Others are experimenting with cryopreservation, freezing tiny pieces of plant tissue in liquid nitrogen and later regrowing whole plants from them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why some plants cannot be preserved by conventional seed banks and to describe alternative approaches","To argue that field gene banks are more effective than cryopreservation for preserving seeds that cannot survive freezing","To describe how conventional seed banks freeze and store seeds","To explain why cacao, coconut, and avocado are important crops"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explains that some seeds can't survive freezing and describes two alternative methods (field gene banks and cryopreservation).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The city's first public library opened in 1903 with 4,000 books. Its founder, a retired schoolteacher, insisted that it be free and open to everyone, a radical idea in a town where most institutions were segregated by race and class. <u>Within a year, more than a third of the town's adult residents held library cards.</u> The library's success inspired the creation of reading rooms in three surrounding villages and, later, a mobile library that brought books to farms by horse-drawn wagon.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the founder insisted that the library be free and open to everyone.","It describes the kinds of books the library held when it first opened.","It suggests that many of the town's residents opposed the founder's decision to make the library free and open to everyone.","It provides evidence of the library's early popularity, which helps explain the developments described afterward."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence shows the library's quick success, which the following sentence says inspired further reading rooms and a mobile library.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, nutrition guidelines recommended that people avoid eating eggs because of the cholesterol in their yolks. The advice rested on the reasonable assumption that eating cholesterol would raise the level of cholesterol in the blood. Later research, however, found that for most people, dietary cholesterol has only a modest effect on blood cholesterol; the liver adjusts its own production to compensate. In 2015, U.S. dietary guidelines dropped the specific limit on cholesterol intake, though they continued to recommend eating it in moderation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that eggs should be part of every healthy diet","To explain how the liver produces cholesterol","To describe how a dietary recommendation changed as research challenged the assumption behind it","To criticize nutrition researchers for making recommendations about eggs that were based on assumptions rather than evidence"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes the original recommendation, its underlying assumption, later research that challenged it, and the resulting change in guidelines.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Linguists once assumed that the languages of small, isolated communities would be simpler than those of large societies, since fewer speakers would have less need for complex grammar. The evidence suggests otherwise. <u>Some of the most grammatically intricate languages known—with dozens of verb forms marking subtle distinctions of time, certainty, and source of information—are spoken by communities of only a few hundred people.</u> Indeed, some researchers now propose the reverse: that languages spoken by many adult learners tend to become simpler over time, as irregular forms that are hard to learn gradually disappear.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an assumption that the rest of the text supports with examples from several languages.","It provides evidence against an assumption described earlier, preparing for an alternative proposal.","It explains why the languages of small communities tend to lose irregular verb forms over time.","It describes a method linguists use to measure how complex the grammar of a language is."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence offers counterevidence to the assumption that small-community languages are simpler; the final sentence then presents an alternative hypothesis.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, rumors spread that it was unstable, and a stampede on its walkway killed twelve people. To reassure the public, the circus showman P. T. Barnum led a parade of twenty-one elephants across the bridge in 1884. The bridge held, and confidence in it grew. The episode illustrates a persistent challenge for engineers: a structure's safety depends not only on its design but also, in practical terms, on whether people trust it enough to use it as intended.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To use a historical episode to illustrate a broader point about the relationship between public trust and engineering","To argue that P. T. Barnum's parade was the main reason the Brooklyn Bridge became a successful part of New York's transportation system","To describe the design features that made the Brooklyn Bridge strong enough to support the weight of twenty-one elephants","To explain why a stampede occurred on the Brooklyn Bridge shortly after it opened and how it could have been prevented"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the Barnum episode and then draws a general lesson: a structure's practical safety depends on public trust as well as design.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Hamid, a retired schoolteacher, is walking through the market where he once shopped with his late wife.<br><br>The fruit seller still called out the same prices in the same singsong voice, as if no time had passed at all. Hamid bought three oranges, though he would eat only one. Leila had always bought three—one for each of them and one, she said, for luck. <u>He did not believe in luck, but he found that he could not bring himself to buy only two.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It shows that a character has come to believe in luck since the death of someone close to him.","It explains why a character, who once shopped at the market with his late wife, no longer enjoys visiting it.","It suggests that a character buys extra oranges because the fruit seller has raised his prices.","It reveals that a character continues a habit associated with someone he has lost, despite not sharing her belief."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hamid buys three oranges as Leila did, though he doesn't believe in luck—the habit honors her memory, not a belief.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor but support roughly a quarter of all marine species. This extraordinary diversity depends on a partnership between corals and microscopic algae that live inside their tissues, supplying the corals with food through photosynthesis. When water becomes too warm, the partnership breaks down: the corals expel the algae and turn white, a process called bleaching. <u>If temperatures fall quickly enough, the algae can return and the coral may survive; if not, the coral starves.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the main reason that coral reefs support so many species of marine life.","It introduces a method scientists have developed to prevent coral bleaching in warming water.","It explains the possible outcomes of the process described in the previous sentence.","It provides evidence that coral bleaching occurs only in waters that are unusually polluted."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The previous sentence describes bleaching; the underlined sentence explains what can happen afterward depending on temperature.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Philosopher Iris Holloway argues that we misunderstand boredom when we treat it merely as an unpleasant feeling to be avoided. Boredom, she contends, is a signal: it tells us that our current activity fails to engage our abilities or interests, much as hunger signals a need for food. Seen this way, the constant availability of distraction—phones, streaming video, endless feeds—may do us harm, not because entertainment is bad in itself but because it silences a signal that might otherwise prompt us to seek more meaningful activities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that people should give up their phones and streaming video in order to avoid boredom","To present a philosopher's view that a common feeling serves a useful purpose that modern distractions may undermine","To compare the feeling of boredom with the feeling of hunger and to explain how the body produces each of these feelings","To describe the results of studies showing that people who are often bored tend to be less successful"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text presents Holloway's argument that boredom is a useful signal and that constant distraction may silence it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Romans built roads that lasted for centuries, many of them still visible today. Their durability came from careful layering. Builders first dug a trench and filled it with large stones; on top came layers of smaller stones and gravel bound with lime mortar; finally, the surface was paved with tightly fitted blocks. <u>The roads were also slightly curved, higher in the center than at the edges, so that rainwater would drain away rather than pool and seep into the foundation.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes an additional design feature that contributed to the roads' durability.","It explains why Roman roads were built in straight lines across the landscape.","It presents a weakness in Roman road construction that caused many roads to fail.","It introduces a technique that the Romans borrowed from earlier civilizations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After describing the layered construction, the underlined sentence adds another feature (the curved surface for drainage) that helped the roads last.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1977, two spacecraft named Voyager were launched to study the outer planets. Each carried a gold-plated record containing sounds and images from Earth—greetings in fifty-five languages, music from many cultures, the sound of rain and of a human heartbeat—in case the craft were ever found by intelligent beings. The chance of such a discovery is extremely remote; space is vast, and the records will take tens of thousands of years to approach another star. The records, some have argued, may matter less as messages to others than as a statement of how humanity chose to represent itself.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes an object and then argues that it should be replaced with a more effective way of communicating with other beings.","The text describes the mission of two spacecraft and then explains how the spacecraft's discoveries changed scientists' understanding of the planets.","The text describes the contents of a record and then explains why scientists chose each of the sounds and images that it contains.","The text describes an object, notes how unlikely it is to serve its stated purpose, and then suggests a different way to understand its significance."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the Golden Records, explains that discovery by aliens is very unlikely, and then offers another interpretation of their significance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians studying the diaries of nineteenth-century women have often focused on the entries that describe dramatic events: births, deaths, journeys, and wars. Historian Clara Whitfield argues that the most revealing entries are often the most routine. Notes about the price of flour, the weather, or which neighbor came to help with the washing, she contends, reveal the economic pressures and networks of mutual aid that shaped women's daily lives—subjects the writers rarely discussed directly because they took them for granted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that nineteenth-century women's diaries are more reliable than other sources from the same period","To describe the dramatic events that nineteenth-century women most often wrote about in their diaries","To present a historian's argument that overlooked material in a type of source can reveal important information","To explain why nineteenth-century women rarely discussed economic pressures directly in their diaries"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts historians' usual focus with Whitfield's argument that routine entries reveal important aspects of women's lives.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Cheetahs are the fastest land animals, capable of reaching speeds above 100 kilometers per hour. Yet speed alone does not explain their success as hunters. <u>A study that fitted wild cheetahs with tracking collars found that their hunts rarely involved top speed; instead, the cats relied on rapid acceleration and the ability to change direction sharply.</u> Their long tails, which act as counterweights during turns, and their semi-retractable claws, which grip the ground like cleats, may matter as much as their raw speed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that cheetahs are the fastest land animals in the world.","It presents evidence supporting the claim that speed alone does not account for the cheetah's hunting success.","It explains why cheetahs are less successful hunters than other large cats.","It describes a weakness of tracking collars as a method for studying how cheetahs hunt, which led researchers to doubt the cats' speed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text claims speed alone doesn't explain cheetahs' success; the underlined sentence supports this with collar data showing acceleration and turning mattered more.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from an essay about learning to cook.<br><br>My grandmother never measured anything. When I asked how much salt to add, she would say "enough," and when I asked how long to knead the dough, she would say "until it's ready." For years I found this maddening. I wanted rules, numbers, a recipe I could follow. <u>It took me a long time to understand that she was not withholding the recipe from me; she was teaching me to pay attention.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It marks a shift from the author's past frustration to a later understanding of what her grandmother was doing.","It reveals that the author's grandmother refused to share her recipes because she wanted to keep them secret.","It explains why the author eventually stopped cooking with her grandmother and began following written recipes.","It suggests that the author still finds her grandmother's way of cooking frustrating and difficult to learn."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence moves from the author's earlier frustration to her later realization that the grandmother was teaching attentiveness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, the standard treatment for a sprained ankle was rest, ice, compression, and elevation. Recent research has questioned parts of this approach. Some studies suggest that applying ice may slow healing by reducing the inflammation that brings repair cells to the injury. Others have found that patients who begin gentle movement within a few days recover faster than those who rest completely. Many sports-medicine specialists now recommend a modified approach, though they disagree about how much ice, if any, is beneficial.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that applying ice to a sprained ankle is always harmful and should be avoided completely","To explain how inflammation brings repair cells to an injury and why this process is necessary","To compare the recovery times of patients who rest a sprained ankle completely with those of patients who treat it with ice","To describe how research has led to changes in a standard medical recommendation, though some questions remain unresolved"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the traditional treatment, research challenging it, and the resulting modified approach, noting continuing disagreement.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1930s, the U.S. government hired writers to collect the life stories of people who had been enslaved. The resulting interviews, more than 2,300 in all, are an invaluable record. <u>Historians caution, however, that the circumstances of the interviews shaped what was said.</u> Most interviewers were white, many interviewees were elderly and poor, and in the segregated South, speaking frankly to a white stranger about the cruelty of slavery could be dangerous. Some accounts may therefore present a gentler picture of slavery than the speakers privately held.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the main reason historians consider the interviews an invaluable record.","It presents a criticism of the government program that collected the interviews, which the rest of the text then refutes.","It introduces a qualification about a historical source that the rest of the text explains.","It explains why the government decided to collect interviews in the 1930s."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After calling the interviews invaluable, the underlined sentence introduces a caution; the following sentences explain the circumstances that may have shaped the accounts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Beavers are often called ecosystem engineers. By building dams, they convert streams into ponds and wetlands, which slow the flow of water, trap sediment, and create habitat for fish, amphibians, and birds. During droughts, beaver ponds can keep water on the landscape when surrounding areas are dry; during wildfires, they can form green refuges where animals shelter. Some land managers in the western United States have begun reintroducing beavers or building artificial "beaver dam analogs" to restore degraded streams.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that beavers should be reintroduced into every degraded stream in the western United States to prevent wildfires","To describe the ecological effects of an animal's behavior and how people are making use of them","To explain how beavers choose the locations where they build their dams","To compare the habitats created by beavers with those created by other animals"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the benefits of beaver dams and notes that land managers are reintroducing beavers or imitating their dams.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The first maps of the ocean floor, made in the 1950s by geologist Marie Tharp, revealed a deep valley running along the center of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Tharp argued that the valley was a rift where the seafloor was being pulled apart—evidence for the then-controversial idea that continents drift. Her supervisor initially dismissed this interpretation as "girl talk." <u>Within a decade, however, accumulating evidence had made seafloor spreading a cornerstone of the theory of plate tectonics.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It shows that an interpretation that was initially dismissed came to be widely accepted.","It explains how the Mid-Atlantic Ridge was formed by the drifting of the continents.","It describes the methods Tharp used to create the first maps of the ocean floor.","It suggests that Tharp's supervisor eventually took credit for her interpretation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence, beginning with "however," shows that the dismissed interpretation became central to plate tectonics.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economists distinguish between two kinds of unemployment. Cyclical unemployment rises and falls with the economy: during a recession, businesses lay off workers, and when the economy recovers, many are rehired. Structural unemployment is different. It occurs when workers' skills no longer match the jobs available—when, for instance, factories close permanently because of automation. Structural unemployment may persist even when the economy is growing, since the jobs that return are often different from those that were lost.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a problem and then proposes several government policies that could help to solve it.","The text presents a theory about unemployment and then describes evidence that has challenged the theory.","The text describes the causes of recessions and then explains how businesses respond to economic downturns.","The text introduces a distinction and then explains each of the two categories, emphasizing how they differ."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text introduces the distinction, explains cyclical unemployment, then explains structural unemployment and how it differs.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor Ruth Asawa is known for her hanging wire sculptures, intricate forms that look like nested lanterns or seed pods. She learned the looping technique she used from craftspeople in Mexico who made wire baskets for carrying eggs. <u>Asawa rejected the idea that craft was inferior to fine art, treating a humble basket-making technique as a legitimate basis for serious sculpture.</u> Her sculptures, once dismissed by some critics as decorative, now hang in major museums around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the artist traveled to Mexico to learn how to make wire baskets.","It describes the criticism that some critics directed at the artist's sculptures.","It conveys the artist's view of a distinction, a view that her work's later reception seems to support.","It suggests that the artist preferred making traditional egg baskets to making the wire sculptures that now hang in museums."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence gives Asawa's view that craft is not inferior to fine art; the final sentence notes that her once-"decorative" work now hangs in major museums, which supports her view.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains. Brain-imaging studies show otherwise: over the course of a day, virtually every region of the brain is active, and even simple tasks engage multiple areas at once. The brain is also costly to run, consuming about twenty percent of the body's energy despite making up only about two percent of its weight. An organ that expensive, neuroscientists point out, would be unlikely to evolve if most of it went unused.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain how brain-imaging studies measure which areas of the brain are active","To refute a popular belief by presenting evidence and an argument against it","To argue that people could improve their intelligence by using more of their brains","To compare the energy needs of the brain with those of the body's other organs"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text states the ten-percent myth and refutes it with imaging evidence and an evolutionary argument about the brain's energy cost.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1920s, the botanist Nikolai Vavilov traveled across five continents collecting seeds of crop plants and their wild relatives. He noticed that the diversity of many crops was concentrated in a few regions—the mountains of Ethiopia, the Andes, the Fertile Crescent—and proposed that these were the places where the crops had first been domesticated. <u>His seed collection, stored in what is now St. Petersburg, became one of the first seed banks in the world.</u> During the siege of the city in World War II, scientists guarding the collection starved rather than eat the seeds.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It introduces the significance of the collection, which the next sentence illustrates with a dramatic episode.","It presents the main evidence supporting Vavilov's theory that crops were first domesticated in a few regions of the world.","It explains why the diversity of many crops is concentrated in mountainous regions.","It describes a problem that prevented Vavilov from completing his collection of seeds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence identifies the importance of the seed collection; the final sentence describes the scientists' sacrifice to protect it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Musicologist Teresa Lindqvist argues that the familiar distinction between "folk" and "classical" music obscures how often the two have borrowed from each other. Many celebrated classical composers built works on folk melodies, she notes, while folk musicians adopted instruments, forms, and harmonies from concert music. Treating the categories as separate, Lindqvist contends, has led scholars to overlook this exchange and to undervalue the musicians—often anonymous—who sustained the folk traditions that classical composers drew upon.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that classical music is more sophisticated than folk music because it uses more complex harmonies and forms","To describe how folk musicians adopted instruments and forms from the composers of classical music","To explain why the musicians who sustained folk traditions have usually remained anonymous","To present a scholar's argument that a common distinction hides an important relationship and has had negative consequences"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents Lindqvist's argument that the folk/classical distinction obscures mutual borrowing and has led scholars to undervalue folk musicians.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When a hummingbird drinks nectar, its tongue was long thought to work like a straw, drawing liquid upward through capillary action. High-speed video has revealed a different mechanism. <u>As the tongue enters the nectar, its two thin tips spread apart and then curl inward, trapping the liquid and carrying it into the bird's mouth.</u> Because this process is faster than capillary action, it allows the bird to drink up to twenty times per second.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why scientists originally believed the tongue worked like a straw.","It presents evidence that hummingbirds drink more nectar than other birds do.","It describes the mechanism revealed by the method mentioned in the previous sentence.","It introduces a question about hummingbird feeding that the text leaves unanswered."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The previous sentence says high-speed video revealed a new mechanism; the underlined sentence describes it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Maya is waiting for the results of an audition for a youth orchestra.<br><br>Maya told herself that it didn't matter. There would be other auditions, other orchestras, other years. She told herself this on the bus home and again at dinner, and again when she lay awake at midnight listening to the refrigerator hum. By morning she had told herself so many times that it no longer sounded like a sentence at all, only a sound, like the refrigerator, that went on and on without meaning anything.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show that a character has accepted that she will not be chosen for the orchestra","To show how a character's attempts to reassure herself fail to convince her","To describe a character's careful preparations for an important audition","To show that a character regrets having auditioned for the youth orchestra"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Maya repeats a reassurance until it becomes meaningless noise, revealing that her attempts to convince herself aren't working.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some scientists have proposed injecting tiny reflective particles into the upper atmosphere to cool the planet by reflecting sunlight, imitating the cooling that follows large volcanic eruptions. Supporters argue that the method could be inexpensive and fast-acting. Critics raise several concerns: the particles would have to be replenished continually, since they settle out within a few years; regional rainfall patterns could be disrupted; and if the injections ever stopped suddenly, temperatures could rebound rapidly. Many researchers agree that more study is needed before any such project is attempted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a proposal, presents arguments made for and against it, and notes a widely shared view about what should happen next.","The text describes a natural phenomenon and then explains how scientists first discovered its causes.","The text presents a proposal, describes its supporters' arguments, and then argues that it should be adopted quickly to prevent harm.","The text compares two methods of cooling the planet and concludes that one is clearly superior to the other."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the geoengineering proposal, gives supporters' and critics' arguments, and ends with the consensus that more study is needed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, streetlights have been replaced with energy-efficient LEDs that emit more blue light than older bulbs. <u>Ecologists have raised concerns about this change.</u> Blue light is especially disruptive to nocturnal animals: it can draw insects away from their habitats, confuse migrating birds, and disorient sea turtle hatchlings, which use the brightness of the horizon over the ocean to find the water. Some cities have responded by installing warmer-colored LEDs that save energy while emitting less blue light.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the benefits of LEDs that were described in the previous sentence.","It explains why cities decided to replace older streetlights with LEDs.","It presents a conclusion that the text later shows to be mistaken.","It introduces a problem that the following sentences describe in detail."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence introduces ecologists' concerns, which the next sentence explains (effects of blue light on animals).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The term "serendipity" is often used to describe accidental discoveries, such as penicillin or the microwave oven. Sociologist Dev Raman argues that this framing is misleading. In nearly every celebrated case he studied, the "accident" was noticed only because the discoverer had deep expertise that allowed them to recognize its significance; countless others had likely encountered the same phenomenon and ignored it. Serendipity, Raman concludes, is less a matter of luck than of prepared minds encountering chance events.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe how penicillin and the microwave oven were discovered by scientists who were working on other problems","To argue that most important scientific discoveries have been the result of luck rather than careful research","To present a sociologist's argument that a common understanding of accidental discovery underestimates the role of expertise","To explain why many people who encounter unusual phenomena ignore them, while experts tend to investigate them more thoroughly"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents Raman's argument that serendipitous discoveries depend on the expertise that lets discoverers recognize chance events.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Western scholars long assumed that the tradition of Indian classical dance had been transmitted unchanged for two thousand years, preserved in ancient treatises. Dance historian Anjali Rao argues that the forms performed today were substantially reconstructed in the twentieth century. <u>After colonial authorities discouraged temple dance in the late 1800s, many traditions nearly disappeared, and the dancers and scholars who revived them drew on surviving practitioners, ancient texts, and their own creative choices.</u> The result, Rao emphasizes, is no less authentic for being partly modern; all living traditions change.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that the dance tradition was preserved unchanged by colonial authorities.","It provides the historical background supporting the historian's claim that the dance forms were reconstructed.","It explains why Western scholars believed that Indian classical dance had been transmitted unchanged for two thousand years.","It suggests that the revived forms are less authentic than the ancient forms described in treatises."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rao claims the forms were reconstructed; the underlined sentence explains how near-disappearance and revival led to that reconstruction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most medicines are tested on adults, and doses for children are often estimated by scaling down adult doses according to body weight. Pharmacologist Lena Kowalski points out that this approach can be dangerous. Children's livers and kidneys, which process drugs, mature at different rates; a newborn may clear a drug from the body far more slowly than an adult, while a toddler may clear it faster. As a result, a dose adjusted for weight may be too high for an infant and too low for a young child.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why a common method of determining children's medicine doses can be unreliable","To argue that medicines should never be given to children until they have been tested on adults","To describe how the liver and kidneys process drugs in adults and in children","To compare the effectiveness of different medicines used to treat children and adults"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the weight-based dosing method and explains, through Kowalski, why it can be unreliable given children's developing organs.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The Silk Road is often imagined as a single highway along which merchants carried silk from China to Rome. In reality, it was a shifting network of routes, and few traders traveled its full length. <u>Goods typically passed through many hands, each merchant carrying them a short distance before selling them to the next.</u> Along with silk, spices, and glass, the network carried ideas, religions, technologies, and diseases—including, historians believe, the plague that devastated Europe in the fourteenth century.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why silk was the most valuable good traded along the Silk Road.","It provides evidence that merchants on the Silk Road spread disease deliberately.","It describes the route that most merchants followed when they carried silk, spices, and glass from China all the way to Rome.","It elaborates on the claim that the Silk Road was not the single route of popular imagination."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text says few traders traveled the full length; the underlined sentence elaborates by describing how goods were passed along in short stages.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Emmy Noether proved in 1915 that every symmetry in the laws of physics corresponds to a quantity that is conserved. Because the laws of physics are the same at all times, for example, energy is conserved; because they are the same in all places, momentum is conserved. The theorem, now known as Noether's theorem, gave physicists a powerful tool: by identifying the symmetries of a system, they could predict which quantities would remain constant. Physicists still regard it as one of the most important results in theoretical physics.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that energy and momentum are the most important quantities in physics","To explain why the laws of physics are the same at all times and in all places, and how this makes energy and momentum constant","To describe a mathematical result, illustrate it with examples, and explain why it was useful","To compare the work of Emmy Noether with that of Albert Einstein"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text states Noether's theorem, gives examples (energy, momentum), and explains its usefulness to physicists.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Desert plants have evolved many strategies for surviving drought. Cacti store water in thick stems and have replaced leaves with spines, reducing water loss. Some desert annuals avoid drought altogether: their seeds lie dormant for years and sprout only after heavy rain, completing their entire life cycle in a few weeks. <u>The creosote bush takes yet another approach, growing roots that spread widely just below the surface to capture even light rainfall before it evaporates.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an exception to the strategies described in the previous sentences.","It provides a third example of the general claim made in the first sentence.","It explains why creosote bushes grow larger than most other desert plants.","It describes the strategy that the text identifies as the most effective."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first sentence claims desert plants have many strategies; the text gives cacti, annuals, and then the creosote bush as a third example.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the fossil record from that period is so sparse, paleontologist Ines Okafor cautions that any reconstruction of the animal's behavior must remain ______: new specimens could easily overturn the current picture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["provisional","definitive","arbitrary","conventional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Okafor warns that new specimens could overturn the reconstruction, so it must be considered temporary and open to revision—"provisional." "Definitive" is the opposite, "arbitrary" suggests it lacks any basis, and "conventional" doesn't relate to the possibility of revision.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics who expected the director's new film to be as ______ as her earlier work—which featured sprawling casts and elaborate, interlocking plotlines—were surprised by its stark simplicity: two characters, one room, and almost no dialogue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["austere","derivative","lighthearted","intricate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The earlier work is described as having "sprawling casts and elaborate, interlocking plotlines," which contrasts with the new film's "stark simplicity." "Intricate" captures that complexity. "Austere" describes the new film, not the earlier work.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the senator's public statements on the trade agreement were ______, carefully avoiding any clear endorsement or rejection, her private letters reveal that she strongly opposed it from the beginning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["vehement","candid","equivocal","inflammatory"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The statements "carefully avoid[ed] any clear endorsement or rejection," which is the meaning of "equivocal." "Vehement" and "inflammatory" suggest strong expression, and "candid" suggests openness, all of which contradict the description.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Early accounts described the explorer as fearless, but his journals suggest a more ______ figure: he wrote repeatedly of his doubts, revised his routes to avoid danger, and once turned back within sight of his goal because a storm seemed likely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reckless","circumspect","eminent","gregarious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The journals reveal caution—doubting, avoiding danger, turning back. "Circumspect" means cautious and wary. "Reckless" is the opposite, and "eminent" and "gregarious" are unrelated to caution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The connection between the two historical events is ______ at best; the only evidence linking them is a single letter whose authenticity has been questioned by several scholars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tenuous","incontrovertible","deliberate","ubiquitous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The connection rests on one questionable letter, so it is weak—"tenuous." "Incontrovertible" means impossible to dispute, the opposite. "Deliberate" and "ubiquitous" do not describe the strength of a connection.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novelist's prose is famously ______; she rarely uses two words where one will do, and some of her chapters are only a few sentences long.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ornate","obscure","sentimental","economical"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Using as few words as possible is economy of language—"economical." "Ornate" means elaborately decorated, the opposite. "Obscure" and "sentimental" don't relate to brevity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the new species of frog had been found only in a single valley, the biologists were reluctant to call it ______ to that region until they had surveyed the surrounding mountains, where similar habitat exists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indigenous","hostile","endemic","incidental"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The biologists hesitate to say the frog is found only in that region until they have searched nearby. "Endemic" means native to and restricted to a particular area. "Indigenous" means native but does not imply restriction, so it doesn't capture the biologists' caution about whether the frog occurs elsewhere.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The company's apology was widely criticized as ______: it was issued only after a week of public outrage, consisted of two vague sentences, and made no commitment to change.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effusive","perfunctory","unprecedented","conciliatory"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A brief, vague apology issued reluctantly and without commitment is one performed minimally, as a mere formality—"perfunctory." "Effusive" (overflowing) contradicts "two vague sentences." "Conciliatory" suggests a genuine effort to make peace, which critics did not see.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' initial results seemed to show that the drug improved memory, but when the study was repeated with a larger sample, the effect disappeared, suggesting that the original finding had been ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["spurious","robust","predictable","proprietary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An effect that disappears with a larger sample was likely not real—it was "spurious." "Robust" means strong and reliable, the opposite. "Predictable" and "proprietary" do not fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the rival theory outright, the physicist acknowledged that it explained several observations her own model could not and proposed that the two might be combined.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["embracing","corroborating","inventing","dismissing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Rather than" sets up a contrast with the physicist's open-minded response. She did not reject the rival theory, so "dismissing" is correct. "Embracing" and "corroborating" are closer to what she actually did, so they don't contrast with it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's plan has been called ______ by supporters and opponents alike: both sides agree that it would be impossible to fund with the city's current revenue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["visionary","contentious","impracticable","frugal"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both sides agree the plan cannot be funded, which means it cannot be carried out—"impracticable." "Contentious" means causing disagreement, but the sentence says both sides agree. "Visionary" and "frugal" don't match the funding problem.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The archive's catalog is so ______ that researchers often spend days locating a single document: items are listed under inconsistent headings, and many entries contain errors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["exhaustive","haphazard","concise","pristine"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Inconsistent headings and errors indicate a lack of order—"haphazard." "Exhaustive" means thorough, and "pristine" means in perfect condition; neither fits. "Concise" doesn't explain the difficulty.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the artist's paintings from the 1960s were dismissed at the time as ______—mere imitations of styles then fashionable in New York—critics today see them as anticipating trends that emerged only decades later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["derivative","visionary","eccentric","monumental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dash defines the blank: "mere imitations of styles then fashionable." "Derivative" means imitative and unoriginal. "Visionary" reflects today's view, not the view at the time.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The witness's account was remarkably ______: she recalled not only the color of the car but the dent in its rear bumper and the first three characters of its license plate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguous","fanciful","terse","meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Recalling small, precise details shows careful attention—"meticulous." "Ambiguous" and "fanciful" suggest unclear or imagined details, and "terse" means brief.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Scientists have long debated whether the decline in the bird population is ______ to the new pesticide or to habitat loss that began around the same time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["susceptible","indifferent","attributable","comparable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The debate concerns the cause of the decline. "Attributable to" means caused by. "Susceptible to" means vulnerable to, which would describe the birds, not the decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The theory was once ______ among economists, but a series of financial crises exposed its weaknesses, and today few defend it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","ascendant","controversial","nascent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The contrast ("but... today few defend it") indicates that the theory was once dominant—"ascendant." "Obscure" and "nascent" would not contrast with its current lack of support, and "controversial" doesn't set up the decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiator's tone was ______ throughout the talks; even when the other side made demands she considered unreasonable, she responded calmly and suggested possible compromises.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["conciliatory","belligerent","sardonic","indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Responding calmly and proposing compromises reflects a desire to make peace—"conciliatory." "Belligerent" and "sardonic" are hostile or mocking, and "indifferent" suggests she didn't care, which contradicts suggesting compromises.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The poet's late work is ______ to many readers: densely allusive, full of references to obscure medieval texts, and written in a syntax so compressed that individual lines can be read several ways.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accessible","trite","melodious","impenetrable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The description—densely allusive, obscure, compressed—suggests that the work is very hard to understand: "impenetrable." "Accessible" is the opposite. "Trite" and "melodious" don't relate to difficulty.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The sudden drop in the lake's water level did not ______ the decline of the fishing industry so much as accelerate a process that had begun decades earlier with overfishing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["halt","reverse","initiate","conceal"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contrasts starting the decline with speeding up an existing process. "Initiate" (start) is what the drop did not do. "Halt" and "reverse" don't contrast with "accelerate" in the right way.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Rahul Menon's findings are ______ with earlier studies: although his team used different methods and studied a different population of whales, they reached nearly identical conclusions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incompatible","consistent","contemporaneous","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reaching nearly identical conclusions means the findings agree with earlier studies—"consistent." "Incompatible" is the opposite. "Contemporaneous" refers to time, and "redundant" implies the study was unnecessary, which the use of different methods argues against.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The tribe's oral histories had long been ______ by historians who considered them unreliable, but recent archaeological finds have confirmed several events the stories describe.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["discounted","celebrated","transcribed","fabricated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The historians considered the stories unreliable, so they gave them little weight—"discounted." The contrast with recent confirmation supports this. "Fabricated" means made up, which would refer to the people who told the stories, not the historians.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The microscope's resolution is ______ by the wavelength of visible light; no matter how well its lenses are made, it cannot distinguish objects smaller than about 200 nanometers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["enhanced","determined arbitrarily","obscured","constrained"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The wavelength of light sets a limit that cannot be overcome, so resolution is "constrained" (limited). "Enhanced" is the opposite, and "obscured" suggests hidden rather than limited.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's memoir is unusually ______ about her own mistakes: she devotes an entire chapter to a negotiation she believes she mishandled, sparing herself no criticism.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["evasive","nostalgic","forthright","grandiose"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Openly discussing one's own mistakes without sparing oneself is "forthright" (direct and honest). "Evasive" is the opposite, and "nostalgic" and "grandiose" don't relate to self-criticism.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$To prevent the virus from spreading, health officials imposed ______ restrictions on travel to and from the affected region, allowing only medical personnel and emergency supplies to pass.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["nominal","stringent","voluntary","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Allowing only medical personnel and supplies indicates very strict rules—"stringent." "Nominal" means slight, and "voluntary" contradicts the imposed nature. "Temporary" doesn't describe how strict the restrictions were.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's hypothesis was bold, but her colleagues found her evidence ______: the data were drawn from only three sites, and the patterns she identified could easily have arisen by chance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconclusive","compelling","fraudulent","abundant"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The limited data and possibility of chance patterns mean the evidence does not settle the question—"inconclusive." "Compelling" and "abundant" are the opposite, and "fraudulent" suggests dishonesty, which the text does not imply.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many of the composer's contemporaries regarded his use of dissonance as ______, a deliberate violation of the rules of harmony meant to shock audiences rather than to serve any musical purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["integral","subtle","traditional","gratuitous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contemporaries thought the dissonance served no musical purpose and was used only to shock—"gratuitous" (unnecessary, unjustified). "Integral" means essential, the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the two languages share many words, their grammars are strikingly ______; one places verbs at the end of sentences, while the other places them at the beginning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["analogous","rudimentary","divergent","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The grammars differ in a fundamental way, so they are "divergent." "Analogous" means similar, the opposite. "Rudimentary" and "archaic" don't describe difference.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The engineer insisted that the cracks in the bridge's supports were ______ and posed no threat to its stability, but the city ordered a full inspection anyway.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["structural","superficial","extensive","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The engineer claimed the cracks posed no threat, meaning they were only on the surface—"superficial." "Structural" and "extensive" would suggest a threat.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The bird's song is ______ in its variety: a single male may produce more than two thousand distinct phrases, rarely repeating any of them during a morning's performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prodigious","monotonous","rudimentary","derivative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two thousand distinct phrases represents an enormous range—"prodigious" (remarkably great). "Monotonous" is the opposite, and "rudimentary" and "derivative" don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as a recluse, the inventor was in fact quite ______, hosting weekly dinners for scientists and artists and maintaining a vast correspondence with friends abroad.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reticent","eccentric","prolific","sociable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hosting dinners and corresponding with friends contradicts the image of a recluse, showing she enjoyed company—"sociable." "Reticent" fits the recluse description, not the contrast. "Prolific" means productive, which isn't the point.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the treaty's language was ______, each nation interpreted it in its own favor, and disputes over its meaning continued for decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["explicit","innocuous","ambiguous","eloquent"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Language open to different interpretations is "ambiguous." "Explicit" is the opposite. "Innocuous" and "eloquent" don't explain the interpretive disputes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's decision to display the forged paintings alongside the originals was intended to be ______: visitors could compare them side by side and learn to recognize the forger's telltale techniques.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["deceptive","instructive","frivolous","punitive"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The purpose was to teach visitors to recognize forgeries—"instructive." "Deceptive" is the opposite of the museum's intention, and "frivolous" and "punitive" don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new evidence does not ______ the theory entirely, but it does suggest that the theory's original formulation was too simple to account for the full range of observations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["invalidate","support","predict","clarify"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence says the evidence complicates but doesn't entirely disprove the theory. "Invalidate" (show to be false) is what the evidence does not do. "Support" wouldn't fit the contrast with "but... too simple."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In contrast to the ______ predictions of many analysts, who expected the company to collapse within a year, the small firm not only survived but doubled its revenue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["optimistic","tentative","accurate","dire"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The analysts expected collapse, so their predictions were extremely negative—"dire." "Optimistic" is the opposite. "Accurate" contradicts the outcome.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scholar argues that the folktale's many versions, though they differ in their details, share a ______ structure: a journey, a test, and a return home transformed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["chaotic","superficial","common","unique"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Versions that differ in details but share the same elements have a shared, or "common," structure. "Unique" would mean each had a different structure.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The council's response to the flooding was criticized as ______; officials waited nearly a week before sending aid to the hardest-hit neighborhoods.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precipitous","dilatory","draconian","meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Waiting nearly a week reflects slowness—"dilatory" (tending to delay). "Precipitous" means overly hasty, the opposite. "Draconian" (harsh) and "meticulous" (careful) don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's portrayal of the scientist is largely ______; it focuses on her achievements and glosses over the controversies that marked her career.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flattering","balanced","damning","dispassionate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Focusing on achievements and ignoring controversies presents the scientist in a favorable light—"flattering." "Balanced" and "dispassionate" are contradicted by the glossing over, and "damning" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' range has ______ dramatically in recent decades; once found across much of the continent, it now survives only in a few isolated mountain valleys.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expanded","fluctuated","stabilized","contracted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Going from much of a continent to a few valleys means the range has shrunk—"contracted." "Expanded" is the opposite, and "fluctuated" or "stabilized" don't describe a steady decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's tone in the essay is ______: she describes the politicians' promises in such exaggerated, admiring terms that readers understand she means the opposite.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["earnest","nostalgic","ironic","objective"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Saying something in a way that conveys the opposite meaning is irony. "Earnest" (sincere) is the opposite, and "nostalgic" and "objective" don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript survives only in fragments, scholars must ______ much of its content from quotations in later works by other authors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["translate","reconstruct","dismiss","publish"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Without the full manuscript, scholars must piece together its content from other sources—"reconstruct." "Translate" and "publish" assume the content is available, and "dismiss" doesn't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The proposed law has drawn ______ support: environmental groups, business associations, and labor unions—organizations that rarely agree on anything—have all endorsed it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["broad","tepid","covert","partisan"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Support from many very different groups is wide-ranging—"broad." "Partisan" means from one side only, the opposite. "Tepid" (lukewarm) and "covert" (hidden) don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the treatment helped some patients, the researchers stressed that its benefits were ______ and that it should not replace standard therapies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dramatic","permanent","unanimous","modest"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The researchers caution that the treatment shouldn't replace standard therapies, suggesting its benefits are limited—"modest." "Dramatic" would argue for wider use.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's interpretation of the sonata was ______: rather than follow the tempos indicated in the score, she slowed some passages almost to a halt and rushed through others.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["faithful","conventional","idiosyncratic","hesitant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ignoring the score's tempos in a personal, unusual way is "idiosyncratic." "Faithful" and "conventional" are contradicted by her departures from the score.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The historian argues that the king's reputation for cruelty is largely ______, the product of propaganda spread by the rival dynasty that overthrew him.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accurate","undeserved","inherited","irrelevant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the reputation comes from enemy propaganda, it isn't justified by fact—"undeserved." "Accurate" contradicts the claim of propaganda.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two scientists' accounts of the discovery are ______: each claims to have been the first to notice the anomaly, and each describes the other as merely assisting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["irreconcilable","complementary","identical","corroborative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The accounts make conflicting claims that can't both be true—"irreconcilable." "Complementary" and "corroborative" suggest they fit together or support each other, and "identical" contradicts the conflicting claims.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her study of nineteenth-century letters, historian Ana Lucía Ferreira found that many writers used elaborate politeness formulas not out of genuine deference but as a ______ for criticism, softening complaints that would otherwise have seemed rude.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["substitute","deterrent","prerequisite","vehicle"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The politeness formulas carried and delivered criticism in a softened form; they served as a means of conveying it—a "vehicle." A "substitute" would replace criticism, but the formulas accompanied it. A "deterrent" would discourage it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The early drafts of the novel reveal how ______ the author's process was: chapters were rewritten a dozen times, characters were added and deleted, and the ending changed completely at least three times.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effortless","secretive","laborious","linear"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewriting chapters a dozen times and repeatedly changing the ending shows the process required great effort—"laborious." "Effortless" is the opposite, and "linear" contradicts the repeated revisions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the economist's model accurately predicted the recession, some of her colleagues regarded its success as ______, noting that the same model had failed to predict three earlier downturns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inevitable","fortuitous","deserved","systematic"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The colleagues suspect the correct prediction was a matter of luck, since the model had failed before. "Fortuitous" means happening by chance. "Inevitable," "deserved," and "systematic" all imply the success was not due to luck.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because octopuses live only a year or two and rarely interact with their parents, researchers were surprised to find that their problem-solving abilities are so ______; such intelligence is usually associated with long-lived, social animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sophisticated","limited","instinctive","predictable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The researchers' surprise implies that octopuses' problem-solving is advanced despite the traits usually associated with intelligence. "Sophisticated" fits. "Limited" would not be surprising.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The critic praised the novel's dialogue as ______, noting that the characters speak in the halting, overlapping, often ungrammatical way that real people do.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stilted","poetic","formal","naturalistic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Dialogue that mimics how real people speak is "naturalistic." "Stilted" and "formal" are the opposite, and "poetic" suggests artful rather than realistic.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Despite its reputation as a ______ genre, interested only in cheap thrills, the gothic novel often explored serious questions about power, inheritance, and the constraints placed on women.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["rigorous","obscure","frivolous","venerable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast ("Despite... the gothic novel often explored serious questions") signals that the genre was thought to be unserious—"frivolous," which matches "interested only in cheap thrills."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers did not claim that the drug cured the disease; their claim was more ______: that it slowed the disease's progression in some patients.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sweeping","modest","dubious","novel"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Saying the drug slowed progression in some patients is a smaller, more limited claim than saying it cured the disease—"modest." "Sweeping" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The village's isolation, once a hardship, has become an unexpected ______: its pristine landscapes and traditional way of life now attract thousands of visitors each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["asset","burden","anomaly","constraint"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The isolation now brings benefits (attracting visitors), so it has become something valuable—an "asset." "Burden" and "constraint" describe it as a hardship, which the sentence says it no longer is.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The witness's testimony was ______ by phone records showing that she had called the police at exactly the time she described.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["contradicted","fabricated","dismissed","corroborated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Phone records matching her account confirm it—"corroborated." "Contradicted" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The playwright's characters are often ______ figures, neither heroes nor villains, whose motives remain unclear even to themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["archetypal","sympathetic","ambiguous","flamboyant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Characters who are neither heroes nor villains and whose motives are unclear are "ambiguous." "Archetypal" suggests typical, clear-cut models, which contradicts the description.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Researchers had assumed that the cave's paintings were created over a short period, but radiocarbon dating revealed a surprisingly ______ history of use, with some images added more than ten thousand years after the first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["abbreviated","protracted","violent","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Images added over ten thousand years indicates a long, extended history—"protracted." "Abbreviated" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The candidate's speech was ______ with references to local history, mentioning the town's founding, its mills, and the flood of 1936 in a way that delighted the hometown crowd.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["replete","devoid","fraught","tinged"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The speech was full of references—"replete." "Devoid" means lacking. "Fraught" suggests danger or tension, and "tinged" suggests only a slight trace.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's earlier work had been dismissed as ______, but her theory gained acceptance once new instruments allowed others to observe the phenomena she had predicted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["empirical","conclusive","plagiarized","speculative"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Her earlier work was dismissed because it lacked observational confirmation; it was based on conjecture—"speculative." "Empirical" and "conclusive" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the controversy, the museum's statement inflamed it, as many readers interpreted the carefully worded apology as an admission of wrongdoing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prolonging","documenting","defusing","provoking"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Rather than" contrasts with "inflamed"; the museum hoped to calm the controversy—"defusing." "Prolonging" and "provoking" are similar to inflaming.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species is remarkably ______: it thrives in deserts, grasslands, and even cities, eating everything from insects to discarded food.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragile","adaptable","solitary","specialized"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Thriving in many habitats and eating many foods shows flexibility—"adaptable." "Specialized" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The essay's argument is ______; each point builds logically on the one before, leading the reader step by step to a conclusion that seems almost inevitable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cogent","digressive","polemical","derivative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument that builds logically to a convincing conclusion is "cogent" (clear and persuasive). "Digressive" means wandering off topic, the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the painting's colors now appear muted, chemical analysis suggests they were once quite ______, before centuries of exposure to light faded the pigments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subdued","uniform","translucent","vivid"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with "now appear muted" and "faded" indicates the colors were originally bright—"vivid." "Subdued" describes their current state.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiations stalled because both sides remained ______: neither would accept any proposal that required it to give up even a small portion of its original demands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accommodating","ambivalent","intransigent","apathetic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Refusing to give up any part of their demands is "intransigent" (unwilling to compromise). "Accommodating" is the opposite. "Ambivalent" and "apathetic" suggest uncertainty or lack of interest.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's memoir offers a ______ account of her childhood, mixing fond memories of summers by the lake with painful recollections of her parents' difficult marriage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["idealized","nuanced","perfunctory","fictitious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mixing positive and painful memories shows a balanced, complex view—"nuanced." "Idealized" would omit the painful parts. "Perfunctory" means minimal effort.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The initial reports of the earthquake's damage were ______; officials later discovered that hundreds more buildings had collapsed than first estimated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["understated","exaggerated","precise","sensational"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If more damage was later found, the initial reports made it seem less severe than it was—"understated." "Exaggerated" and "sensational" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some critics view the director's reliance on silence and long, static shots as ______, but admirers argue that these techniques allow viewers to notice details that faster-paced films would obscure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["innovative","hectic","commercial","tedious"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"But" signals that the critics' view contrasts with the admirers' praise. Critics therefore find the techniques boring—"tedious." "Innovative" would be praise, not criticism.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The surgeon's hands were ______ even after twelve hours in the operating room; colleagues marveled that she could still perform delicate sutures without the slightest tremor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["weary","nimble","steady","callused"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Performing delicate work without tremor shows her hands were "steady." "Weary" contradicts the point, and "nimble" focuses on quickness rather than lack of tremor.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the professor's lectures were famous for their wit, his published writing was surprisingly ______, consisting mostly of dry technical reports.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["whimsical","pedestrian","provocative","eloquent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Surprisingly" signals contrast with "wit"; dry technical reports are dull and ordinary—"pedestrian." "Whimsical" and "eloquent" would match the wit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two species look almost identical, but their songs are ______, which is how ornithologists first realized that they were not the same bird.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["distinct","similar","faint","complex"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The songs revealed that the birds were different species, so the songs must differ clearly—"distinct." "Similar" would not have helped distinguish them.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The company's claims that its product was environmentally friendly proved ______; investigators found that its factories released pollutants well above legal limits.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["modest","verifiable","candid","specious"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Claims that look plausible but turn out false are "specious." "Verifiable" and "candid" suggest the claims were true, and "modest" doesn't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the senator's speech made no ______ mention of the controversial bill, reporters could only guess at her position from her general remarks about government spending.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["oblique","lengthy","explicit","hostile"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Reporters had to guess her position because she never directly mentioned the bill—"explicit." An "oblique" mention would be indirect, which matches what reporters had to work with.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The archaeologists were careful not to ______ the burial site's contents, photographing each object in place before removing anything.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["catalog","disturb","preserve","excavate"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Being careful and photographing objects in place shows they wanted to avoid changing their arrangement—"disturb." "Catalog" and "preserve" are what they wanted to do.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the ancient city was once a ______ center of trade, attracting merchants from three continents, by the tenth century it had dwindled to a small farming village.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["thriving","modest","remote","fledgling"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast with "dwindled to a small farming village" shows the city was once very successful—"thriving." "Modest" and "fledgling" don't contrast with decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's report was notable for its ______; she acknowledged every limitation of her data and avoided drawing any conclusion the evidence did not clearly support.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambition","brevity","originality","rigor"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Acknowledging limitations and avoiding unsupported conclusions shows strictness and care—"rigor."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many readers found the novel's ending ______, since the mystery at the center of the plot is never solved and several major characters simply disappear from the story.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["predictable","triumphant","unsatisfying","sentimental"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An unresolved mystery and disappearing characters would leave readers without closure—"unsatisfying." "Predictable" contradicts the unresolved ending.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The governor's decision to veto the bill was ______: just a week earlier, she had publicly promised to sign it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inevitable","unexpected","unanimous","routine"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Vetoing a bill after promising to sign it would surprise people—"unexpected." "Inevitable" and "routine" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' conclusion that the fish can recognize individual human faces may seem ______, but their experiments were carefully designed to rule out other explanations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["far-fetched","self-evident","uncontroversial","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"May seem... but" signals that the conclusion appears implausible even though the experiments support it. "Far-fetched" fits. "Self-evident" and "uncontroversial" would not require the defense that follows.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The musician's early recordings, made on cheap equipment in her bedroom, have a ______ quality that many fans prefer to the polished sound of her later studio albums.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refined","synthetic","monotonous","raw"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with "polished" and the cheap equipment suggest an unrefined sound—"raw." "Refined" describes the later albums.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist was ______ about the results of her experiment, refusing to discuss them publicly until they had been reviewed by other researchers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effusive","careless","guarded","indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Refusing to discuss results publicly reflects caution—"guarded." "Effusive" is the opposite, and "careless" and "indifferent" contradict her deliberate restraint.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The river's course is ______, changing every few years as floods carve new channels through the soft sediment of the plain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fixed","mutable","navigable","subterranean"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A course that changes every few years is changeable—"mutable." "Fixed" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The art historian's claim that the painting is by Vermeer rests on ______ evidence: no document links it to the artist, and its attribution depends entirely on similarities in style.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["circumstantial","documentary","irrefutable","anecdotal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Evidence based on stylistic similarity rather than direct proof is indirect—"circumstantial." "Documentary" is explicitly ruled out, and "irrefutable" is too strong. "Anecdotal" refers to personal stories.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Far from being ______, the island's ecosystem is extraordinarily fragile; the introduction of a single invasive species could disrupt the entire food web.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diverse","isolated","ancient","resilient"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Far from being" sets up the opposite of "extraordinarily fragile": able to withstand disturbance—"resilient."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The essayist's style is ______; she moves from personal anecdote to scientific explanation to literary analysis within a single paragraph, often without transitions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","methodical","eclectic","restrained"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Moving among many different modes reflects a varied, wide-ranging style—"eclectic." "Monotonous" and "methodical" suggest uniformity or order.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The proposal was criticized as ______ because it would address only the symptoms of the city's housing shortage, not its underlying causes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["radical","superficial","comprehensive","costly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Addressing only symptoms, not causes, is shallow—"superficial." "Comprehensive" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The violin's sound is so ______ that it can be heard clearly over a full orchestra, even in the farthest rows of a large concert hall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resonant","muted","shrill","delicate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being heard clearly over an orchestra in a large hall indicates a full, carrying sound—"resonant." "Muted" and "delicate" are the opposite, and "shrill" has a negative connotation not supported by the context.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The general's reputation for ______ was well earned: in thirty years of service, she never made a decision without first consulting her officers and weighing every alternative.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impulsiveness","cruelty","eloquence","deliberation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Consulting others and weighing every alternative is careful consideration—"deliberation." "Impulsiveness" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The theory remains ______ among physicists: some regard it as the most promising path toward a unified theory, while others dismiss it as untestable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","settled","divisive","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Physicists hold sharply opposing views, so the theory causes disagreement—"divisive." "Settled" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The committee's report did not reject the proposal outright; instead, it ______ its support, endorsing the plan only on the condition that an independent review of its costs be completed first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["withdrew","qualified","concealed","exaggerated"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Endorsing a plan "only on the condition" that something happen limits that support—"qualified" it. "Withdrew" would mean removing support entirely, which the text says did not happen.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her account of the expedition, the botanist is ______ about her own role, crediting her guides and assistants for nearly every discovery and mentioning her own contributions only in passing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-effacing","self-serving","self-assured","self-evident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Crediting others and barely mentioning her own contributions shows modesty—"self-effacing." "Self-serving" is the opposite, "self-assured" means confident, and "self-evident" means obvious.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two historians agree on the facts of the rebellion but differ sharply in their ______ of them: one sees a spontaneous uprising, the other a carefully planned revolt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["verification","recollection","presentation","interpretation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$They agree on the facts but disagree about what the facts mean (spontaneous or planned)—their "interpretation." "Verification" would concern whether the facts are true, on which they agree.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the study's sample was small, its findings were ______ enough—they held across every age group and in each of the four cities studied—to persuade the agency to fund a larger trial.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["surprising","preliminary","consistent","controversial"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The dashes explain the blank: the findings held across every group and city, meaning they were "consistent." "Surprising" and "controversial" don't match the explanation, and "preliminary" wouldn't explain why they were persuasive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's portrayal of the town is affectionate but not ______; she depicts its residents' warmth and generosity alongside their narrow-mindedness and suspicion of outsiders.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sincere","uncritical","detailed","humorous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The portrayal includes both virtues and flaws, so it is affectionate without ignoring problems—not "uncritical." Saying it is not "sincere," "detailed," or "humorous" would not fit the description of a balanced portrayal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Scholars have long ______ the origins of the manuscript, which bears no date, no author's name, and no clear indication of where it was written.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disputed","documented","celebrated","fabricated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With no date, author, or location, the origins are uncertain and therefore subject to disagreement—"disputed." "Documented" contradicts the lack of information.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new regulations are intended to ______ the risk of contamination rather than eliminate it entirely, since some exposure to bacteria in food processing is unavoidable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["eradicate","assess","disregard","mitigate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The regulations reduce rather than eliminate the risk—"mitigate" means lessen. "Eradicate" means eliminate, which the sentence contrasts with.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the poem appears at first to be a simple description of a winter landscape, its imagery of silence and stillness ______ a deeper meditation on grief.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismisses","precedes","conveys","interrupts"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The imagery carries or expresses the deeper meaning—"conveys." "Dismisses" and "interrupts" suggest opposition, and "precedes" suggests the meditation comes later rather than within the imagery.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The young composer's first symphony was ______ by older critics, who praised its technical skill but dismissed its emotional range as limited.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["lauded unreservedly","damned with faint praise","ignored entirely","denounced vehemently"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Praising technical skill while dismissing emotional range is a mix of mild praise and criticism—"damned with faint praise." "Lauded unreservedly" and "denounced vehemently" are too extreme, and "ignored entirely" contradicts the critics' comments.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' decline was so ______ that, for decades, no one noticed it; only when researchers compared population counts from the 1970s with recent surveys did the scale of the loss become clear.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["gradual","precipitous","erratic","localized"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A decline that went unnoticed for decades must have happened slowly—"gradual." "Precipitous" (steep and sudden) would likely have been noticed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ her critics directly, the scientist responded by publishing a detailed reanalysis of her data, letting the evidence speak for itself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acknowledge","cite","ignore","rebut"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast is between answering critics directly and responding indirectly with evidence. "Rebut" (argue against) captures the direct response she avoided. "Ignore" doesn't fit, because she did respond.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's remarks were deliberately ______, allowing both governments to claim that the agreement favored their own position.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["candid","conciliatory","ambiguous","provocative"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Remarks that each side could interpret in its own favor were open to more than one meaning—"ambiguous." "Conciliatory" is close in spirit but doesn't explain why both sides could claim victory.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have praised the biography for its ______, noting that the author draws on thousands of letters, interviews with nearly everyone who knew the subject, and archives in four countries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["brevity","thoroughness","objectivity","lyricism"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Drawing on extensive sources demonstrates comprehensiveness—"thoroughness." "Objectivity" concerns bias, which the list of sources doesn't address.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the medication's side effects are ______—appearing only after several months of use—they were not detected in the initial short-term trials.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["delayed","severe","transient","unpredictable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dashes explain that side effects appear only after months, so they are "delayed." "Transient" means short-lived, which is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many readers mistake the narrator's calm, measured voice for indifference, but a closer reading reveals the ______ grief beneath her restraint.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["feigned","fleeting","trivial","profound"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A closer reading reveals strong feeling under her restraint, contrasting with apparent indifference—"profound" grief. "Feigned" means fake, and "fleeting" and "trivial" undercut the contrast.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The city's architecture ______ its history of conquest: Roman columns, Moorish arches, and Gothic spires stand side by side in its oldest neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscures","contradicts","reflects","predates"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The styles from different conquering cultures show the history of conquest—the architecture "reflects" it. "Obscures" and "contradicts" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researcher's methods were ______: she replicated each experiment at least five times and published all of her raw data so that others could check her analysis.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unorthodox","scrupulous","expedient","covert"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Replicating experiments and publishing raw data reflects careful, honest practice—"scrupulous." "Expedient" suggests convenience over principle, and "covert" means hidden, contradicting publication.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The playwright's dialogue is so ______ that a single line often carries two or three meanings, rewarding audiences who see the play more than once.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["layered","sparse","colloquial","repetitive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lines with multiple meanings are "layered." "Sparse" refers to quantity, "colloquial" to informality, and "repetitive" to repetition, none of which explains multiple meanings.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The flooding was not the cause of the town's economic decline but rather a ______ event, hastening the closure of businesses that were already struggling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["mitigating","concurrent","negligible","precipitating"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The flood hastened closures that were already likely, so it triggered or sped up the outcome—"precipitating." "Mitigating" means lessening, the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the inventor was celebrated in her lifetime, her reputation ______ after her death, and by the 1950s her name was rarely mentioned even in histories of her field.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flourished","stabilized","waned","endured"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Her name being rarely mentioned shows her reputation declined—"waned." "Flourished" and "endured" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The critic described the singer's voice as ______, capable of shifting in a single phrase from a whisper to a full-throated cry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","versatile","strident","fragile"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Shifting easily from a whisper to a cry shows range and flexibility—"versatile." "Strident" (harsh) and "fragile" don't describe that range.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The report's authors were careful to ______ their conclusions, noting that the data came from a single region and might not apply elsewhere.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["hedge","overstate","publicize","abandon"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Noting limitations to avoid overcommitting to conclusions is "hedging." "Overstate" is the opposite, and "abandon" goes too far.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The general's memoir has been criticized as ______: it presents every battle he fought as a triumph and blames his subordinates for every setback.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-deprecating","self-contained","self-critical","self-aggrandizing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Presenting himself as always triumphant and blaming others exaggerates his own importance—"self-aggrandizing." "Self-deprecating" and "self-critical" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The study's findings, though intriguing, are ______; until other laboratories reproduce them, most scientists will treat them with caution.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unfounded","unprecedented","unconfirmed","unremarkable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Results not yet reproduced by others are "unconfirmed." "Unfounded" means without basis, which is too strong—the findings are described as intriguing. "Unremarkable" contradicts "intriguing."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The landscape painter was ______ in her choice of subjects, returning again and again to the same stretch of coastline over a career of more than forty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["capricious","steadfast","indiscriminate","reluctant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Returning to the same subject for forty years shows loyalty and persistence—"steadfast." "Capricious" (unpredictable) and "indiscriminate" (unselective) are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the new tax was intended to reduce smoking, critics argue that its effects have been ______: it falls hardest on low-income smokers, who spend a larger share of their income on cigarettes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["regressive","negligible","progressive","beneficial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A tax that takes a larger share of income from lower-income people is "regressive." "Progressive" means the opposite, and "negligible" doesn't match the hardship described.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novel's narrator is ______: she claims to have been at home on the night of the crime, but later chapters reveal that she was seen across town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["omniscient","sympathetic","detached","unreliable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A narrator whose account is contradicted by later evidence cannot be trusted—"unreliable." "Omniscient" means all-knowing, which doesn't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the scientist's theory was ______ when she first proposed it, it is now taught in introductory biology courses as established fact.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["canonical","rudimentary","heterodox","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast with "now taught... as established fact" implies the theory once went against accepted views—"heterodox." "Canonical" means accepted, which describes its current status.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiators reached only a ______ agreement, settling the dispute over fishing rights but leaving the more contentious question of the maritime border for future talks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unanimous","partial","binding","secret"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Settling one issue while leaving another unresolved is an incomplete agreement—"partial."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Her argument is ______ on a single assumption: that consumers always act in their own financial interest. If that assumption is wrong, the entire argument collapses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["predicated","silent","lenient","fixated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument that depends on an assumption is "predicated" on it. "Fixated" suggests obsession, and "silent" and "lenient" don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's ______ remarks about the budget—brief and noncommittal—did little to reassure residents worried about cuts to library hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inflammatory","exhaustive","impassioned","cursory"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Brief and noncommittal" describes hasty, superficial remarks—"cursory." "Exhaustive" and "impassioned" contradict "brief" and "noncommittal."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some biologists argue that the trait is ______, a remnant of an earlier stage of evolution that no longer serves any purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["adaptive","acquired","vestigial","dominant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A remnant that no longer serves a purpose is "vestigial." "Adaptive" means useful for survival, the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the poet's handwriting was nearly ______, editors disagreed about dozens of words in her final manuscript and published several competing versions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["elegant","illegible","uniform","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Disagreement about many words indicates the handwriting was very hard to read—"illegible."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The government's response to the crisis was ______: it announced new policies almost daily, only to reverse many of them within the week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["erratic","decisive","sluggish","measured"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Announcing and then reversing policies constantly shows inconsistency—"erratic." "Decisive" and "measured" are contradicted by the reversals, and "sluggish" contradicts the daily announcements.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's claim was ______ by later research: experiments in three countries confirmed every major prediction her model had made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refuted","anticipated","obscured","vindicated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Later confirmation of her predictions proved her right—her claim was "vindicated." "Refuted" is the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The architect's design is deceptively simple; its ______ facade conceals an interior of complex, interlocking spaces.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ostentatious","crumbling","unassuming","intricate"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The facade looks simple, which contrasts with the complex interior—"unassuming." "Ostentatious" and "intricate" contradict "deceptively simple."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The historian cautions that the diaries, written decades after the events they describe, may be ______; memory, she notes, tends to reshape the past to fit the present.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fabricated","distorted","illegible","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Memory reshaping the past suggests the diaries may misrepresent events unintentionally—"distorted." "Fabricated" implies deliberate invention, which the historian does not suggest.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The young chess player's style was ______ for someone her age: rather than launching risky attacks, she preferred slow, patient maneuvering that gradually wore down her opponents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uncharacteristically cautious","typically aggressive","notably erratic","deliberately reckless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her slow, patient play is unusual for a young player, who would typically take risks. "Uncharacteristically cautious" captures both the style and the contrast with her age.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species was thought to be extinct, so the discovery of a small population in a remote forest was ______ for conservationists, offering a second chance to protect it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["a setback","a formality","an inconvenience","a windfall"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Finding a species thought extinct is an unexpected gift—"a windfall." The other choices are negative or neutral.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The film's critics found its message ______, complaining that it lectured audiences about environmental responsibility rather than letting the story speak for itself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subtle","ambiguous","heavy-handed","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Lecturing audiences instead of letting the story speak suggests an unsubtle approach—"heavy-handed." "Subtle" and "ambiguous" are the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as a pioneer of abstract art, the painter regarded her work as ______ representational: each of her apparently abstract canvases, she insisted, depicted a specific landscape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["superficially","fundamentally","occasionally","reluctantly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$She insisted that each canvas depicted a specific landscape, so she saw her work as representational at its core—"fundamentally." "Superficially" would mean only on the surface, which contradicts her insistence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The council's decision was ______ by a single vote, and opponents immediately announced plans to challenge it in court.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["narrowly approved","unanimously passed","quietly withdrawn","overwhelmingly rejected"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A decision decided "by a single vote" that opponents want to challenge was "narrowly approved." "Unanimously" and "overwhelmingly" contradict "a single vote."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many ecologists have attributed the decline of North American bumblebees primarily to pesticides, particularly neonicotinoids, which can impair bees' ability to forage and navigate. Laboratory studies have repeatedly shown that exposure to these chemicals reduces colony growth.<br><br>Text 2<br>A team led by ecologist Priya Raman analyzed bumblebee records spanning more than a century. They found that species declined most sharply in regions where summer temperatures had risen beyond the bees' tolerance, regardless of local pesticide use. Raman acknowledges that pesticides harm bees but argues that their effects may be secondary to those of a warming climate.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Raman and her team (Text 2) most likely respond to the ecologists' explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the laboratory studies described in Text 1 were poorly designed and that pesticides do not harm bees.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely agree that pesticides are the primary cause of bumblebee decline but argue that warming makes their effects worse.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the ecologists in Text 1 should have studied bee populations over a longer period of time.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the explanation in Text 1 identifies a real factor but may overstate its importance relative to another factor."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Raman "acknowledges that pesticides harm bees" but argues that their effects "may be secondary" to climate. She would therefore accept the factor in Text 1 as real while disputing its primacy.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians argue that the printing press caused the Protestant Reformation. By making it possible to reproduce Martin Luther's writings quickly and cheaply, the press spread his ideas faster than church authorities could suppress them; without it, they contend, the Reformation would likely have been contained, as earlier reform movements were.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mateo Ruiz cautions against treating the printing press as a cause of the Reformation. Earlier reformers, he notes, also had followers who copied and circulated their writings. What distinguished Luther's era was a combination of factors: political rulers who saw advantage in breaking with Rome, widespread resentment of church taxes, and, yes, a new technology that amplified dissent. The press, Ruiz argues, accelerated a movement whose causes lay elsewhere.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ruiz (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ruiz would argue that the printing press played no meaningful role in the spread of Luther's ideas during the Reformation.","Ruiz would agree that earlier reform movements failed mainly because they lacked a technology like the printing press.","Ruiz would agree that the printing press helped spread Luther's ideas but would dispute that it was the cause of the Reformation.","Ruiz would argue that political rulers used the printing press to suppress Luther's writings rather than to spread them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ruiz grants that the press "amplified dissent" and "accelerated" the movement but argues that its causes "lay elsewhere," disagreeing with Text 1's causal claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Urban planner Hannah Cole argues that cities should eliminate minimum parking requirements, which force developers to build a set number of parking spaces with every new building. These rules, she contends, raise the cost of housing, encourage driving, and waste land that could be used for homes or parks.<br><br>Text 2<br>Transportation researcher David Ng agrees that parking requirements often produce more parking than is needed. But he warns that eliminating them abruptly in neighborhoods with poor public transit could push parking onto residential streets, frustrating residents and generating political backlash. He recommends reducing requirements gradually while improving bus and rail service.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Cole (Text 1) and Ng (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that cities should eliminate all parking requirements immediately in every neighborhood.","Both would agree that minimum parking requirements often lead to more parking than is needed.","Both would agree that improving public transit is less important than reducing the cost of housing.","Both would agree that political backlash is the main reason cities have kept parking requirements."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Cole argues the requirements waste land, and Ng "agrees that parking requirements often produce more parking than is needed." They disagree about how quickly to change them.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For decades, the standard view held that the domestication of dogs began when humans deliberately captured and tamed wolf pups. Hunters, according to this view, recognized the value of wolves as hunting partners and guards and selectively bred the tamest animals.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Laura Fenn proposes a different account. Wolves that were less fearful of humans, she suggests, began scavenging near human camps, where food scraps were plentiful. Over generations, these wolves became increasingly tolerant of people—in effect, domesticating themselves—long before humans began deliberately breeding them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts view the origins of dog domestication?$q$, $q$["Text 1 emphasizes deliberate human action in starting domestication, while Text 2 suggests the process began with changes in the wolves' own behavior.","Text 1 argues that domestication happened quickly, while Text 2 argues that it happened over thousands of years through deliberate breeding.","Text 1 argues that wolves were domesticated as guards, while Text 2 argues that they were domesticated mainly as hunting partners.","Text 1 describes evidence from fossils, while Text 2 relies mainly on observations of how modern wolves behave around people."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 describes humans deliberately capturing and breeding wolves, while Fenn suggests that wolves' own scavenging behavior began the process ("domesticating themselves").$q$),
  ($q$cs-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many literary critics regard the novel's ambiguous ending—in which the protagonist's fate is never revealed—as a weakness. The author, they argue, raises questions throughout the book that readers reasonably expect to be answered, and the refusal to resolve them feels like an evasion.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Amara Diallo contends that the ending is the novel's greatest strength. The book, she notes, is about the uncertainty of life in a city undergoing rapid change, where people disappear from one another's lives without explanation. A tidy resolution, Diallo argues, would betray the experience the novel sets out to depict.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Diallo (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Diallo would agree that the ending is a weakness but argue that it is outweighed by the novel's other strengths.","Diallo would argue that the protagonist's fate is in fact revealed if the novel is read carefully enough.","Diallo would agree that readers expect answers but argue that most novels should not provide them.","Diallo would argue that the critics fail to see how the unresolved ending reflects the experience the novel portrays."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Diallo argues that the ambiguity reflects the novel's theme of uncertainty and that a resolution would "betray the experience the novel sets out to depict," directly countering the critics' view that it's a weakness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Advocates of four-day workweeks point to trials in which companies that reduced hours without cutting pay reported stable or even improved productivity. Employees in these trials reported less stress and burnout, and many companies chose to keep the shorter schedule after the trials ended.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Sofia Brandt notes that most companies in such trials volunteered to participate, and that they were disproportionately in office-based industries where output is hard to measure precisely. Before concluding that a four-day week works generally, she argues, researchers need to study companies that did not volunteer, including those in manufacturing, health care, and retail.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brandt (Text 2) most likely respond to the advocates' claims in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Brandt would likely argue that the employees in the trials described in Text 1 overstated how much their stress and burnout had decreased.","Brandt would likely argue that four-day workweeks reduce productivity in office-based industries but not in manufacturing or retail.","Brandt would likely caution that the trials described in Text 1 may not reflect how a four-day week would work across all kinds of businesses.","Brandt would likely agree that the trials prove four-day workweeks are beneficial and urge all companies to adopt them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Brandt points out that participating companies volunteered and were concentrated in certain industries, so the results may not generalize.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Astronomer Elena Ruiz argues that the search for life beyond Earth should focus on planets similar to our own: rocky worlds orbiting at a distance from their stars where liquid water can exist on the surface. Life as we know it requires liquid water, she notes, and searching for familiar conditions is the most efficient use of limited telescope time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Planetary scientist Jonah Webb agrees that liquid water is likely essential for life but argues that the search has been too narrow. Some of the most promising environments in our own solar system, he notes, are not Earth-like at all: Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's moon Enceladus are icy worlds with oceans hidden beneath their frozen surfaces, kept liquid by internal heat rather than by sunlight.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Webb (Text 2) most likely respond to Ruiz's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Webb would argue that liquid water is not necessary for life and that scientists should search for life in completely dry environments.","Webb would agree that liquid water is important but argue that Ruiz's approach could overlook places where water exists under very different conditions.","Webb would agree with Ruiz's approach and argue that Europa and Enceladus are too far away to be studied with telescopes.","Webb would argue that scientists should stop searching for life on rocky, Earth-like planets entirely and focus their limited telescope time only on icy moons with hidden oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Webb "agrees that liquid water is likely essential" but says the search "has been too narrow," pointing to icy moons with subsurface oceans—places Ruiz's Earth-like criteria would miss.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The long-held explanation for the collapse of Classic Maya cities around 900 CE is a prolonged drought. Sediment cores from lakes in the region show sharp declines in rainfall during the period, and many cities were abandoned as the drought intensified.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Rosa Castillo accepts that a severe drought occurred but notes that it affected cities unevenly: some cities in the driest areas survived for centuries, while others in relatively wetter areas collapsed. She argues that the cities' responses to drought—shaped by warfare, political rivalries, and how they managed their water systems—determined which endured.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Castillo (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Castillo would argue that the drought alone cannot explain why some cities collapsed and others survived.","Castillo would argue that the sediment cores in Text 1 provide no reliable evidence about rainfall during the period.","Castillo would argue that the Classic Maya cities collapsed mainly because of warfare, not because of any drought.","Castillo would agree that the drought explains the collapse and add that it was even more severe than the cores suggest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Castillo accepts the drought but notes its uneven effects, arguing that cities' responses, not the drought alone, determined their fate.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Mark Ellison argues that the habit of multitasking—texting while studying, for instance—harms learning. In his experiments, students who switched between tasks remembered less and made more errors than those who focused on one task at a time. The brain, he concludes, cannot truly do two demanding things at once.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Dana Kim agrees that switching between demanding tasks reduces performance. Her research, however, found that some combinations of tasks interfere very little: people can listen to instrumental music while doing math, for example, with no measurable loss. The key, Kim argues, is whether the tasks draw on the same mental resources.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kim (Text 2) most likely respond to Ellison's conclusion in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Kim would argue that Ellison's experiments were flawed because students who multitask perform as well as those who focus.","Kim would argue that listening to music while studying harms learning more than texting while studying does.","Kim would agree with Ellison that people can never perform two activities at once without some loss of performance.","Kim would agree with Ellison's findings about demanding tasks but argue that his conclusion does not apply to every pair of tasks."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Kim agrees that switching between demanding tasks hurts performance but shows some combinations don't interfere, limiting how broadly Ellison's conclusion applies.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Supporters of rewilding—restoring ecosystems by reintroducing species that once lived there—point to the return of wolves to Yellowstone National Park in 1995. The wolves reduced the number of elk, they argue, which allowed willows and aspens along rivers to recover, which in turn stabilized riverbanks and brought back beavers and songbirds.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Tomas Berg considers this story oversimplified. Elk numbers in Yellowstone also fell because of hunting outside the park, drought, and growing populations of bears and cougars. And in many places, he notes, willows have not recovered, because decades of elk browsing and the loss of beavers lowered water tables beyond what wolves alone can reverse.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Berg (Text 2) most likely respond to the supporters' account in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Berg would argue that wolves had no effect on the number of elk in Yellowstone National Park.","Berg would agree with the account in Text 1 and argue that the return of wolves fully explains the recovery of willows, aspens, beavers, and songbirds.","Berg would argue that the account in Text 1 attributes too much of the change to wolves and overlooks other factors.","Berg would argue that the elk population in Yellowstone increased after wolves were reintroduced."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Berg calls the story "oversimplified," citing other causes of elk decline and noting that willows haven't recovered everywhere—so the account gives wolves too much credit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many art historians regard photography's invention in the 1830s as a threat that pushed painters away from realism. Since cameras could now record appearances faithfully, they argue, painters turned to styles—such as Impressionism—that emphasized what cameras could not capture: color, light, and the subjective experience of seeing.<br><br>Text 2<br>Art historian Nadia Okafor argues that photography did not simply push painters away from realism; it also shaped how they saw. Many Impressionists studied photographs closely, she notes, and borrowed features such as blurred motion and unusual cropping, in which figures are cut off by the edge of the frame—effects first seen in snapshots.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okafor (Text 2) most likely respond to the art historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Okafor would argue that photography had little effect on painting during the nineteenth century.","Okafor would argue that the relationship between photography and painting involved influence as well as competition.","Okafor would agree that photography pushed painters away from realism and add that it made painting less popular.","Okafor would argue that Impressionist painters were the first to develop blurred motion and unusual cropping."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okafor argues that photography "did not simply push painters away" but "also shaped how they saw," with Impressionists borrowing its effects—influence alongside the competition described in Text 1.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Rafael Ortiz argues that remote work will permanently reduce demand for office space in large cities. Companies have discovered that many employees can work productively from home, he notes, and they are unlikely to keep paying for offices that sit mostly empty.<br><br>Text 2<br>Real-estate analyst June Park agrees that overall demand for office space has fallen but argues that the decline is uneven. Older buildings with poor ventilation and few amenities are emptying, she notes, while newer buildings with modern features are attracting tenants. Companies, Park contends, are not abandoning offices so much as trading down in quantity and up in quality.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Ortiz (Text 1) and Park (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that overall demand for office space has declined.","Both would agree that remote work has made employees less productive.","Both would agree that newer office buildings are losing tenants as quickly as older ones.","Both would agree that companies will soon return to using as much office space as before."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ortiz predicts reduced demand, and Park "agrees that overall demand for office space has fallen," though she sees the decline as uneven.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some linguists argue that emojis are degrading written communication. Instead of crafting precise sentences to express feelings, they contend, writers increasingly rely on a limited set of small pictures whose meanings are vague and easily misunderstood.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Carmen Ibáñez sees emojis differently. In face-to-face conversation, she notes, people convey tone through facial expressions and gestures; in text messages, those cues are missing, and misunderstandings are common. Emojis, she argues, restore some of this lost information, functioning less as replacements for words than as a kind of written body language.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ibáñez (Text 2) most likely respond to the linguists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ibáñez would agree that emojis are vague and easily misunderstood but argue that writers should use even more of them in order to make their meaning clearer to readers.","Ibáñez would argue that emojis are more precise than words and should replace written sentences in most messages.","Ibáñez would agree that emojis degrade writing but argue that face-to-face conversation is degrading as well.","Ibáñez would argue that the linguists misunderstand the purpose emojis serve, which is to add cues that text otherwise lacks rather than to replace precise writing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ibáñez says emojis function "less as replacements for words than as a kind of written body language," countering the claim that they replace precise sentences.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Nutrition researchers have long recommended that people eat breakfast, citing studies showing that breakfast eaters tend to weigh less than people who skip the meal. Breakfast, the reasoning goes, prevents overeating later in the day.<br><br>Text 2<br>Epidemiologist Kenji Watanabe points out that most such studies were observational: they compared people who already ate breakfast with people who didn't. Breakfast eaters may differ in other ways, such as exercising more or sleeping better. In randomized trials, in which participants were assigned to eat or skip breakfast, Watanabe notes, breakfast had little effect on weight.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Watanabe (Text 2) most likely respond to the reasoning in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Watanabe would argue that skipping breakfast causes people to weigh less than people who eat breakfast.","Watanabe would agree with the recommendation in Text 1 and argue that it is supported by randomized trials.","Watanabe would argue that the evidence cited in Text 1 does not show that breakfast itself causes lower weight.","Watanabe would argue that people who eat breakfast exercise less and sleep worse than people who skip it."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Watanabe notes that observational studies can't separate breakfast from other differences and that randomized trials found little effect—so the cited evidence doesn't establish causation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Architect Paolo Ferri argues that new buildings in historic districts should closely imitate the styles of their older neighbors. Buildings that clash with their surroundings, he contends, disrupt the visual harmony that gives such districts their character and value.<br><br>Text 2<br>Architect Lin Zhao believes that imitation produces lifeless copies. New buildings in historic districts, she argues, should be recognizably contemporary but respectful of their surroundings—matching the height, scale, and materials of nearby buildings without reproducing their decorative details.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Ferri (Text 1) and Zhao (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that new buildings in historic districts should reproduce the decorative details of older buildings.","Both would agree that new buildings in historic districts should be designed with their surroundings in mind.","Both would agree that historic districts should not allow any new buildings to be constructed.","Both would agree that contemporary styles disrupt the character of historic districts."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ferri wants imitation to preserve harmony; Zhao wants buildings to be "respectful of their surroundings" in height, scale, and materials. Both think surroundings should shape the design.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For much of the twentieth century, scientists believed that the human appendix was a useless remnant of our evolutionary past, a structure that had once helped our ancestors digest plants but now served no function.<br><br>Text 2<br>Immunologist Grace Odum argues that the appendix may serve as a "safe house" for beneficial gut bacteria. After an illness such as severe diarrhea flushes the intestines, she proposes, bacteria sheltered in the appendix can recolonize the gut. Supporting this idea, she notes that people without an appendix appear somewhat more likely to experience recurrent intestinal infections.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Odum (Text 2) most likely respond to the view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Odum would argue that the appendix may have a function that the earlier view failed to recognize.","Odum would agree that the appendix is a useless remnant but argue that it should not be removed.","Odum would argue that the appendix once helped human ancestors digest plants more efficiently.","Odum would argue that bacteria in the appendix cause most intestinal infections in humans."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Odum proposes a function (sheltering beneficial bacteria), contradicting the view that the appendix is useless.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many educators argue that students learn best when they discover concepts on their own. Rather than being told how to solve a problem, students who explore and experiment, these educators contend, develop deeper understanding and remember what they learn longer.<br><br>Text 2<br>Cognitive scientist Paul Mercer acknowledges that discovery can be engaging, but he argues that for beginners it is often inefficient. Novices, he notes, lack the background knowledge to know what to look for, so they may struggle for long periods or draw incorrect conclusions. Explicit instruction, followed by opportunities to practice and explore, tends to produce better results for students new to a subject.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mercer (Text 2) most likely respond to the educators' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Mercer would argue that students should never be allowed to explore or experiment on their own.","Mercer would agree that discovery learning produces deeper and longer-lasting understanding for all students, regardless of their background knowledge.","Mercer would argue that experienced students learn less from discovery than beginners do.","Mercer would argue that the approach in Text 1 may be less effective for students who are new to a subject."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mercer says discovery is "often inefficient" for beginners and that explicit instruction works better for novices.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Liam O'Connor argues that the Irish Famine of the 1840s was caused by a natural disaster: a potato blight that destroyed the main food source of millions of people. Once the blight struck, he contends, mass starvation was nearly unavoidable given the limited agricultural technology of the time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Siobhán Murphy agrees that the blight triggered the famine but argues that its devastating scale was not inevitable. During the worst years, she notes, Ireland continued to export large quantities of grain and livestock to Britain, and relief efforts were limited by policies that favored free markets over aid.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Murphy (Text 2) most likely respond to O'Connor's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Murphy would argue that the potato blight played no role in causing the famine.","Murphy would agree that mass starvation was unavoidable once the blight struck.","Murphy would argue that O'Connor overlooks human decisions that worsened the famine.","Murphy would argue that Ireland imported too much grain from Britain during the famine."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Murphy accepts the blight as the trigger but points to exports and policy choices as reasons the famine was so severe, challenging O'Connor's claim that starvation was "nearly unavoidable."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some researchers argue that social media use is a major cause of rising rates of anxiety and depression among teenagers. They point to the fact that these rates began climbing sharply around 2012, the year smartphones became widespread among adolescents.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Ana Ferreira cautions that the timing alone does not establish cause. Many other changes occurred around the same time, she notes, including economic pressures following the 2008 financial crisis and changes in how mental health conditions were diagnosed and reported. Studies that track individual teenagers over time, Ferreira says, have found links between social media use and well-being, but those links are generally small.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ferreira (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ferreira would argue that social media has no effect at all on teenagers' mental health.","Ferreira would argue that the evidence in Text 1 is not sufficient to show that social media is a major cause of the increase.","Ferreira would agree that social media is the main cause of the increase and argue that studies tracking individual teenagers confirm a strong effect.","Ferreira would argue that smartphones became widespread among adolescents well before 2012."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ferreira says timing alone doesn't establish cause, notes other changes, and reports that measured links are small—so the evidence doesn't show social media is a major cause.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Composer and critic Rhea Lindgren argues that recordings have narrowed how classical music is performed. When listeners grow familiar with a few famous recordings, she contends, they come to expect every performance to sound the same, and performers feel pressure to conform rather than to offer fresh interpretations.<br><br>Text 2<br>Musicologist Ivan Petrov notes that recordings have also made a far wider range of interpretations available than ever before. A listener today can compare dozens of versions of the same symphony, including historical recordings that reveal performance styles that have since disappeared. This access, he argues, has inspired many performers to experiment.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Petrov (Text 2) most likely respond to Lindgren's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Petrov would argue that recordings have encouraged variety in performance as well as the conformity Lindgren describes.","Petrov would agree that recordings have led listeners to expect every performance of a symphony to sound like the few most famous recordings of it.","Petrov would argue that listeners today are less familiar with classical music than listeners in the past.","Petrov would argue that historical recordings should replace live performances of classical music."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Petrov stresses that recordings make many interpretations available and have "inspired many performers to experiment," a counterweight to Lindgren's claim of narrowing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many conservationists argue that zoos play a vital role in protecting endangered species. Breeding programs in zoos, they note, have saved several species from extinction, including the California condor and the Arabian oryx, both of which have since been returned to the wild.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Maya Torres agrees that a handful of zoo breeding programs have been remarkable successes. But she points out that the vast majority of animals in zoos belong to species that are not endangered, and that few zoo breeding programs ever release animals into the wild. Zoos, she argues, should devote far more of their resources to the species most at risk.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Torres (Text 2) most likely respond to the conservationists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Torres would argue that the condor and oryx programs failed to return animals to the wild successfully.","Torres would argue that zoos should stop all breeding programs and focus only on public education.","Torres would agree that most animals in zoos belong to endangered species that will eventually be released.","Torres would accept the examples in Text 1 but argue that they are not typical of what most zoos do."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Torres "agrees that a handful of zoo breeding programs have been remarkable successes" but notes that most zoo animals aren't endangered and few programs release animals—so the examples are exceptions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary scholar Ruth Albright argues that the poet's frequent images of birds should be read as symbols of freedom. In poem after poem, birds escape cages, cross borders, and rise above the troubles of the human characters, embodying the liberty the poet's speakers long for.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Hassan Ali reads the same images differently. Many of the poet's birds, he notes, are injured, trapped, or migrating under threat; in one poem, a flock is driven from its marsh by drainage. For Ali, the birds represent not escape from danger but the vulnerability of creatures caught up in forces they cannot control.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Albright and Ali interpret the poet's bird images?$q$, $q$["Albright focuses on the poet's early poems, while Ali focuses on the poet's later poems.","Albright argues that the birds are realistic descriptions, while Ali argues that they are symbols.","Albright sees the bird images as expressing liberation, while Ali sees them as expressing vulnerability.","Albright argues that the poet admired birds, while Ali argues that the poet feared them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Albright reads the birds as symbols of freedom; Ali reads them as images of creatures caught in forces beyond their control.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Proponents of nuclear power argue that it is essential for reducing carbon emissions. Nuclear plants produce large amounts of electricity without burning fossil fuels, and unlike solar and wind power, they generate electricity continuously regardless of the weather.<br><br>Text 2<br>Energy analyst Rosa Lindqvist does not dispute nuclear power's low emissions. But she notes that new nuclear plants in several countries have taken more than a decade to build and have run billions of dollars over budget. In the time and with the money required to build one plant, she argues, a country could install enough wind, solar, and battery storage to cut more emissions sooner.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lindqvist (Text 2) most likely respond to the proponents' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Lindqvist would argue that nuclear power produces more carbon emissions than its proponents admit.","Lindqvist would argue that the advantages described in Text 1 may be outweighed by the cost and time needed to build new plants.","Lindqvist would agree that nuclear power is essential for reducing emissions and argue that countries should build many more plants as quickly as possible.","Lindqvist would argue that wind and solar power generate electricity continuously regardless of the weather."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lindqvist accepts low emissions but argues that slow, costly construction makes renewables a faster, cheaper way to cut emissions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The traditional view of Neanderthals portrays them as brutish and unsophisticated, incapable of the symbolic thought that characterized early modern humans. For decades, the absence of Neanderthal art seemed to support this view.<br><br>Text 2<br>Recent discoveries have challenged that picture. In caves in Spain, researchers have found red pigment designs dated to more than 64,000 years ago—before modern humans are known to have arrived in Europe. Archaeologist Elena Duarte argues that these designs, if their dating holds up, can only have been made by Neanderthals, suggesting a capacity for symbolic thought.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Duarte (Text 2) most likely respond to the traditional view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Duarte would argue that new evidence, if confirmed, undermines a key support for the traditional view.","Duarte would argue that modern humans arrived in Europe much earlier than researchers previously believed.","Duarte would agree that Neanderthals were incapable of symbolic thought but argue that they used pigments.","Duarte would argue that the absence of Neanderthal art proves that they lived only in Spain."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 says the absence of Neanderthal art supported the traditional view; Duarte argues that cave designs, if correctly dated, show Neanderthals made art.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Henry Cho argues that tariffs—taxes on imported goods—protect domestic industries and the jobs they provide. When foreign goods become more expensive, he contends, consumers buy more domestic products, and factories that might otherwise have closed are able to stay open.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Leila Haddad acknowledges that tariffs can save jobs in protected industries. But she notes that they also raise prices for consumers and for businesses that rely on imported materials. A tariff on steel, for example, may save jobs at steel mills while costing jobs at the many companies that use steel to make cars, appliances, and machinery.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to Cho's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Haddad would argue that tariffs never save jobs in the industries they are designed to protect.","Haddad would agree with Cho's argument and add that tariffs lower prices for consumers.","Haddad would argue that a tariff on steel costs more jobs at steel mills than it saves at the companies that use steel to make cars and appliances.","Haddad would accept part of Cho's argument but argue that it overlooks costs that tariffs impose elsewhere in the economy."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Haddad "acknowledges that tariffs can save jobs in protected industries" but points to higher prices and job losses in industries that use imported materials.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some researchers believe that animals such as chimpanzees and dolphins possess a form of culture: behaviors, such as tool use or particular calls, that are learned from others and differ from one group to another. Different chimpanzee communities, for example, use different techniques to crack nuts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Gregor Weiss is skeptical. Group differences in behavior, he argues, may reflect differences in local environments—the types of nuts or stones available—rather than socially learned traditions. Only experiments that rule out environmental explanations, he contends, can establish that animals have culture in a meaningful sense.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Weiss (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Weiss would argue that chimpanzees do not use tools to crack nuts in any of their communities.","Weiss would agree that animals possess culture and argue that dolphins have more complex culture than chimpanzees.","Weiss would argue that the evidence described in Text 1 may have an explanation other than culture.","Weiss would argue that environmental differences cannot affect animal behavior in any meaningful way."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Weiss suggests group differences may reflect local environments rather than learned traditions, offering an alternative explanation for the evidence in Text 1.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians credit Florence Nightingale's reforms during the Crimean War with dramatically reducing deaths among British soldiers. When she arrived at the army hospital in Scutari in 1854, they note, conditions were filthy, and the death rate fell sharply after she introduced better nursing practices.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Philip Grant points out that the death rate at Scutari actually rose during Nightingale's first months there. It fell sharply only after a government sanitary commission arrived in 1855 and flushed out the hospital's sewers, which had been contaminating its water supply. Nightingale, Grant notes, later studied these statistics and became a lifelong champion of sanitation.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Grant would argue that conditions at the hospital in Scutari were clean when Nightingale arrived in 1854.","Grant would argue that the decline in deaths at Scutari is better explained by the sanitary commission's work than by Nightingale's initial reforms.","Grant would agree that Nightingale's improved nursing practices caused the death rate at Scutari to fall immediately after she arrived at the hospital in 1854.","Grant would argue that Nightingale opposed the sanitary commission's efforts to repair the hospital's sewers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Grant notes deaths fell only after the sanitary commission fixed the sewers, suggesting the decline credited to Nightingale is better attributed to the commission.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics of standardized testing argue that test scores largely reflect students' family income rather than their abilities. Wealthier families can afford tutoring and test preparation, they note, giving their children an unfair advantage.<br><br>Text 2<br>Education researcher Monica Hale agrees that test scores correlate with family income. But she notes that other measures used in admissions, such as essays, extracurricular activities, and recommendation letters, correlate with income even more strongly. Eliminating tests, Hale argues, may make admissions less fair to low-income students, not more.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, the critics in Text 1 and Hale (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that test scores are related to students' family income.","Both would agree that standardized tests should be eliminated from admissions.","Both would agree that essays are a fairer measure of ability than test scores.","Both would agree that tutoring has no effect on students' test scores."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The critics argue that scores reflect income, and Hale "agrees that test scores correlate with family income," though they disagree about what to do.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Novelist Sarah Kim argues that fiction is best written from personal experience. Writers who invent worlds they have never known, she contends, inevitably produce work that feels false, because readers can sense when an author does not truly understand what she describes.<br><br>Text 2<br>Novelist Tomás Reyes argues that imagination is the essence of fiction. Some of the most convincing novels ever written, he notes, depict places and times their authors never experienced. What matters, Reyes says, is not whether a writer has lived something but whether she has done the work—through research, observation, and empathy—to understand it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Reyes (Text 2) most likely respond to Kim's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Reyes would agree that novels about unfamiliar places inevitably feel false to readers.","Reyes would argue that writers should avoid researching the settings of their novels.","Reyes would argue that personal experience makes fiction less convincing to readers.","Reyes would argue that understanding can come from sources other than direct personal experience."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Reyes says the key is understanding, which writers can gain "through research, observation, and empathy," not only through living something.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine biologists have long assumed that the ocean's deepest trenches are nearly lifeless, too cold, dark, and high-pressure to support more than a few hardy species of microbes and scavengers.<br><br>Text 2<br>In 2022, a team led by biologist Ana Silva used robotic landers to survey a trench more than 8,000 meters deep. They recorded dozens of species, including fish, shrimp-like amphipods, and sea cucumbers, many feeding on organic material that drifts down from the surface and collects at the trench floor. Silva argues that trenches may be hotspots of deep-sea life rather than barren extremes.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Silva (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Silva would agree that deep trenches are nearly lifeless and argue that the species she found were rare exceptions.","Silva would argue that most life in deep trenches consists of microbes rather than animals.","Silva would argue that the assumption in Text 1 underestimates the variety of life in deep trenches.","Silva would argue that organic material from the surface never reaches the floors of deep trenches."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Silva's survey found dozens of animal species and led her to suggest trenches may be "hotspots" of life, contradicting the assumption of near-lifelessness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Daniel Reiss argues that the Industrial Revolution began in Britain because of its abundant coal deposits. Cheap, accessible coal powered steam engines and iron furnaces, he contends, giving British manufacturers an advantage no other country could match.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Priya Nair agrees that coal was important but argues that it cannot be the whole story: China also had large coal deposits. Britain's distinctive advantage, she contends, was its unusually high wages, which gave manufacturers a strong incentive to invest in labor-saving machines. Where labor was cheap, there was less reason to mechanize.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Nair (Text 2) most likely respond to Reiss's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Nair would argue that coal played no meaningful role in the Industrial Revolution and that Britain's high wages alone explain why it industrialized first.","Nair would agree that coal mattered but argue that Reiss's explanation cannot account for why industrialization began in Britain rather than elsewhere.","Nair would agree with Reiss and add that China lacked the coal needed to industrialize.","Nair would argue that low wages in Britain discouraged manufacturers from investing in machines."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Nair "agrees that coal was important" but notes that China also had coal, so coal alone can't explain Britain's lead; she adds high wages as the distinctive factor.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some city leaders argue that building more housing will lower rents. When the supply of apartments rises faster than demand, they reason, landlords must compete for tenants, and prices fall.<br><br>Text 2<br>Urban economist Jada Brooks agrees that increasing supply tends to moderate rents over time. But she notes that new construction is usually expensive, high-end housing, and its effects on rents in lower-income neighborhoods can take years to appear. In the meantime, she argues, cities need policies such as rental assistance to protect vulnerable tenants.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brooks (Text 2) most likely respond to the city leaders' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Brooks would accept the reasoning in Text 1 but argue that its benefits may be slow to reach lower-income renters.","Brooks would argue that building more housing, especially expensive high-end housing, causes rents to rise in nearby lower-income neighborhoods.","Brooks would argue that cities should stop building high-end housing altogether.","Brooks would agree that new housing immediately lowers rents in all neighborhoods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Brooks "agrees that increasing supply tends to moderate rents" but notes that effects reach lower-income areas slowly, so other measures are needed meanwhile.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ornithologist Kevin Marsh argues that songbirds learn their songs primarily by imitating adult birds during a critical period early in life. Birds raised in isolation, he notes, produce abnormal songs, which shows that hearing adults is essential.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ornithologist Hana Sato agrees that young birds learn from adults but has found that, in some species, birds continue to modify their songs throughout their lives, adding new phrases they hear from neighbors. Song learning, she argues, is not confined to a single early period in every species.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Sato (Text 2) most likely respond to Marsh's claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Sato would argue that birds raised in isolation produce normal songs.","Sato would argue that young birds do not learn songs from adult birds.","Sato would agree with Marsh that, in every species, song learning ends after a critical period early in life, once young birds have heard adults sing.","Sato would agree that learning from adults is important but argue that Marsh's account of when learning occurs does not apply to all species."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Sato agrees birds learn from adults but finds that some species keep modifying songs throughout life, so learning isn't always confined to an early period.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many critics argue that film adaptations of novels inevitably fall short of their sources. A novel can explore a character's inner thoughts at length, they note, while a film is limited to what can be shown and heard.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film scholar Olivia Grant contends that this comparison misses the point. Film has its own resources—camera movement, editing, music, an actor's face in close-up—that can convey inner life in ways prose cannot. The best adaptations, she argues, do not try to reproduce the novel but to translate it into a different art form.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Grant would agree that film adaptations inevitably fall short of the novels on which they are based.","Grant would argue that novels are unable to explore characters' inner thoughts in depth.","Grant would argue that the critics underestimate film's own ways of conveying characters' inner lives.","Grant would argue that the best adaptations reproduce their source novels as closely as possible."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grant says film has "its own resources" that can convey inner life, countering the critics' claim that film is limited.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some nutrition experts argue that people should avoid all processed foods. Processed foods, they contend, are generally high in salt, sugar, and unhealthy fats, and diets rich in them are linked to obesity and heart disease.<br><br>Text 2<br>Food scientist Raj Patel notes that "processed" covers an enormous range of foods, from sugary snacks to frozen vegetables, canned beans, and whole-grain bread. Many processed foods, he argues, are nutritious and affordable, and advice to avoid all of them may discourage people from eating healthy options that fit their budgets.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Patel (Text 2) most likely respond to the experts' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Patel would argue that sugary snacks are as nutritious as frozen vegetables and canned beans.","Patel would argue that the experts' advice treats a very diverse category of foods as if all were equally unhealthy.","Patel would agree that people should avoid all processed foods, including frozen vegetables and canned beans, because of their salt, sugar, and fat.","Patel would argue that diets high in salt and sugar are not linked to heart disease."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Patel points out that "processed" includes many nutritious foods, so blanket avoidance is misguided.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many astronomers believe that Planet Nine—a hypothetical planet several times the mass of Earth—orbits far beyond Neptune. They point to a group of distant icy objects whose orbits are oddly clustered, which they argue could be explained by the gravitational pull of an unseen planet.<br><br>Text 2<br>Astronomer Felix Wong is unconvinced. The clustering, he notes, may be an illusion created by where telescopes have looked: surveys have observed only certain regions of the sky, so they may have detected only objects whose orbits happen to point in those directions. More complete surveys, Wong argues, are needed before inferring a new planet.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Wong (Text 2) most likely respond to the astronomers' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Wong would argue that the evidence cited in Text 1 might reflect how the observations were made rather than a real pattern.","Wong would argue that Planet Nine has already been observed directly by telescopes.","Wong would agree that the clustered orbits of the distant icy objects prove that a planet several times Earth's mass exists beyond Neptune.","Wong would argue that the distant icy objects do not orbit the Sun at all."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Wong suggests the clustering may be an artifact of survey coverage (an observational bias) rather than evidence of a planet.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Sociologist Martin Hale argues that public libraries are becoming obsolete. With so much information available online, he contends, fewer people need to visit a building to borrow books or look up facts, and cities should reconsider how much they spend on libraries.<br><br>Text 2<br>Librarian and researcher Teresa Cruz notes that library visits in many cities have remained steady or grown. Libraries, she explains, increasingly serve as community centers: they provide free internet access, job-search help, children's programs, and quiet places to study—services especially valuable to people who lack them at home.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Cruz (Text 2) most likely respond to Hale's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Cruz would agree that libraries are becoming obsolete and argue that cities should close them.","Cruz would argue that most people can find all the information they need without using the internet.","Cruz would argue that libraries should stop offering internet access and job-search help and should instead focus on lending books to their patrons.","Cruz would argue that Hale's view overlooks the many roles libraries play beyond lending books and providing facts."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Cruz describes libraries' expanded community roles and steady use, countering Hale's narrow view of their function.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers studying sleep have found that teenagers' internal clocks shift during puberty, making them naturally inclined to fall asleep and wake later. Many sleep scientists therefore recommend that high schools start no earlier than 8:30 a.m.<br><br>Text 2<br>When one school district moved its high school start time from 7:30 to 8:45 a.m., researchers tracked the results. Students slept about forty minutes more per night on average, attendance improved, and grades rose modestly. Some parents complained about the later schedule's effect on after-school activities and jobs.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, which choice best describes the relationship between Text 1 and Text 2?$q$, $q$["The findings in Text 2 show that the recommendation in Text 1 had no measurable effect on how much sleep students got or on their attendance.","The findings in Text 2 suggest that teenagers' internal clocks do not actually shift during puberty.","The findings in Text 2 provide support for the recommendation described in Text 1, though they also note a drawback.","The findings in Text 2 show that earlier start times improve students' attendance and grades."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2's results (more sleep, better attendance and grades after a later start) support Text 1's recommendation, while noting parents' complaints.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many scholars have described the medieval Silk Road trade as dominated by luxury goods: silk, spices, and precious stones that only the wealthy could afford. Because these goods were light and valuable, they argue, they were worth transporting over thousands of miles.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mei Lin argues that this emphasis on luxuries distorts the picture. Documents from oasis towns along the route, she notes, record large volumes of ordinary goods—grain, livestock, cloth, and pottery—traded over shorter distances between neighboring communities. The long-distance luxury trade, she contends, rested on a much larger foundation of local exchange.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lin (Text 2) most likely respond to the scholars' description in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Lin would argue that luxury goods were never transported over long distances on the Silk Road.","Lin would argue that the scholars' account neglects the everyday trade that made up much of the commerce along the routes.","Lin would agree that the Silk Road trade consisted almost entirely of light, valuable luxury goods that only the wealthy could afford to buy.","Lin would argue that grain and livestock were more valuable than silk and spices."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lin argues that documents show large volumes of ordinary goods traded locally, so a focus on luxuries leaves out much of the trade.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Laura Benson argues that praising children for their intelligence can backfire. Children praised for being "smart," her experiments show, tend to avoid challenging tasks afterward, apparently fearing that failure would reveal they are not smart after all.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Omar Haddad has tried to replicate Benson's experiments with larger groups of children. He found the same pattern, but the effect was considerably smaller than in her original studies. Haddad suggests that the way children are praised matters, but perhaps less than early studies implied.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to Benson's findings in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Haddad would agree with the direction of Benson's finding but suggest that its size may have been overstated.","Haddad would argue that praising children for intelligence makes them more likely to seek challenges.","Haddad would argue that Benson's experiments could not be replicated with larger groups.","Haddad would agree that the effect Benson found is as large as her original studies suggested."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Haddad "found the same pattern" but a smaller effect, suggesting the original studies overstated its size.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some art critics argue that museums should display art without explanatory labels. Labels, they contend, tell viewers what to think and see, preventing them from forming their own responses to the work.<br><br>Text 2<br>Curator Daniela Ross agrees that labels can be overbearing, especially when they dictate a single interpretation. But for many visitors, she argues, a short label providing context—when and where a work was made, or what its symbols meant to its original audience—opens up the work rather than closing it off, particularly for art from unfamiliar cultures.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ross (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ross would argue that labels should always tell viewers how a work should be interpreted, especially when the art comes from an unfamiliar culture.","Ross would agree that museums should remove all labels from works of art.","Ross would argue that art from unfamiliar cultures should not be displayed in museums.","Ross would agree that some labels limit viewers' responses but argue that well-designed labels can enrich them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ross concedes labels can be "overbearing" but argues contextual labels open up works for many visitors.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Geologists have traditionally explained the Grand Canyon as the product of the Colorado River slowly cutting through rock over about six million years.<br><br>Text 2<br>Geologist Karen Holt argues that parts of the canyon are far older. Analyzing minerals in the canyon walls that record when the rock was last near the surface, her team concluded that some segments were carved by ancient rivers as much as 70 million years ago, long before the modern Colorado River existed. The modern river, Holt suggests, linked together and deepened canyons that already existed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Holt (Text 2) most likely respond to the traditional explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Holt would argue that the Colorado River played no role in shaping the Grand Canyon.","Holt would agree that the entire Grand Canyon was carved by the Colorado River within the last six million years, as geologists have long believed.","Holt would argue that the traditional explanation leaves out an earlier history of carving by other rivers.","Holt would argue that the Grand Canyon was formed by glaciers rather than by rivers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Holt argues that ancient rivers carved some segments long before the Colorado existed, which the six-million-year explanation does not account for; she still assigns the modern river a role.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Nina Park argues that universal basic income—regular cash payments to all citizens regardless of employment—would reduce people's motivation to work. If people could meet their basic needs without a job, she contends, many would leave the workforce.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Samuel Okoye points to several pilot programs in which participants received guaranteed payments. In most, employment declined only slightly or not at all; some recipients used the money to search for better jobs, start businesses, or complete training. Okoye cautions, though, that pilots were small and temporary, and that permanent programs might have different effects.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okoye (Text 2) most likely respond to Park's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Okoye would agree with Park that guaranteed payments cause most people to leave the workforce.","Okoye would argue that pilot evidence does not support Park's prediction, while acknowledging the evidence has limits.","Okoye would argue that permanent guaranteed-income programs have already proven more effective at encouraging work than the small pilots did.","Okoye would argue that universal basic income should be given only to people without jobs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okoye's evidence shows little decline in work, countering Park, but he cautions that small, temporary pilots may not predict permanent programs.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians argue that the Great Wall of China was ineffective, pointing out that invading armies repeatedly breached it or simply went around it.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Wei Zhang contends that judging the wall by whether it stopped every invasion misunderstands its purpose. The wall, she argues, was designed less to halt large armies than to slow raiding parties, channel trade through controlled gates, and allow signal fires to warn of approaching threats. By those measures, she suggests, it was often quite successful.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Zhang (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Zhang would argue that the historians evaluate the wall by a standard that does not match its intended functions.","Zhang would argue that no invading army ever breached or went around the Great Wall.","Zhang would agree that the wall was ineffective because it failed to stop the large armies that repeatedly breached it or went around it.","Zhang would argue that the wall was built mainly to keep Chinese citizens from leaving."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Zhang says judging the wall by whether it stopped every invasion "misunderstands its purpose," since it was meant to slow raids, control trade, and give warning.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers who study creativity have often argued that people are most creative when they are free from constraints. Limits on time, materials, or subject matter, they contend, restrict the range of ideas people can generate.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Elena Moss found the opposite in a series of experiments. Participants given strict constraints—such as designing a toy using only five specific materials—produced ideas that judges rated as more original than those produced by participants with no limits. Constraints, Moss suggests, push people beyond their first, most obvious ideas.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Moss (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Moss would agree that people are most creative when they are free from all constraints.","Moss would argue that judges are unable to rate the originality of ideas fairly.","Moss would argue that participants with no limits produced more ideas than those with constraints.","Moss would argue that her findings contradict the view that constraints restrict creativity."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Moss found that constrained participants produced more original ideas, contradicting the claim that constraints restrict creativity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many wildlife managers advocate culling—deliberately killing a portion of a population—to control invasive species such as feral pigs, which damage crops and native ecosystems.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Ben Archer notes that culling feral pigs often fails to reduce their numbers for long. Pigs reproduce so quickly that surviving animals can replace the losses within a year or two, and culling can scatter groups into new areas. Archer argues that culling works only when it removes nearly an entire local population at once, which requires coordinated effort across large areas.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Archer (Text 2) most likely respond to the wildlife managers' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Archer would argue that feral pigs do not damage crops or native ecosystems.","Archer would agree that culling small numbers of pigs is the most effective way to control them.","Archer would argue that culling as commonly practiced may not achieve the goal its advocates intend.","Archer would argue that feral pigs reproduce too slowly to recover from culling."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Archer says culling often fails because pigs reproduce quickly and scatter, unless nearly whole populations are removed—so typical culling may not work.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Irene Walsh argues that eyewitness memory is far less reliable than juries tend to believe. In her studies, participants who watched a staged crime often confidently identified the wrong person, especially when investigators asked leading questions.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Tomás Vega agrees that eyewitness memory can be distorted. But his research shows that a witness's first identification, made quickly and under fair conditions, is often quite accurate; errors tend to accumulate when witnesses are questioned repeatedly or shown the suspect more than once. Vega argues that the problem lies less in memory itself than in how it is tested.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Walsh (Text 1) and Vega (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that eyewitness identifications should never be used as evidence in court.","Both would agree that the way witnesses are questioned can affect the accuracy of their identifications.","Both would agree that a witness's confidence is a reliable sign that an identification is accurate.","Both would agree that a witness's first identification is usually less accurate than later ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Walsh notes errors occur "especially when investigators asked leading questions," and Vega says errors accumulate with repeated questioning—both see questioning as affecting accuracy.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some economists argue that inflation is caused mainly by governments printing too much money. When the money supply grows faster than the economy's output, they contend, prices inevitably rise.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Adaeze Nwosu acknowledges that rapid growth in the money supply can cause inflation. But she points to recent episodes in which prices rose sharply because of disruptions in supply—shipping bottlenecks, poor harvests, and spikes in energy costs—even when money growth was modest. Inflation, she argues, can have several distinct causes, and the right policy response depends on which is at work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts view the causes of inflation?$q$, $q$["Text 1 presents a single main cause of inflation, while Text 2 argues that inflation can arise from different causes that call for different responses.","Text 1 argues that inflation is caused by supply disruptions, while Text 2 argues that it is caused mainly by growth in the money supply.","Text 1 treats inflation as a problem governments can control, while Text 2 argues that inflation caused by supply disruptions cannot be addressed by policy at all.","Text 1 argues that governments should print more money, while Text 2 argues that they should print less money."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 attributes inflation mainly to money printing; Nwosu argues it has "several distinct causes," including supply disruptions, requiring different policies.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Scholars of the ancient world often describe the city of Athens as the birthplace of democracy. In the fifth century BCE, Athenian citizens voted directly on laws and on decisions of war and peace, a system that has inspired democratic movements ever since.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Lena Petridis cautions that Athenian democracy excluded most of the people who lived in Athens. Women, enslaved people, and foreign residents—together the large majority of the population—could not vote. Petridis does not deny Athens's influence but argues that calling it a democracy in the modern sense obscures how narrow its political community was.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Petridis (Text 2) most likely respond to the scholars' description in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Petridis would argue that the scholars overstate Athens's influence on later democratic movements, since most of those movements drew on other models instead.","Petridis would agree that Athens was a democracy in the modern sense and that it included most residents.","Petridis would argue that Athens had no influence on the democratic movements that followed it.","Petridis would acknowledge the influence described in Text 1 but argue that the description overlooks the system's exclusions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Petridis "does not deny Athens's influence" but stresses that most residents couldn't vote, so the label "democracy" obscures its narrowness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Engineer Sam Kowalczyk argues that self-driving cars will dramatically reduce traffic deaths. More than 90 percent of crashes involve human error, he notes, such as distraction, fatigue, or impaired driving—problems that automated systems do not have.<br><br>Text 2<br>Transportation safety researcher Ingrid Holm points out that automated systems introduce their own kinds of errors, such as misreading unusual road conditions or failing to recognize objects they were not trained on. She agrees that automation could prevent many crashes caused by human error but argues that its overall effect on safety cannot be known until such systems have been tested over billions of miles in varied conditions.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Holm (Text 2) most likely respond to Kowalczyk's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Holm would argue that Kowalczyk overstates the role of human error, since most crashes are actually caused by poor road conditions and unusual objects.","Holm would agree that self-driving cars will certainly reduce traffic deaths dramatically.","Holm would agree that automation could prevent certain crashes but argue that Kowalczyk's conclusion is premature.","Holm would argue that automated systems will eventually make more errors than human drivers once they are tested over billions of miles."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Holm "agrees that automation could prevent many crashes caused by human error" but argues that the overall effect is unknown until extensive testing is done.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Rachel Stone argues that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change. Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, she notes, and large-scale planting programs could remove billions of tons of carbon from the atmosphere.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Marco Silva agrees that forests store large amounts of carbon. But he warns that planting trees in the wrong places can backfire: in grasslands and savannas, which naturally have few trees, new plantations can dry out soils, displace native species, and even release carbon stored underground. Protecting existing forests, Silva argues, is often more effective than planting new ones.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Stone (Text 1) and Silva (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that trees should be planted in grasslands and savannas.","Both would agree that trees can store significant amounts of carbon.","Both would agree that planting trees is always more effective than protecting forests.","Both would agree that forests release more carbon than they absorb."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Stone says trees absorb carbon, and Silva "agrees that forests store large amounts of carbon," though they differ on where and how to act.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Music historians have often treated the blues as a purely rural tradition, born among Black farmworkers in the Mississippi Delta and only later brought to cities like Chicago.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Darnell Price argues that the blues developed through constant movement between country and city. Musicians traveled widely, he notes, performing in rural juke joints and urban theaters alike, and early recordings made in cities shaped how rural musicians played. The rural-to-urban story, Price contends, reduces a two-way exchange to a one-way journey.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Price (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Price would argue that the historians' account simplifies a relationship in which influence flowed in both directions.","Price would argue that the blues originated in Chicago and only later spread to the Mississippi Delta.","Price would agree that the blues began among rural farmworkers but argue that it developed its distinctive sound only after musicians moved permanently to Chicago.","Price would argue that rural musicians never performed in urban theaters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Price says the rural-to-urban story "reduces a two-way exchange to a one-way journey," describing influence moving both ways.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Medical researcher Hannah Fields argues that annual physical exams for healthy adults are largely unnecessary. Large reviews of the evidence, she notes, have found that routine checkups do not reduce deaths from heart disease or cancer, and they may lead to unnecessary tests.<br><br>Text 2<br>Physician Arjun Mehta agrees that annual exams have not been shown to reduce deaths. But he argues that they serve other purposes: they allow doctors to update vaccinations, discuss changes in health habits, and build relationships with patients that make them more likely to seek care when problems arise.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mehta (Text 2) most likely respond to Fields's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Mehta would argue that Fields relies on flawed reviews and that annual exams do in fact reduce deaths from heart disease and cancer among healthy adults.","Mehta would agree with Fields that annual exams should be eliminated for all healthy adults.","Mehta would argue that unnecessary tests are the main benefit of annual exams.","Mehta would accept the evidence Fields cites but argue that annual exams have benefits that the evidence does not measure."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mehta "agrees that annual exams have not been shown to reduce deaths" but points to other benefits not captured by mortality data.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many linguists have predicted that globalization will cause most of the world's roughly 7,000 languages to disappear within a century, as speakers shift to dominant languages such as English, Spanish, and Mandarin.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Kaya Begay acknowledges that many languages are endangered. But she points to communities that have reversed language loss through immersion schools, recordings of elders, and the use of social media in their heritage languages. These efforts, Begay argues, show that language decline is not inevitable when communities have resources and support.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Begay (Text 2) most likely respond to the linguists' prediction in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Begay would argue that the linguists exaggerate the threat of globalization, since most endangered languages are declining for reasons unrelated to dominant languages.","Begay would agree that most of the world's languages will certainly disappear within a century.","Begay would argue that the linguists' prediction underestimates what communities can do to preserve their languages.","Begay would argue that social media is the main cause of language loss around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Begay acknowledges endangerment but argues that revitalization efforts show decline "is not inevitable," challenging the confident prediction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some biologists argue that the brightly colored feathers of male birds evolved mainly to attract mates. Females, they note, often choose the most colorful males, and over many generations this preference would favor brighter plumage.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Nora Kim suggests that bright colors can also serve as warnings. In some species, she notes, males with brighter plumage are more aggressive defenders of territory, and rivals avoid confronting them. Coloration, Kim argues, may often be shaped by competition among males as well as by female choice.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kim (Text 2) most likely respond to the biologists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Kim would argue that females never choose mates based on the color of their feathers.","Kim would argue that the biologists' explanation may be incomplete because coloration can also be shaped by competition among males.","Kim would argue that the biologists are mistaken about female choice, since in most species females prefer the least colorful and least aggressive males.","Kim would argue that brightly colored males are less aggressive than duller males."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Kim says coloration "may often be shaped by competition among males as well as by female choice," adding to rather than rejecting the biologists' explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many reviewers have praised the documentary for its objectivity. The filmmaker, they note, never appears on screen, uses no narration, and simply lets the people she filmed speak for themselves.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film critic Jonah Abrams argues that no documentary is truly objective. Every film, he points out, is shaped by decisions about whom to film, which moments to include, and how to order them. A documentary without narration, Abrams contends, may actually conceal the filmmaker's perspective more effectively than one that states it openly.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Abrams (Text 2) most likely respond to the reviewers' praise in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Abrams would argue that the absence of narration does not make a documentary objective and may hide the filmmaker's choices.","Abrams would agree that the documentary is objective because the filmmaker never appears on screen.","Abrams would argue that documentaries with narration are more honest than those without it, because narration always reveals the filmmaker's true opinions.","Abrams would argue that the people in the documentary did not actually speak for themselves."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Abrams argues that editing choices shape every film and that lack of narration may conceal the filmmaker's perspective, countering the reviewers' praise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian George Lyle argues that the transcontinental railroad, completed in the United States in 1869, was a triumph of engineering and national unity. It cut travel time across the continent from months to about a week, binding the country's eastern and western regions together.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mei Chen does not dispute the railroad's importance but focuses on the people who built it. Thousands of Chinese immigrants did much of the most dangerous work in the Sierra Nevada, she notes, at lower pay than white workers, and many died in accidents and avalanches. Their contributions, Chen argues, were largely erased from celebrations of the railroad's completion.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Chen (Text 2) most likely respond to Lyle's account in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Chen would argue that the railroad did not significantly reduce travel time across the continent.","Chen would argue that Lyle overstates the railroad's role in uniting the country, since its completion did little to change travel between the East and the West.","Chen would argue that the railroad was built mainly by workers who were paid more than others.","Chen would accept the railroad's significance but argue that accounts like Lyle's leave out the experiences of the workers who built it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Chen "does not dispute the railroad's importance" but emphasizes that Chinese workers' dangerous labor was erased from celebrations—an omission in triumphal accounts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some neuroscientists argue that learning a second language in childhood leads to lasting cognitive benefits, such as an improved ability to switch between tasks and ignore distractions. Bilingual children, they note, often outperform monolingual children on tests of these skills.<br><br>Text 2<br>A large study by psychologist Kirsten Dahl compared thousands of bilingual and monolingual children and found no consistent differences in task-switching or attention once family income and parental education were taken into account. Dahl suggests that earlier studies, which were often small, may have detected differences caused by other factors.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Dahl (Text 2) most likely respond to the neuroscientists' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Dahl would argue that bilingual children perform much worse than monolingual children on tests of attention.","Dahl would agree that bilingualism produces lasting improvements in task-switching for all children.","Dahl would argue that the advantage described in Text 1 may reflect other factors rather than bilingualism itself.","Dahl would argue that family income has no effect on children's performance on cognitive tests."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Dahl's large study found no difference after controlling for income and education, suggesting earlier findings reflected those factors.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many urban planners favor building tall residential towers to house growing city populations. Towers, they argue, fit many people onto small plots of land, preserving open space and supporting public transit.<br><br>Text 2<br>Architect Ingrid Sørensen argues that cities can achieve similar densities with mid-rise buildings of five to eight stories arranged around shared courtyards, as in many European cities. Such buildings, she notes, are cheaper to build per apartment, use less energy for elevators and heating, and encourage residents to know their neighbors.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Sørensen (Text 2) most likely respond to the planners' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Sørensen would argue that cities should avoid dense housing and build single-family homes instead.","Sørensen would agree that dense housing is valuable but argue that it need not take the form the planners favor.","Sørensen would agree that tall towers are the only way to house growing city populations.","Sørensen would argue that mid-rise buildings achieve lower densities than towers but are worth building anyway because they help residents know their neighbors."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sørensen says mid-rise buildings can "achieve similar densities" with added benefits—accepting density as a goal but proposing a different form.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Wildlife biologist Owen Price argues that domestic cats are a major threat to birds. In the United States alone, he notes, outdoor cats kill an estimated 1 to 4 billion birds each year, making them one of the leading human-related causes of bird deaths.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Leah Marsh agrees that cats kill enormous numbers of birds but notes that most of those killed are common species whose populations are stable. Habitat loss and collisions with buildings, she argues, pose greater threats to declining species and deserve more attention than they receive.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Price (Text 1) and Marsh (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that outdoor cats kill very large numbers of birds.","Both would agree that cats are the main threat to declining bird species.","Both would agree that collisions with buildings kill more birds than cats do.","Both would agree that the bird species killed by cats are mostly declining species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Price cites billions of birds killed, and Marsh "agrees that cats kill enormous numbers of birds," though they differ about which threats matter most.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many art historians regard the Renaissance as a sharp break from the Middle Ages, when artists rediscovered the realism and proportions of ancient Greek and Roman art after centuries of neglect.<br><br>Text 2<br>Art historian Caterina Bellini argues that the break was less sharp than often claimed. Medieval artists, she notes, studied and copied ancient works throughout the period, and some fourteenth-century painters were already experimenting with depth and naturalistic detail. The Renaissance, Bellini contends, intensified trends that had been developing for generations.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bellini (Text 2) most likely respond to the art historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Bellini would argue that medieval artists never studied or copied ancient Greek and Roman art.","Bellini would agree that the Renaissance marked a complete break with the art of the Middle Ages.","Bellini would argue that fourteenth-century painters rejected naturalistic detail in their work.","Bellini would argue that the historians exaggerate how abruptly the Renaissance departed from earlier art."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Bellini says the break was "less sharp than often claimed" and that the Renaissance "intensified trends" already underway.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many researchers credit the decline in smoking in the United States since the 1960s mainly to public health campaigns that informed people about the dangers of tobacco.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Rafael Duarte argues that rising cigarette taxes played at least as large a role. His analysis of state data found that smoking rates fell fastest in states that raised taxes most sharply, even after accounting for differences in public health campaigns. Young people, who have less money to spend, were especially responsive to higher prices.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts explain the decline in smoking?$q$, $q$["Text 1 focuses on the decline of smoking among young people, while Text 2 focuses on older adults.","Text 1 argues that smoking rates have increased since the 1960s, while Text 2 argues that they have declined.","Text 1 emphasizes the role of information about health risks, while Text 2 emphasizes the role of price.","Text 1 argues that taxes reduced smoking, while Text 2 argues that public health campaigns had no effect."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 1 credits public health campaigns (information), while Duarte emphasizes cigarette taxes (price).$q$),
  ($q$cs-h271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some scientists argue that the extinction of many large animals in the Americas around 13,000 years ago—mammoths, giant ground sloths, and saber-toothed cats among them—was caused mainly by human hunters, who arrived in the region around the same time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Paleontologist Grace Hollis notes that the extinctions also coincided with rapid climate change at the end of the last ice age, which transformed the grasslands many large animals depended on. Hollis argues that hunting and climate change likely worked together: animals already stressed by shrinking habitats may have been unable to withstand additional pressure from human hunters.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Hollis (Text 2) most likely respond to the scientists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Hollis would argue that human hunters played no role in the extinction of large animals in the Americas.","Hollis would argue that the scientists' explanation is incomplete because it does not account for the role of climate change.","Hollis would argue that climate change alone caused the extinctions, since the large animals died out before hunters could have had any significant effect.","Hollis would argue that the extinctions occurred long before humans arrived in the Americas."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Hollis argues hunting and climate change "likely worked together," adding a factor that the hunting-focused explanation omits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many teachers believe that students should be allowed to use calculators freely in math classes. Calculators, they argue, free students from tedious arithmetic and allow them to focus on understanding concepts and solving complex problems.<br><br>Text 2<br>Math educator Rosa Delgado agrees that calculators can be valuable tools. But she cautions that students who rely on them too early may never develop number sense—the intuitive understanding of how numbers relate—that helps them notice when an answer is unreasonable. Delgado recommends that students master basic arithmetic before using calculators routinely.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Delgado (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Delgado would accept the benefits the teachers describe but argue that the timing of calculator use matters.","Delgado would argue that calculators should never be used in any math class.","Delgado would agree that students should use calculators freely from the very beginning of their schooling.","Delgado would argue that number sense is unimportant for solving complex problems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Delgado "agrees that calculators can be valuable tools" but recommends mastering arithmetic first—so she objects to early, unrestricted use.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary critic Alan Hughes argues that a novel's meaning is determined by its author's intentions. To understand a work correctly, he contends, readers should seek out what the author meant to convey, using letters, interviews, and other biographical evidence.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Yuna Park argues that once a novel is published, its meaning is no longer controlled by its author. Readers from different times and places, she notes, find meanings in classic works that their authors could not have foreseen, and these readings can be just as valid as the author's own.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Hughes (Text 1) and Park (Text 2) view the meaning of a novel?$q$, $q$["Hughes argues that biographical evidence is unreliable, while Park argues that readers should rely on it.","Hughes values letters and interviews as evidence of meaning, while Park argues that biographical evidence is useful only for understanding contemporary novels.","Hughes argues that authors rarely have clear intentions, while Park argues that they always do.","Hughes locates a work's meaning in the author's intentions, while Park believes readers can validly find meanings the author did not intend."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hughes ties meaning to authorial intention; Park argues that readers' unforeseen interpretations can be equally valid.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Supporters of daylight saving time argue that shifting clocks forward in spring saves energy. With more daylight in the evening, they contend, people use less electricity for lighting.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Laura Bennett studied electricity use in a U.S. state that adopted daylight saving time statewide in 2006. She found that household electricity use actually rose slightly, because savings on lighting were outweighed by increased use of air conditioning in the evening and heating in the morning.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bennett (Text 2) most likely respond to the supporters' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Bennett's findings support the claim in Text 1 by showing that daylight saving time reduces electricity use.","Bennett's findings show that daylight saving time has no effect on how people use lighting in the evening.","Bennett's findings challenge the claim in Text 1 by showing that other energy uses offset the savings on lighting.","Bennett's findings show that people use more lighting in the evening during daylight saving time."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Bennett found that lighting savings were outweighed by higher heating and cooling use, so electricity use rose—contradicting the energy-saving claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Anthropologist Peter Lang argues that early farming communities were healthier than the hunter-gatherer groups that preceded them. Farming, he contends, provided a more reliable food supply, reducing the risk of starvation.<br><br>Text 2<br>Anthropologist Amina Yusuf points to skeletons from early farming villages, which often show signs of poorer health than those of earlier hunter-gatherers: shorter stature, more tooth decay, and evidence of nutritional deficiencies. Diets based on a few crops, she argues, lacked the variety of foraged foods, and crowded villages helped diseases spread.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Yusuf (Text 2) most likely respond to Lang's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Yusuf would agree that early farmers were healthier than hunter-gatherers because of their reliable food supply.","Yusuf would argue that skeletal evidence contradicts Lang's claim about the health of early farmers.","Yusuf would argue that hunter-gatherers were more likely to experience tooth decay than early farmers.","Yusuf would argue that early farming villages were less crowded than hunter-gatherer camps."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Yusuf cites skeletons showing poorer health among early farmers, contradicting Lang's claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many critics consider the composer's late string quartets her finest work, praising their emotional depth and structural complexity. Written after she lost most of her hearing, they argue, the quartets reveal an inner world freed from outside influence.<br><br>Text 2<br>Musicologist Anton Becker agrees that the late quartets are extraordinary. But he notes that the composer's letters from the period show her intensely engaged with the music of younger composers, which friends played for her on the piano so she could feel the vibrations. The quartets, Becker argues, reflect dialogue with her contemporaries, not isolation from them.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Becker (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Becker would share the critics' high opinion of the quartets but dispute their claim about the composer's isolation from outside influence.","Becker would argue that the late quartets reflect the influence of the composer's hearing loss more than the critics recognize, making them less original.","Becker would agree that the quartets were written in complete isolation from other music.","Becker would argue that the composer did not lose her hearing late in her life."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becker "agrees that the late quartets are extraordinary" but argues they reflect dialogue with contemporaries, not freedom from outside influence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some ocean scientists propose fertilizing parts of the ocean with iron to fight climate change. Iron, they note, stimulates the growth of plankton, which absorb carbon dioxide; when the plankton die, some sink to the deep ocean, carrying carbon with them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Oceanographer Helen Ruiz notes that field experiments have produced disappointing results. Although iron triggered plankton blooms, most of the carbon they absorbed was released back into the water when the plankton were eaten or decomposed near the surface, and only a small fraction sank. The blooms also risked depleting oxygen and disrupting food webs.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ruiz (Text 2) most likely respond to the proposal in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ruiz would argue that iron fertilization fails because iron does not stimulate plankton growth in the open ocean, though it may do so in coastal waters.","Ruiz would agree that iron fertilization has been proven to remove large amounts of carbon.","Ruiz would argue that plankton never absorb carbon dioxide from the water.","Ruiz would argue that field experiments suggest the proposal is less effective than its advocates expect and carries risks."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ruiz reports that little carbon sank and that blooms carried ecological risks, undermining the proposal's promise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians portray the Vikings primarily as raiders who terrorized the coasts of Europe, plundering monasteries and towns from the late eighth century onward.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ingrid Larsen emphasizes that raiding was only one part of Viking activity. Vikings also established extensive trade networks stretching from the Arctic to Baghdad, founded towns such as Dublin, and settled as farmers in places including Iceland and Greenland. Focusing on raids, she argues, obscures the economic and cultural roles that Vikings played.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Larsen (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' portrayal in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Larsen would argue that the Vikings never raided monasteries or towns in Europe.","Larsen would agree that the Vikings were primarily raiders but argue that their raids were less destructive than the historians suggest.","Larsen would argue that the historians' portrayal is too narrow because it overlooks the Vikings' other activities.","Larsen would argue that Viking trade networks did not extend beyond Scandinavia."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Larsen says raiding was "only one part" of Viking activity and that focusing on it obscures trade, town-founding, and settlement.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Ruth Feldman argues that happiness is largely determined by genetics. Studies of identical twins raised apart, she notes, show that their levels of happiness are strikingly similar, suggesting that each person has a relatively fixed "set point."<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Diego Morales agrees that genes influence happiness. But his long-term studies show that major life events—such as long-term unemployment or disability—can shift people's happiness levels for years, and that some people never return to their former baseline. The set point, Morales argues, is less fixed than once believed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Feldman (Text 1) and Morales (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that happiness is completely fixed and cannot change over a lifetime.","Both would agree that genetics has some influence on how happy people are.","Both would agree that major life events have no lasting effect on happiness.","Both would agree that identical twins raised apart have very different levels of happiness."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Feldman emphasizes genetic influence, and Morales "agrees that genes influence happiness," though he thinks the set point is more changeable.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many conservationists support creating large marine protected areas where fishing is banned. Such areas, they argue, allow fish populations to recover, and fish that grow large and abundant inside protected zones eventually spill over into surrounding waters, benefiting fishers.<br><br>Text 2<br>Fisheries scientist Kwame Boateng notes that the benefits depend heavily on enforcement. In several protected areas he studied, illegal fishing was so common that fish populations inside the boundaries were no larger than those outside. Boateng supports protected areas but argues that they require patrols and monitoring to deliver the benefits their supporters expect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boateng (Text 2) most likely respond to the conservationists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Boateng would support the conservationists' goals but argue that protected areas succeed only when rules are enforced.","Boateng would argue that marine protected areas never allow fish populations to recover.","Boateng would argue that protected areas harm fishers because the fish that recover inside them rarely spill over into the surrounding waters.","Boateng would argue that fish inside protected areas never spill over into surrounding waters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Boateng "supports protected areas" but found that without enforcement they didn't help, so benefits depend on patrols and monitoring.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians attribute the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 CE mainly to invasions by Germanic peoples, whose armies overran Roman territory and eventually deposed the last emperor in the West.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Julia Marchetti argues that the invasions succeeded because the empire had already been weakened from within. Decades of civil wars, a declining tax base, and reliance on hired soldiers whose loyalty was uncertain, she contends, left the Western Empire unable to resist pressures it had withstood for centuries.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Marchetti (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Marchetti would argue that Germanic peoples never invaded Roman territory.","Marchetti would agree that the invasions were decisive but argue that they were carried out mainly by hired soldiers who had turned against Rome.","Marchetti would argue that the Western Roman Empire was stronger in 476 CE than ever before.","Marchetti would argue that the historians' explanation overlooks internal problems that made the invasions successful."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Marchetti argues the invasions succeeded because of internal weaknesses, which the invasion-focused explanation omits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the senator had publicly opposed the bill for months, her eventual vote in its favor was less a reversal than a ______ shift: she had been quietly negotiating amendments all along, and the final version incorporated nearly all of them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precipitous","calculated","grudging","capricious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Her vote resulted from long negotiation, so the shift was deliberate and planned: "calculated." "Capricious" means impulsive, and "precipitous" means sudden, both of which contradict the long negotiation. "Grudging" implies reluctance, but the text suggests she got what she wanted.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have called the novelist's prose ______, but her admirers argue that its spareness is deliberate: by withholding description, she forces readers to imagine the characters' surroundings for themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ornate","florid","austere","lyrical"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The admirers defend the prose's "spareness," so the critics must have described it with a word meaning severely plain: "austere." "Florid" and "ornate" mean elaborately decorated, and "lyrical" means expressive and musical. None of these describes spareness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's new director has been praised for making the collection more accessible, but some longtime staff members view her changes as ______: in their view, she has replaced careful scholarship with crowd-pleasing spectacle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["an imposition","a restoration","a dilution","an innovation"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The staff believe scholarship has been replaced with spectacle, a weakening of quality: "a dilution." "An innovation" is neutral or positive, "a restoration" implies returning to an earlier state, and "an imposition" stresses force rather than lowered quality.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Historian Ana Lucía Ortega argues that the treaty's significance has been ______ by later scholars, who read into it a commitment to self-governance that its eighteenth-century authors likely never intended.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismissed","inflated","obscured","documented"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Later scholars read more into the treaty than its authors intended, overstating its importance: "inflated." "Obscured" and "dismissed" suggest the significance was hidden or downplayed, the opposite. "Documented" is neutral.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The chef's approach to Peruvian cuisine is ______: she preserves the techniques her grandmother taught her while freely incorporating ingredients from Japan, Italy, and West Africa.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["insular","syncretic","derivative","orthodox"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Blending inherited techniques with ingredients from many cultures is "syncretic." "Orthodox" and "insular" imply strict tradition or isolation, and "derivative" implies unoriginal copying, an unfavorable judgment the text doesn't make.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Early reviews of the film were ______; some critics called it a masterpiece, while others dismissed it as self-indulgent, and few took a middle position.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["polarized","lukewarm","unanimous","tentative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reviews split sharply into opposite extremes with few in between, so they were "polarized." "Lukewarm" implies moderate reactions, "unanimous" implies agreement, and "tentative" implies hesitation, none of which fits strong opposing judgments.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her memoir, the physicist recalls that her early interest in science was not encouraged but merely ______ by her teachers, who neither discouraged her questions nor went out of their way to answer them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tolerated","applauded","fostered","stifled"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The teachers neither discouraged nor actively supported her, so they "tolerated" her interest. "Fostered" and "applauded" imply encouragement, which the text denies, and "stifled" implies discouragement, which it also denies.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new evidence does not overturn the prevailing theory about the bridge's collapse, but it does ______ it: investigators now believe the corrosion that was blamed for the failure was worsened by a design flaw in the supporting cables.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["complicate","refute","corroborate","supersede"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The evidence adds a contributing factor without overturning the theory, making it more complex: "complicate." "Refute" and "supersede" mean overturning or replacing, which the text denies, and "corroborate" means simply confirming, which ignores the added factor.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Kwame Osei found that residents of the neighborhood were ______ about the proposed redevelopment: they welcomed the promised jobs and new stores but feared that rising rents would eventually force them out.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sanguine","ambivalent","indifferent","adamant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Residents held both positive and negative feelings at once, so they were "ambivalent." "Indifferent" means not caring, "adamant" means firmly decided, and "sanguine" means optimistic, none of which captures the mix.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The composer's late works are notably ______; after decades of writing sprawling symphonies, she turned to short pieces for solo piano, some lasting less than a minute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["verbose","elaborate","concise","fragmentary"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Short pieces after sprawling ones are "concise." "Verbose" and "elaborate" suggest length or complexity. "Fragmentary" suggests incompleteness, not brevity by design, so it misdescribes finished short pieces.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some economists warn that the city's reliance on a single industry makes its economy ______: a downturn in tourism, they note, would leave thousands of residents without work and few other employers to turn to.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precarious","stagnant","robust","diversified"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Dependence on one industry leaves the economy insecure and at risk: "precarious." "Robust" and "diversified" contradict the warning, and "stagnant" means not growing, which isn't the concern described.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the long-standing rivalry between the two research groups, the conference organizers deliberately scheduled their presentations back to back, hoping a direct exchange of ideas would prove productive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acknowledge","exacerbate","sidestep","resolve"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The organizers chose confrontation over avoidance, so the rejected option is to "sidestep" the rivalry. "Exacerbate" would mean worsening it, which is closer to what they risked doing. "Acknowledge" and "resolve" don't contrast with putting the groups together.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the archaeologists' initial excavation turned up only a few pottery shards, the team remained ______, convinced that the site's location on an ancient trade route made more significant finds likely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["undeterred","complacent","indifferent","disillusioned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite meager results, the team kept its confidence and continued: "undeterred." "Disillusioned" means disappointed, "indifferent" means uninterested, and "complacent" implies smug inaction rather than determined persistence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The poet's language is deceptively ______: her poems use the vocabulary of everyday speech, yet a careful reading reveals layers of allusion to classical mythology and scripture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","convoluted","plain","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Deceptively" signals that the surface hides complexity, so the language seems simple: "plain." "Obscure," "archaic," and "convoluted" describe language that appears difficult, which contradicts "vocabulary of everyday speech."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Researchers had expected the drug to produce a modest improvement, so they were surprised when its effects proved ______: patients' symptoms eased within hours rather than weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["gradual","negligible","immediate","sporadic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Symptoms eased within hours, far faster than expected: "immediate." "Gradual" contradicts "hours rather than weeks," "negligible" contradicts the improvement, and "sporadic" implies irregularity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The director's adaptation is ______ to the novel in its plot, reproducing nearly every scene, yet it transforms the book's tone, turning a bleak tragedy into something closer to dark comedy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indifferent","faithful","antithetical","peripheral"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reproducing nearly every scene shows adherence to the source: "faithful." "Antithetical" means opposed, "indifferent" means unconcerned, and "peripheral" means marginal, none of which fits close adherence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Hye-jin Park's study of wild bonobos challenges the assumption that their peaceful reputation is ______; she observed frequent but brief conflicts that were quickly resolved through grooming and other reconciliation behaviors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unqualified","unfounded","exaggerated","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Park found that bonobos do fight but reconcile quickly, so their peacefulness is real but not absolute. She challenges the assumption that it is "unqualified." Challenging the idea that the reputation is "exaggerated" or "unfounded" would mean defending the reputation in full, which her observation of frequent conflicts doesn't do. "Recent" is unrelated to her findings.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many of the town's residents regard the annual festival as ______, an event so long established that few can imagine the summer without it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["novel","sacrosanct","ephemeral","superfluous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$An event so established that its absence is unimaginable is treated as untouchable: "sacrosanct." "Ephemeral" means short-lived, "superfluous" means unnecessary, and "novel" means new.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The committee's report was ______ in its criticism of the agency, cataloging dozens of specific failures and naming the officials responsible for each.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["perfunctory","conciliatory","oblique","unsparing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Listing many specific failures and naming officials shows harsh thoroughness: "unsparing." "Perfunctory" means superficial, "oblique" means indirect, and "conciliatory" means seeking to make peace.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Tomás Ferreira notes that the dialect's grammar, often dismissed as sloppy, is in fact remarkably ______, following rules for verb agreement as consistent as those of the standard language.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["systematic","idiosyncratic","fluid","rudimentary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Following consistent rules means the grammar is "systematic," which rebuts the charge of sloppiness. "Idiosyncratic" means peculiar to an individual, "rudimentary" means basic, and "fluid" implies flexibility rather than consistency.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Despite its ______ appearance, the deep-sea anglerfish is only a few centimeters long; photographs taken at close range make it look far larger and more threatening than it is.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diminutive","drab","innocuous","fearsome"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Photographs make the fish look "larger and more threatening," so its appearance is "fearsome." "Diminutive" and "innocuous" describe the reality, not the appearance, and "drab" concerns color.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two scientists' accounts of the discovery ______ on almost every detail, from the date of the key experiment to who first noticed the anomaly, a discrepancy that historians have never fully explained.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["elaborate","hinge","converge","diverge"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A "discrepancy" means the accounts differ, so they "diverge." "Converge" means agree, "elaborate" means add detail, and "hinge" means depend.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the playwright's dialogue often seems ______, each apparently aimless exchange later proves essential to the plot, planting details that become significant in the final act.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["terse","digressive","didactic","incisive"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Apparently aimless" exchanges seem to wander off topic: "digressive." "Incisive" and "terse" mean sharp or brief, and "didactic" means instructive, none of which fits "aimless."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The governor's proposal was ______ enough to win support from both parties: it raised the funding that one side wanted while leaving untouched the tax rates the other side was determined to protect.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["pragmatic","quixotic","doctrinaire","partisan"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A practical compromise designed to satisfy both sides is "pragmatic." "Partisan" and "doctrinaire" imply rigid loyalty to one side or ideology, and "quixotic" implies unrealistic idealism.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The botanist's field notes, written over four decades, are ______: they record not only the plants she collected but also weather, local names, the songs of nearby birds, and conversations with farmers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["encyclopedic","fragmentary","specialized","cursory"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Notes covering plants, weather, names, birds, and conversations are comprehensive: "encyclopedic." "Cursory" means hasty, "specialized" means narrowly focused, and "fragmentary" means incomplete.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some historians consider the general's retreat a ______ blunder, but others argue that it preserved his army to fight again and was therefore a shrewd decision.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["catastrophic","tactical","minor","calculated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast with "shrewd decision" requires a word suggesting a disastrous mistake: "catastrophic." "Calculated" implies deliberate planning, which supports the other view. "Tactical" is neutral, and "minor" understates a debate over its significance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In contrast to her outspoken predecessor, the new ambassador is known for her ______; she rarely gives interviews and chooses her public statements with great care.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["bravado","volubility","reticence","candor"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rarely speaking and choosing words carefully reflect "reticence." "Candor," "volubility," and "bravado" describe openness, talkativeness, or boldness, all closer to the outspoken predecessor.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The study's conclusions are ______ at best: its sample included only twenty-three participants, all of whom were recruited from a single university's psychology department.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unassailable","spurious","provisional","definitive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A tiny, unrepresentative sample makes the conclusions tentative and open to revision: "provisional." "Definitive" and "unassailable" contradict "at best," and "spurious" (false) goes further than the text, which questions the conclusions' strength rather than calling them false.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than simply cataloging the ruins, archaeologist Leila Haddad sought to ______ the daily routines of the people who lived there, using food residues, tool wear, and the layout of rooms to infer how households spent their time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["preserve","embellish","commemorate","reconstruct"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Inferring daily routines from evidence means building a picture of the past: "reconstruct." "Commemorate" means to honor, "embellish" means to add fictional detail, and "preserve" means to protect the physical ruins, which is closer to cataloging.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The memo's language was carefully ______, allowing the company to suggest that layoffs were possible without committing to any specific plan.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["explicit","inflammatory","equivocal","emphatic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Language that suggests without committing is deliberately ambiguous: "equivocal." "Emphatic" and "explicit" mean forceful or clear, and "inflammatory" means provocative.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Ines Calloway's proof, at just two pages, was praised for its ______; earlier attempts to prove the same result had run to more than fifty pages and relied on extensive computer calculations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscurity","rigor","elegance","ambition"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A short, clear proof replacing long, computation-heavy ones is praised for "elegance." "Rigor" refers to logical strictness, which the long proofs presumably also had, and "ambition" and "obscurity" don't match the contrast with length.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the hurricane caused extensive flooding, the city's newly built seawall ______ the worst of the damage, protecting the historic district that had been devastated by a similar storm decades earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["foreshadowed","exacerbated","mitigated","precipitated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The seawall reduced the severity of the damage: "mitigated." "Precipitated" and "exacerbated" mean caused or worsened, and "foreshadowed" means hinted at in advance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Hamid's essays are ______ in the best sense: they move easily from economics to poetry to personal memory, drawing connections that a specialist in any single field might miss.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["haphazard","eclectic","pedantic","esoteric"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ranging widely across fields "in the best sense" is "eclectic." "Haphazard" implies disorder, the bad sense of breadth. "Esoteric" means understood by few, and "pedantic" means fussily focused on minor details.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new regulations are intended to ______ the practice of overfishing rather than to eliminate it entirely; officials acknowledge that some boats will continue to exceed their quotas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["curb","eradicate","sanction","document"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reducing a practice without eliminating it is to "curb" it. "Eradicate" means to eliminate entirely, which the text rules out. "Sanction" is ambiguous (it can mean approve), and "document" means record.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Far from being ______ observers of their environment, honeybees actively modify it: workers fan their wings to cool the hive, and they collect plant resins to seal cracks against invading microbes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["meticulous","casual","vigilant","passive"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Far from" signals the opposite of "actively modify," so the word must be "passive." "Vigilant" and "meticulous" describe attentive observation, which doesn't contrast with action, and "casual" doesn't oppose "actively."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The architect's design ______ the building's historical facade while replacing its crumbling interior entirely, so that from the street the structure looks almost exactly as it did in 1890.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["renovates","retains","replicates","obscures"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The facade is kept while the interior is replaced: "retains." "Renovates" implies changing the facade, "obscures" means hiding it, and "replicates" means making a copy, which would replace the original.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The candidate's speech was strikingly ______, avoiding the bold promises of her rivals and instead emphasizing the limits of what any single official could accomplish.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","grandiose","evasive","strident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Avoiding bold promises and emphasizing limits reflects restraint: "measured." "Grandiose" and "strident" describe the opposite. "Evasive" implies avoiding questions, but she directly addresses limits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$While most of the expedition's members wrote ______ accounts of their journey, describing heroic struggles against the elements, the ship's cook kept a diary that dwells mainly on boredom, bad food, and petty arguments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["candid","romanticized","meticulous","mundane"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Accounts of "heroic struggles" that contrast with the cook's unglamorous diary are "romanticized." "Mundane" and "candid" describe the cook's diary, and "meticulous" doesn't capture the contrast in tone.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some scholars argue that the proverb's meaning has been ______ over centuries of use: what originally warned against greed is now commonly understood as advice about patience.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["codified","preserved","clarified","distorted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A meaning that shifted from a warning about greed to advice about patience has been altered or twisted: "distorted." "Clarified" and "preserved" imply the meaning was improved or kept, and "codified" means formally recorded.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Rosa Maldonado describes the relationship between the fig tree and its pollinating wasp as ______: neither species can reproduce without the other.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["parasitic","opportunistic","incidental","obligate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$When neither species can reproduce without the other, the relationship is required, or "obligate." "Opportunistic" and "incidental" imply the relationship is optional, and "parasitic" implies one species harms the other.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The trade negotiations, which had seemed close to collapse in March, were ______ in April when both sides agreed to drop their most contentious demands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["derailed","revived","stalled","concluded"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Talks near collapse that recovered after concessions were "revived." "Stalled" and "derailed" mean stopped, and "concluded" would require an agreement, not merely renewed progress.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's portrait of her father is notably ______: she describes his generosity and humor alongside his temper and his long absences, refusing to either idealize or condemn him.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sentimental","balanced","scathing","laudatory"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Presenting both strengths and flaws without idealizing or condemning is "balanced." "Laudatory" and "sentimental" lean toward idealizing, and "scathing" toward condemning.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The fossil record for early birds remains ______: crucial transitional forms are known from only a handful of specimens, and entire lineages are represented by single bones.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["contested","definitive","abundant","sparse"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Few specimens and single bones mean the record is thin: "sparse." "Abundant" and "definitive" contradict this, and "contested" refers to disagreement rather than scarcity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Initially ______ about the value of video games in classrooms, the teacher changed her mind after observing how a simulation game helped struggling students grasp the basics of supply and demand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indifferent","ignorant","skeptical","enthusiastic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$She "changed her mind" after seeing the game's benefits, so she must have doubted its value: "skeptical." "Enthusiastic" doesn't fit a change toward approval, "ignorant" implies she knew nothing, and "indifferent" implies she had no opinion to change.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The documentary is ______ in its use of archival footage, splicing clips from different decades together without labels, so that viewers may mistake a 1970s protest for one from the 1990s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cavalier","innovative","sparing","scrupulous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Splicing unlabeled clips so that viewers are misled shows careless disregard for accuracy: "cavalier." "Scrupulous" means the opposite, "sparing" means using little, and "innovative" is positive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Historians once viewed the trading post as ______ to the region's economy, a minor outpost of little importance, but new analysis of shipping records shows that it handled nearly a third of the colony's fur exports.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detrimental","marginal","indispensable","integral"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"A minor outpost of little importance" defines "marginal." "Integral" and "indispensable" describe the newer view, and "detrimental" means harmful.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novelist ______ the conventions of the detective story: the crime is never solved, the detective is barely mentioned after the first chapter, and the culprit's identity is irrelevant to the book's concerns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subverts","embraces","exemplifies","codifies"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Leaving the crime unsolved and sidelining the detective undermines genre expectations: "subverts." "Embraces," "exemplifies," and "codifies" all mean following or reinforcing the conventions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Engineers found that the old bridge's iron, far from being ______, had retained most of its original strength after 150 years, a durability they attributed to the high purity of the ore.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["malleable","degraded","pristine","reinforced"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Far from being" signals the opposite of retained strength, so the word means weakened: "degraded." "Pristine" and "reinforced" align with strength, and "malleable" refers to flexibility rather than weakening.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although she is often described as a ______ figure in the history of photography, Lena Vukovic in fact worked within a thriving circle of women photographers whose influence on her was considerable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["solitary","seminal","derivative","marginal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast is with working in a thriving circle that influenced her, so the common description must portray her as isolated: "solitary." "Seminal" means influential, which doesn't contrast with the circle. "Marginal" and "derivative" concern importance and originality, not isolation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the new vaccine requires only a single dose and no refrigeration, public health officials expect it to ______ immunization campaigns in remote regions, where maintaining cold storage has been a persistent obstacle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["complicate","supplant","facilitate","postpone"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Removing the refrigeration requirement makes campaigns easier: "facilitate." "Complicate" and "postpone" mean the opposite, and "supplant" means replace.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The council's decision to delay the vote was widely seen as ______; members hoped that by the time it was held, public anger over the proposal would have faded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unprecedented","principled","strategic","impulsive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Delaying so that anger fades is a calculated move: "strategic." "Impulsive" means unplanned, "principled" implies a moral motive, and "unprecedented" concerns novelty, not motive.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The translator's notes ______ the many ambiguities of the original text, explaining in each case why she chose one meaning over another rather than concealing the difficulty.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resolve","acknowledge","ignore","exaggerate"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$She explains her choices "rather than concealing the difficulty," so she openly admits the ambiguities: "acknowledge." "Resolve" would mean eliminating the ambiguities rather than discussing them, "ignore" is the opposite, and "exaggerate" adds an unsupported judgment.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Measurements from the probe were ______ with earlier predictions: the planet's magnetic field was almost exactly as strong as models had forecast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incompatible","tangential","contemporaneous","consistent"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Results that match predictions are "consistent with" them. "Incompatible" means the opposite, "tangential" means only loosely related, and "contemporaneous" means occurring at the same time.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers were careful not to ______ their results: although the treatment reduced symptoms in mice, they stressed that it had not yet been tested in humans and might not work the same way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["withhold","replicate","overstate","contextualize"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Stressing limitations shows care not to exaggerate: "overstate." "Replicate" means repeat, "withhold" means conceal, and "contextualize" describes what they actually did, not what they avoided.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Unlike her earlier novels, which unfold over decades, the author's latest book is ______ in scope, confined to a single afternoon in a single kitchen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expansive","ambiguous","episodic","circumscribed"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A book confined to one afternoon in one room has a narrowly limited scope: "circumscribed." "Expansive" describes the earlier novels, "episodic" means made up of loosely connected episodes, and "ambiguous" concerns meaning, not scope.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the council members disagreed sharply about the budget, their debate remained ______, with each side acknowledging the other's concerns and avoiding personal attacks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["civil","inconclusive","acrimonious","perfunctory"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Acknowledging concerns and avoiding personal attacks despite sharp disagreement makes the debate "civil." "Acrimonious" contradicts this, "perfunctory" means done without effort, and "inconclusive" concerns the outcome rather than the tone.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' range has ______ dramatically over the past century; once found across much of the continent, the frog now survives in only a few isolated mountain streams.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expanded","fluctuated","contracted","stabilized"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Going from much of a continent to a few streams means the range shrank: "contracted." "Fluctuated" implies going up and down, and "expanded" and "stabilized" contradict the decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Political theorist Jonah Adebayo argues that civic trust is ______: built slowly over generations, it can be destroyed by a single scandal and may take decades to recover.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragile","intangible","resilient","reciprocal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Something easily destroyed and slow to recover is "fragile." "Resilient" means the opposite. "Intangible" and "reciprocal" are true of trust in general but don't match the emphasis on easy destruction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The film's special effects, groundbreaking when it was released in 1977, now look ______ to modern audiences accustomed to computer-generated imagery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["innovative","seamless","dated","ominous"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Effects that were once groundbreaking but now compare poorly with modern imagery look "dated." "Innovative" and "seamless" describe how they once seemed, and "ominous" concerns mood.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's memoir is less a record of events than an act of ______: she consistently portrays her own decisions as wise and blames setbacks on colleagues.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-discovery","self-justification","self-effacement","self-criticism"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Portraying one's decisions as wise and blaming others is defending oneself: "self-justification." "Self-criticism" and "self-effacement" are the opposite, and "self-discovery" implies personal insight that the description doesn't suggest.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The chemist's discovery was ______: she had been searching for a better adhesive when she noticed that a failed batch formed an unusually strong, flexible film.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["serendipitous","derivative","methodical","inevitable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finding something valuable by accident while looking for something else is "serendipitous." "Methodical" and "inevitable" imply a planned or certain result, and "derivative" means unoriginal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript's author is unknown, scholars have tried to ______ its origins from indirect evidence: the type of parchment, the style of the handwriting, and the dialect in which it is written.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disregard","verify","fabricate","infer"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Drawing conclusions from indirect clues is to "infer." "Verify" means to confirm something already proposed, "fabricate" means to invent, and "disregard" means to ignore.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have described the singer's voice as ______, noting that it can shift in a single phrase from a fragile whisper to a full-throated roar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","versatile","restrained","strident"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A voice moving from whisper to roar has a wide range: "versatile." "Monotonous" means unvarying, "strident" means harsh, and "restrained" means held back.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some economists consider the region's rapid growth ______, warning that it depends on high commodity prices that could fall at any time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unsustainable","equitable","unprecedented","incremental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Growth dependent on volatile prices that could collapse is "unsustainable." "Unprecedented" concerns novelty, "equitable" concerns fairness, and "incremental" contradicts "rapid."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The play's humor is ______; its jokes depend on references to obscure eighteenth-century politics, and few modern audience members understand them without footnotes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["slapstick","topical","broad","recondite"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Humor that requires obscure knowledge is "recondite." "Broad" and "slapstick" describe accessible, physical humor. "Topical" means relevant to current events, which describes the jokes in their own time but not their present obscurity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's hypothesis was initially met with ______, but within a decade, accumulating evidence had persuaded most of her colleagues.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acclaim","indifference","hostility","incredulity"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with later being "persuaded" indicates initial disbelief: "incredulity." "Acclaim" doesn't need persuasion, "indifference" suggests no reaction, and "hostility" implies anger rather than doubt about the evidence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The settlement's layout suggests that its founders ______ defense over convenience: houses were clustered tightly on a hilltop far from the river that supplied their water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["neglected","sacrificed","reconciled","prioritized"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Choosing a defensible hilltop despite the distance from water means valuing defense more: "prioritized." "Sacrificed" and "neglected" reverse the relationship, and "reconciled" implies balancing the two.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's exhibition ______ the conventional narrative of the city's founding, giving equal space to the Indigenous communities whose land the settlers occupied.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refutes","simplifies","reiterates","broadens"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Adding Indigenous perspectives to the founding story widens it: "broadens." "Reiterates" means repeats, "simplifies" means reduces, and "refutes" means disproves, which overstates adding perspectives.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The coach's strategy was ______: rather than relying on a few star players, she rotated the entire roster so that no one would be exhausted by the final minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["hierarchical","egalitarian","improvised","conservative"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Giving all players roughly equal time rather than relying on stars is "egalitarian." "Hierarchical" is the opposite. "Improvised" means unplanned, and "conservative" means cautious.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the two languages share many words, their grammars are ______; one marks tense with verb endings, the other with separate particles placed at the end of the sentence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["interchangeable","divergent","rudimentary","analogous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Grammars that mark tense in completely different ways are "divergent." "Analogous" and "interchangeable" imply similarity, and "rudimentary" means basic.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists had long considered the bird ______ to the island, but genetic analysis revealed that its closest relatives live on the mainland, two hundred kilometers away, and that the two populations still occasionally interbreed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dormant","invasive","migratory","endemic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The surprise is that the bird has close, interbreeding mainland relatives, which contradicts the belief that it was unique to the island: "endemic." "Invasive" implies harmful introduction, "dormant" means inactive, and "migratory" wouldn't be contradicted by mainland relatives.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The editor praised the young journalist's reporting as ______, noting that every claim in her articles was supported by documents or on-the-record sources.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["speculative","sensational","partisan","scrupulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Supporting every claim with evidence shows careful accuracy: "scrupulous." "Sensational" and "speculative" imply exaggeration or guesswork, and "partisan" implies bias.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The city's housing shortage is ______, the result of decades of restrictive zoning rather than a sudden surge in population.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["transient","unforeseen","exaggerated","entrenched"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A problem built up over decades is deeply established: "entrenched." "Transient" means short-lived, "unforeseen" means unexpected, and "exaggerated" questions whether the shortage is real.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The memoirist's tone toward her younger self is gently ______: she recounts her teenage certainties with affection but makes clear how little she then understood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detached","reverent","bitter","ironic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Presenting her past certainties with affection while showing how naive they were creates gentle irony. "Reverent" implies admiration, "bitter" implies resentment, and "detached" implies a lack of the affection described.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the sculptor's reputation, the forgery scandal, in an odd twist, increased interest in her genuine works and drove up their prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diminishing","establishing","defending","enhancing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Rather than" sets up a contrast with the increased interest, so the expected effect would have been to damage her reputation: "diminishing." "Enhancing" describes what actually happened, and "establishing" and "defending" don't form the contrast.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The policy's effects have been ______: wealthy suburbs have benefited from lower taxes, while poorer neighborhoods have seen their schools lose funding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uneven","uniform","temporary","negligible"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Benefits for some areas and losses for others mean the effects are "uneven." "Uniform" is the opposite, "negligible" means minor, and "temporary" concerns duration.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Physicist Ayo Bello argues that the experiment's surprising result is likely an ______, caused by a faulty sensor, rather than evidence of new physics.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["axiom","artifact","innovation","anomaly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In science, an "artifact" is a false result introduced by equipment or method, which matches "caused by a faulty sensor." An "anomaly" is any unexpected result, which could still be evidence of new physics, so it doesn't capture Bello's point. "Axiom" and "innovation" don't fit.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The festival's organizers took a ______ approach to programming, booking only performers who had appeared at previous festivals and avoiding any experimental acts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inclusive","cautious","daring","haphazard"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Booking only familiar performers and avoiding experiments is "cautious." "Daring" is the opposite, "haphazard" implies a lack of planning, and "inclusive" contradicts the exclusion of new acts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as ______, the desert supports a surprising diversity of life, including more than two hundred species of birds and dozens of flowering plants adapted to bloom after rare rains.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fertile","temperate","barren","pristine"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast with "surprising diversity of life" requires a word meaning lifeless: "barren." "Fertile" would not contrast, "temperate" concerns climate, and "pristine" means unspoiled.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The biographer's account is ______ by her close friendship with her subject; she omits the composer's well-documented feuds and portrays him as universally beloved.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["enriched","validated","compromised","complicated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Omitting unflattering facts because of friendship damages the account's reliability: "compromised." "Enriched" and "validated" are positive, and "complicated" doesn't convey the loss of reliability.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, ecologists assumed that the decline of an Arctic seabird, the ivory gull, was driven mainly by shrinking sea ice. <u>Yet when biologist Freya Nilsen analyzed eggs collected over forty years, she found mercury concentrations rising steadily, reaching levels known to impair reproduction in other bird species.</u> Nilsen does not dismiss the role of sea ice, but she argues that pollution may be an equally important and long-overlooked factor.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to propose an additional explanation for the gull's decline.","It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to reject the view that sea ice loss has harmed the gull.","It describes a method that ecologists had long used to measure the effects of sea ice loss.","It provides an example of a species whose reproduction is known to be impaired by mercury."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mercury evidence leads Nilsen to argue that pollution is another important factor. The choice beginning "It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to reject…" is tempting, but the text says she does "not dismiss the role of sea ice," so she adds an explanation rather than rejecting one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1913 novel. The narrator describes a character returning to her childhood home.<br><br>The house was smaller than she remembered, as houses always are. The porch sagged a little toward the east, and the lilac her mother had planted had grown wild, its branches pressing against the parlor window. <u>Yet when she stepped inside, the smell of the place—wax, woodsmoke, and something faintly sweet she could never name—undid all her composure at once.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the character is disappointed by how much the house has deteriorated since her childhood.","It shows that a sensory detail affects the character more powerfully than the changes she has observed.","It explains why the character remembers the house as being larger than it actually is.","It reveals that the character has never been able to identify the source of the house's distinctive smell."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After a calm inventory of visible changes, "Yet" introduces the smell, which overwhelms her ("undid all her composure"). The choice beginning "It reveals that the…" picks out a minor detail rather than the sentence's function, and the choice beginning "It suggests that the…" misreads her emotional response as disappointment.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that the first maps of the world were produced to help travelers find their way. Historian Paulo Mendes points out, however, that some of the oldest surviving maps, such as a Babylonian clay tablet from around 600 BCE, show places that no traveler could have reached, including mythical islands beyond a surrounding ocean. Such maps, Mendes argues, were less practical guides than statements about how their makers understood the cosmos and their own place within it.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a historian's argument about ancient maps, raises an objection to that argument, and then explains why the objection ultimately fails.","It describes a historical artifact, explains how it was made, and then compares it with later maps.","It presents a common assumption, offers evidence that complicates it, and then describes an alternative interpretation.","It traces the development of mapmaking from ancient Babylon to the present day."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text opens with the assumption that maps guided travelers, gives the Babylonian tablet as counterevidence, and ends with Mendes's alternative view. It raises no objection to Mendes, and it doesn't explain how the tablet was made or trace history to the present.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying urban birds have found that great tits in cities sing at higher pitches than those in forests. <u>Low-frequency traffic noise, the researchers note, tends to mask lower-pitched songs.</u> Birds that sing higher may therefore be better able to communicate with potential mates and rivals in noisy environments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that urban birds have difficulty attracting mates.","It offers an alternative explanation for the observation that city birds sing at higher pitches than forest birds.","It describes a problem that the researchers expected but did not observe in their study of urban birds.","It provides information that helps explain why city birds might benefit from singing at higher pitches."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The fact about traffic noise sets up the explanation in the final sentence: higher songs avoid being masked. It doesn't offer an alternative explanation or describe an unobserved problem, and the text doesn't claim urban birds have difficulty attracting mates.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1854, physician John Snow mapped cholera deaths in a London neighborhood and noticed that they clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. Most experts at the time believed cholera spread through foul air. <u>Snow persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle, and new cases in the area soon declined.</u> His work is now considered a founding moment in epidemiology, though some historians note that the outbreak was already subsiding when the handle was removed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why most experts of the time believed that cholera spread through foul air.","It describes an action and outcome that seemed to support Snow's view, which the final sentence then qualifies.","It describes the method Snow used to map the cholera deaths in the London neighborhood.","It provides conclusive proof that cholera was spread through contaminated water rather than foul air, settling the debate described earlier."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence reports the intervention and the decline in cases, which appear to confirm Snow's theory. The final sentence then notes the outbreak was already subsiding. The choice beginning "It provides conclusive proof…" is tempting but ignores that qualification, so "conclusive" overstates the case.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1908 essay.<br><br>The city dweller tells himself he is too busy to look at the sky. He crosses the square each morning with his eyes on the pavement, calculating the day's affairs. But the sky is there all the same, doing extraordinary things above his hat: clouds assembling and dissolving, light shifting from pearl to brass. He need only lift his head.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To encourage readers to notice the beauty that is available to them even in the midst of busy lives","To compare the skies above cities with those above the countryside","To argue that city life prevents people from appreciating the natural world","To describe the daily routine of a typical city dweller in the early twentieth century, from his morning walk to his evening return"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay contrasts the busy man's downward gaze with the remarkable sky and ends with "He need only lift his head," urging attention. The choice beginning "To argue that city…" overstates: the text says the beauty is available, not that city life prevents seeing it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Sara Lindqvist studied towns that received new public libraries in the early twentieth century, funded by philanthropist Andrew Carnegie. Comparing them with similar towns whose library applications were rejected, she found that the library towns later produced more patents per resident. <u>Lindqvist acknowledges that towns eager enough to apply may have differed in other ways, but she notes that rejected towns had also applied, so eagerness alone cannot explain the gap.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It introduces a second finding of Lindqvist's study about the towns that applied for Carnegie libraries.","It explains why Carnegie chose to fund libraries in some towns but not in others.","It concedes that Lindqvist's findings may be explained by differences among towns that she did not measure.","It anticipates a possible objection to Lindqvist's conclusion and explains why her study design addresses it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence raises the objection that eager towns might differ, then explains that comparing with rejected applicants rules this out. The choice beginning "It concedes that Lindqvist's…" captures only the concession and misses the rebuttal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Octavia Mendes's novel <i>The Salt Road</i> follows three generations of a family of salt harvesters on the coast of Portugal. <u>Mendes devotes long passages to the technical details of the work—raking the crystals, timing the tides, reading the wind—sometimes for several pages at a time.</u> Some reviewers found these passages tedious, but others argued that they convey, as no summary could, how completely the work shaped the characters' lives.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Mendes chose to write about salt harvesters.","It summarizes the plot of the novel's first section.","It provides evidence that the novel's account of salt harvesting is historically inaccurate.","It describes a feature of the novel about which reviewers disagreed."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence identifies the long technical passages, which the final sentence shows reviewers judged differently. It doesn't summarize plot, address accuracy, or explain her motives.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most volcanoes form along the boundaries of tectonic plates, where plates collide or pull apart. The Hawaiian Islands, however, sit in the middle of the Pacific Plate, thousands of kilometers from any boundary. Geologists explain them by a "hot spot": a plume of hot material rising from deep in Earth's mantle. As the Pacific Plate moves slowly northwest over this stationary plume, new volcanoes form, and older ones are carried away and become extinct.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare the Hawaiian Islands with volcanoes that form at plate boundaries","To explain how a group of volcanoes formed in a location where volcanoes would not typically be expected","To describe the process by which volcanoes on the Hawaiian Islands become extinct as they are carried away from the hot spot","To argue that most volcanoes form along the boundaries of tectonic plates"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text sets up Hawaii as an exception and explains its formation by the hot spot. The general rule is background, not an argument, and extinction is only one part of the explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1920 short story. Mrs. Halloran has just received a letter.<br><br>She read it twice, then folded it into precise thirds and set it on the mantel beside the clock. She wound the clock, though it did not need winding. She straightened the photograph of her son in his uniform, wiped a speck of dust from the glass, and then, very carefully, sat down.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the text's focus on Mrs. Halloran's actions?$q$, $q$["It conveys her emotional response to the letter indirectly through deliberate, controlled behavior.","It suggests that she is preparing her house for her son's return.","It emphasizes her habitual concern with cleanliness and order.","It shows that she is indifferent to the news contained in the letter and is occupied instead with routine household chores."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her unnecessary, meticulous actions, such as winding a clock that doesn't need it, hint at a strong emotion she is holding in check. The choice beginning "It emphasizes her habitual…" reads the actions literally and misses their emotional significance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Psychologists once thought that infants were born with little ability to perceive the world, experiencing what William James called a "blooming, buzzing confusion." Experiments since the 1960s have overturned this view. <u>By measuring how long infants look at different images, researchers have shown that newborns prefer faces to other patterns and can distinguish their mother's voice from a stranger's within days of birth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a limitation of the experiments conducted since the 1960s.","It explains why William James believed that newborn infants experience the world as a \"blooming, buzzing confusion.\"","It suggests that newborns prefer their mother's voice to her face.","It offers examples of findings that challenged an earlier view of infant perception."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence gives specific findings that overturned the "blooming, buzzing confusion" view. It doesn't explain James's reasoning, identify a limitation, or compare voice with face.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Botanist Lina Okoro has been studying the Wollemi pine, a tree known from fossils and thought extinct until a small grove was discovered in an Australian canyon in 1994. Fewer than one hundred wild trees survive. <u>Because nearly all of them are genetically identical, a single disease could wipe out the entire wild population.</u> To guard against this, Okoro and her colleagues have propagated thousands of seedlings and distributed them to botanical gardens around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the Wollemi pine was thought to be extinct until 1994.","It suggests that the seedlings distributed to botanical gardens around the world are more genetically diverse than wild trees.","It identifies a risk that motivates the conservation strategy described in the final sentence.","It describes a disease that has already damaged the wild population of Wollemi pines."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The genetic uniformity creates a vulnerability that the seedling program addresses. The sentence doesn't explain the belief in extinction or claim diversity among seedlings, and it describes a hypothetical, not actual, disease.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Literary critics often describe poet Langley Ross as a nature poet, citing her many poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons. Scholar Imani Brooks argues that this label is misleading. In Ross's poems, Brooks observes, natural images almost always serve as metaphors for human relationships: a frozen river stands for a strained marriage, a migrating flock for children leaving home. For Ross, Brooks concludes, nature was less a subject than a language.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why literary critics have described Ross as a nature poet","To describe several of Ross's poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons","To present a scholar's argument that a common characterization of a poet overlooks how she used natural imagery","To argue that Ross's poems about human relationships are more successful than her poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents Brooks's view that the "nature poet" label misses Ross's metaphorical use of nature. It makes no judgment about which poems are more successful, and the critics' reasoning and the poem examples serve Brooks's argument.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 2015, a team of engineers set out to build a bridge in rural Rwanda using only local materials and labor. <u>The nearest cement supplier was a two-day drive away, and the community had no access to heavy machinery.</u> The engineers turned to a design based on traditional suspension bridges, using steel cables salvaged from old mining equipment and timber cut from nearby forests. The bridge, completed in four months, now carries hundreds of people across the river each day.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes constraints that help explain the engineers' choice of design and materials.","It provides evidence that traditional suspension bridges are stronger than modern bridges.","It suggests that the bridge took longer to complete than the engineers expected.","It explains why the engineers decided to build a bridge in rural Rwanda."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lack of cement and machinery explains the turn to a traditional design with local materials. The sentence doesn't explain why they built a bridge at all, and the text gives no information about expected timelines or comparative strength.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many studies have found that people who own dogs live longer, on average, than people who do not. Some researchers have concluded that dog ownership improves health, perhaps by encouraging exercise. Epidemiologist Rafael Duarte urges caution. People who are already healthy and active, he notes, may be more likely to get dogs in the first place, which would produce the same pattern even if dogs had no effect on health at all.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a researcher's claim and then offers several pieces of evidence that support it.","It presents a hypothesis about dogs and health, describes an experiment designed to test that hypothesis, and then reports the results.","It presents a finding and a conclusion drawn from it, then describes a researcher's concern about that conclusion.","It describes two competing studies and explains why one is more reliable than the other."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text gives a correlation, a causal interpretation, and Duarte's concern about reverse causation. No experiment is described, and there are no competing studies or supporting evidence for a claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1913 novel set on the American prairie.<br><br>The shabby little town looked as if it had been set down on the prairie by accident. <u>None of the houses seemed to belong there; they huddled together as if each had been blown in by the same wind and was waiting for another to carry it away.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how the town's founders chose its location on the prairie.","It suggests that the town was recently damaged by a powerful storm.","It extends the opening sentence's portrayal of the town as impermanent and out of place.","It introduces a contrast between the town's appearance and the prosperity of its residents."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The image of houses blown in and waiting to be carried away develops the opening sentence's idea that the town was "set down ... by accident." The wind is figurative, not a literal storm, and nothing in the text concerns the residents' prosperity or the founders' choices.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Plastic recycling rates in many countries remain below 10 percent. Chemist Ji-woo Han argues that the problem is partly chemical: <u>most plastics are made of long polymer chains that degrade each time they are melted and reshaped, so recycled plastic is usually weaker than new plastic.</u> Han's lab is developing polymers that can be broken down into their original building blocks and reassembled without any loss of quality.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the results of an experiment conducted in Han's lab.","It suggests that recycled plastic is unsuitable for any practical use.","It offers a reason that recycling rates are expected to rise in the coming years.","It explains a limitation of current plastics that Han's research aims to overcome."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The degradation of polymer chains is the chemical problem Han's new polymers address. The text doesn't predict rising rates or describe experimental results, and "unsuitable for any practical use" overstates "usually weaker."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Mexican painter Remedios Varo arrived in Mexico City in 1941 as a refugee from war in Europe, she supported herself by designing advertisements and decorating furniture. <u>Only in the 1950s, after more than a decade of such work, was she able to devote herself fully to painting.</u> The intricate, dreamlike canvases she produced in the final decade of her life are now considered among the most important works of Surrealism.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It establishes that the works for which Varo is now celebrated were produced relatively late in her life.","It suggests that the years Varo spent designing advertisements and decorating furniture shaped the style of her later paintings.","It indicates that Varo's paintings were unpopular during her lifetime.","It explains why Varo left Europe for Mexico City in 1941."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By noting that full-time painting came only in the 1950s, the sentence prepares for the point that her major works came in her final decade. The text doesn't connect her commercial work to her style or describe her paintings' popularity during her life.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologists have long been puzzled by the yawn. It appears in nearly all vertebrates, from fish to humans, yet its function remains unclear. One hypothesis holds that yawning increases oxygen intake, but experiments in which people breathed air with extra oxygen found no change in how often they yawned. <u>A more recent hypothesis proposes that yawning cools the brain: studies have found that people yawn more when the air is cooler than their body temperature and less when it is hotter.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why yawning appears in nearly all vertebrates.","It provides additional evidence against the hypothesis that yawning increases oxygen intake.","It offers a conclusive answer to the puzzle introduced in the first sentence.","It presents an alternative explanation along with evidence that is consistent with it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After rejecting the oxygen hypothesis, the text offers the brain-cooling hypothesis with supporting temperature data. The sentence doesn't address oxygen, and "conclusive" overstates a hypothesis the text calls "more recent," not proven.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In her history of the American lunch counter, Dana Whitfield traces how these inexpensive restaurants spread through department stores and drugstores in the early twentieth century, serving shoppers and workers who wanted a quick meal. <u>Whitfield then turns to the 1960 sit-ins in Greensboro, North Carolina, where Black students sat at a whites-only Woolworth's counter and refused to leave until they were served.</u> The ordinary lunch counter, she argues, became a powerful symbol precisely because it was so familiar.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why lunch counters became less popular after the early twentieth century.","It describes Whitfield's personal involvement in the civil rights movement.","It provides an example of how lunch counters spread through department stores and drugstores.","It introduces an event that shows how an everyday institution took on broader significance."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sit-ins illustrate how a familiar, ordinary place became symbolically powerful, as the final sentence explains. The sentence isn't an example of the counters' spread, and nothing indicates their decline or Whitfield's personal involvement.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For years, the standard advice for preventing peanut allergies in children was to avoid giving them peanuts until age three. <u>A 2015 clinical trial, however, found that infants who regularly ate peanut-containing foods starting in their first year were about 80 percent less likely to develop an allergy than infants who avoided peanuts.</u> Health agencies soon revised their guidelines to recommend early introduction for most infants.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a finding that health agencies initially rejected because of the trial's small size.","It presents a finding that contradicted earlier advice and prompted a change in official recommendations.","It explains why some infants are more likely than others to develop peanut allergies.","It describes a finding that confirmed the earlier advice to delay feeding peanuts to children until they reach the age of three."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The trial's result ran counter to the avoidance advice and led agencies to change their guidelines. It didn't confirm the old advice, it doesn't explain individual susceptibility, and the text says nothing about agencies rejecting it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1921 novel. The narrator is a young clerk.<br><br>Mr. Pell never raised his voice. He did not need to. When he was displeased, he would simply set down his pen, remove his spectacles, and polish them slowly with a white handkerchief, and the whole office would fall silent, every clerk bent over his ledger as though his life depended on a column of figures.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the daily tasks performed by clerks in an office in the early twentieth century","To explain why Mr. Pell has become displeased with the work of the narrator and his colleagues","To portray a character whose authority is expressed through quiet gestures rather than open anger","To suggest that the clerks in the office secretly mock Mr. Pell's habits and mannerisms"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text shows that Mr. Pell commands the office through small, deliberate actions instead of shouting. It describes his effect, not the clerks' tasks, and nothing suggests mockery or explains a particular cause of his displeasure.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Urban planner Mei Lin studied two neighborhoods with similar populations and incomes. One had wide streets designed for cars; the other had narrow streets lined with shops and small parks. <u>Residents of the second neighborhood walked an average of 40 minutes more per week and reported knowing more of their neighbors by name.</u> Lin argues that street design can shape not only how people travel but also how connected they feel to their communities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how Lin selected the two neighborhoods to compare in her study.","It presents findings that Lin uses as the basis for a broader claim about street design.","It suggests that residents of the neighborhood with wide streets were less healthy overall than residents of the other neighborhood.","It offers a limitation that weakens Lin's conclusion about street design."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The walking and neighbor-knowledge findings support Lin's claim in the final sentence. The selection criteria come in the first part of the text, nothing in the sentence weakens her conclusion, and health isn't discussed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians have long debated why the Indus Valley civilization, which flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BCE, declined. Earlier theories blamed invasion or a sudden catastrophe. More recent research points to a gradual weakening of the monsoon, which would have reduced the river flooding that farmers depended on. <u>Sediment cores from lakes in the region show a drying trend beginning around 2200 BCE, consistent with this explanation.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why earlier historians believed that invasion or a sudden catastrophe had caused the civilization's decline around 2200 BCE.","It offers physical evidence that supports the more recent explanation for the civilization's decline.","It presents evidence that a sudden catastrophe destroyed the civilization around 2200 BCE.","It indicates that the civilization was most prosperous around 2200 BCE, before declining."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sediment cores show gradual drying, which supports the monsoon hypothesis. A "trend" is gradual, not a sudden catastrophe, and the sentence says nothing about invasion theories or peak prosperity.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many readers know Mary Seacole only as a nurse who cared for British soldiers during the Crimean War. Her 1857 autobiography reveals a more varied life. Born in Jamaica, Seacole ran a hotel in Panama, traveled widely in the Caribbean, and learned herbal medicine from her mother. In Crimea, she operated not a hospital but a combined store and restaurant, from which she provided care to soldiers on the side.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare Seacole's work during the Crimean War with that of other nurses","To show that Seacole's life was more complex than the familiar image of her suggests","To describe the herbal remedies that Seacole learned from her mother in Jamaica","To argue that Seacole's contributions as a nurse have been greatly exaggerated by historians"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text contrasts the narrow popular image with the broader life described in her autobiography. It doesn't claim her nursing was exaggerated, herbal medicine is only one detail, and no other nurses are discussed.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some deep-sea fish produce red light, an unusual ability because most deep-sea creatures cannot see red wavelengths. <u>The stoplight loosejaw, for example, emits red light from organs beneath its eyes, allowing it to illuminate prey that cannot see the light and therefore does not know it is being watched.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a fish that is an exception to the pattern of red light production.","It explains why most deep-sea creatures are unable to see red wavelengths of light.","It gives an example of how a fish benefits from the unusual ability described in the first sentence.","It suggests that the stoplight loosejaw is the only deep-sea fish that can produce red light, which makes it unusual among predators."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The loosejaw illustrates red light production and shows its advantage: hidden illumination of prey. The sentence doesn't explain why others can't see red, the fish isn't an exception, and "for example" implies other species exist.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Composer Hiroshi Tanabe's early works were dense and dissonant, admired by critics but rarely performed. In his forties, he began incorporating folk melodies from his native Hokkaido into his music. <u>Audiences responded enthusiastically, and his later symphonies became staples of orchestral programs in Japan.</u> Some critics, however, complained that Tanabe had sacrificed his originality for popularity.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the public reception of Tanabe's change in style, which the final sentence contrasts with a critical response.","It suggests that Tanabe's early works were more original than his later symphonies.","It provides evidence that critics admired Tanabe's later works more than his early ones.","It explains why Tanabe began incorporating folk melodies from his native Hokkaido into his music after years of writing dissonant works."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence reports audiences' enthusiasm for the new style, which contrasts with some critics' complaint. It doesn't explain his motive, and it concerns audiences, not critics. The final judgment about originality belongs to the critics, not this sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Astronomer Nadia Osei searches for planets orbiting other stars by watching for tiny dips in a star's brightness, which can occur when a planet passes in front of it. <u>The method has a built-in bias: it detects only planets whose orbits happen to be aligned edge-on with Earth, so most planetary systems are invisible to it.</u> Osei and her colleagues correct for this bias statistically when estimating how common planets are.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how planets passing in front of their stars cause the tiny dips in brightness that Osei and her colleagues watch for.","It suggests that Osei's estimates of how common planets are cannot be trusted.","It identifies a shortcoming of the method that the final sentence explains how researchers address.","It describes an alternative method that Osei uses to detect planets."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence names the alignment bias, and the final sentence says how Osei corrects for it. The choice beginning "It suggests that Osei's…" is tempting but ignores that correction, and the sentence describes no alternative method.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1905 novel. Lily is attending a party.<br><br>Lily laughed at the right moments and said the expected things, but her mind was elsewhere. She had learned long ago that a smile could be worn like a garment, put on in the morning and taken off at night, and that no one at such gatherings ever looked closely enough to see the seams.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the comparison of a smile to a garment?$q$, $q$["It indicates that Lily enjoys the party more than she expected she would.","It emphasizes that Lily is dressed more elegantly than the other guests at the party.","It reveals that the other guests at the party are also concealing their true feelings behind smiles worn like garments.","It suggests that Lily's cheerfulness is a deliberate performance that others fail to recognize."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A smile "worn like a garment" with hidden "seams" depicts her cheerfulness as a costume no one sees through. The comparison isn't literal, and it doesn't say anything about other guests concealing their feelings.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Andrés Villa interviewed 120 people who had moved from rural villages to Mexico City. Many described their first months as lonely and disorienting. <u>Yet nearly all reported that within two years they had built close networks of friends, often through churches, sports leagues, or people from their home regions.</u> Villa argues that cities, often portrayed as isolating, can foster strong communities for newcomers who find the right institutions.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why migrants from rural villages often experience loneliness during their first months in the city.","It presents a finding that complicates the initial impression of migrants' experience and supports Villa's argument.","It suggests that most migrants to Mexico City eventually return to their home regions.","It provides evidence that cities are generally more isolating than rural villages."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Yet" turns from early loneliness to later community, which supports Villa's claim that cities can foster community. The sentence doesn't explain loneliness or mention returning home, and the choice beginning "It provides evidence that…" reverses Villa's argument.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The modern bicycle seems simple, but its design took decades to develop. Early versions in the 1860s, nicknamed "boneshakers," had iron-rimmed wooden wheels and pedals attached directly to the front wheel. To go faster, makers enlarged the front wheel, producing the towering "high-wheeler" of the 1870s, which was fast but prone to dangerous falls. Only in the 1880s did the "safety bicycle," with equal-sized wheels and a chain drive, combine speed with stability.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a problem with the modern bicycle and then proposes a solution to that problem.","It makes a general claim about the bicycle and then traces a series of designs that illustrate it.","It compares two bicycle designs and argues that the earlier one was better than the later one.","It explains how bicycles are manufactured today and then describes how they are maintained."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text claims the design took decades and then traces the boneshaker, high-wheeler, and safety bicycle. It proposes no solution, covers three designs without preferring the earlier ones, and doesn't discuss manufacturing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologists studying sea turtles have found that the temperature of the sand in which eggs incubate determines the hatchlings' sex: warmer sand produces more females. <u>On some beaches in northern Australia, more than 99 percent of young green turtles are now female.</u> Researchers worry that if temperatures continue to rise, some populations may eventually lack enough males to reproduce.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the population of green turtles in northern Australia is declining rapidly.","It explains why sea turtles in northern Australia prefer to lay their eggs on warmer beaches.","It provides a striking example of the effect described in the first sentence, which sets up the concern in the final sentence.","It presents evidence that sand temperature has little effect on whether sea turtle hatchlings are male or female, contrary to earlier studies."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The extreme female ratio illustrates temperature-dependent sex determination and leads to worries about too few males. It supports, not undercuts, the effect, and the text discusses neither nesting preferences nor current population decline.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Clara Weiss studies whistled languages, in which speakers convey words by whistling rather than speaking. These languages arise in places with rugged terrain, such as mountain valleys, where a whistle carries much farther than a shout. <u>Silbo Gomero, used on the Canary Island of La Gomera, can be understood at distances of up to five kilometers.</u> Weiss argues that whistled languages show how flexibly human language adapts to its environment.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It offers a specific example that illustrates the advantage of whistled languages described in the previous sentence.","It describes a whistled language that differs from the others discussed in the text in being used mainly on flat, open terrain.","It presents evidence that whistled languages are more expressive than spoken languages.","It explains how speakers of whistled languages learn to produce words by whistling."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Silbo Gomero's five-kilometer range illustrates how whistles carry farther than shouts. The sentence doesn't address learning or expressiveness, and La Gomera is mountainous, consistent with the rugged-terrain pattern.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the painter Alma Thomas retired from teaching in 1960, at age sixty-eight, she had exhibited only occasionally. Over the next decade, she developed a distinctive style of bright, mosaic-like brushstrokes. In 1972, she became the first Black woman to have a solo exhibition at the Whitney Museum of American Art. Her late flowering, critics have noted, challenges the common assumption that artists do their most important work when young.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that artists who begin painting seriously late in life generally produce better and more original work than younger artists","To present an artist's career as a counterexample to a widely held assumption about artistic achievement","To describe the technique that Alma Thomas used to create her mosaic-like brushstrokes","To explain why Alma Thomas exhibited her paintings only occasionally while she was teaching"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text traces Thomas's late success and ends by noting that it challenges the idea that artists peak young. It doesn't explain her technique or her early infrequent exhibiting, and it doesn't claim late starters are generally better.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most leaves are green because they contain chlorophyll, which absorbs red and blue light and reflects green. <u>In autumn, deciduous trees stop producing chlorophyll, and as it breaks down, yellow and orange pigments that were present all along become visible.</u> Red colors, by contrast, come from pigments that many trees produce only in autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light but reflects green light.","It describes a process that causes leaves to produce yellow and orange pigments in autumn.","It explains the source of some autumn colors, setting up a contrast with the source of red.","It suggests that red pigments are present in leaves throughout the year."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence explains that yellow and orange were hidden all along, and "by contrast" introduces red pigments made only in autumn. The choice beginning "It describes a process…" misreads it: the yellow pigments are revealed, not produced, in autumn. The choice beginning "It suggests that red…" reverses the text.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have found that children who are read to frequently develop larger vocabularies than those who are not. <u>Psychologist Leah Grant points out, however, that the books parents choose may matter as much as how often they read: picture books contain, on average, more rare words than everyday conversation does.</u> Grant suggests that parents who simply talk more with their children may not provide the same benefits as reading together.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that the frequency with which parents read to their children has no effect on the size of children's vocabularies.","It describes the method Grant used to measure the size of children's vocabularies.","It introduces a qualification about what makes reading beneficial, which leads to Grant's suggestion in the final sentence.","It explains why parents tend to choose picture books rather than other kinds of books."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grant adds that book content matters, specifically rare words in picture books, which explains her final suggestion about talk versus reading. She says book choice matters "as much as" frequency, not that frequency has no effect.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1937 novel.<br><br>Grandmother kept the farm's accounts in a black notebook that no one else was permitted to touch. Every evening she sat at the kitchen table and wrote in it by lamplight, her lips moving silently. When she died, my father opened the notebook at last. It contained no numbers at all, only the names of birds she had seen that day, each followed by a single word: <i>early</i>, <i>late</i>, <i>alone</i>, <i>many</i>.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Grandmother refused to let anyone else touch the notebook.","It reveals that the notebook served a purpose different from the one the family had assumed.","It suggests that Grandmother had become confused during the final years of her life and had stopped keeping the farm's accounts.","It confirms that Grandmother kept careful records of the farm's finances."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The family believed the notebook held accounts, but it held bird observations, a surprise revealed only at the end. It doesn't confirm finances, the reason for secrecy is left implicit, and nothing suggests confusion; the entries are orderly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that the Great Wall of China is visible from the Moon with the naked eye. <u>In fact, the wall, though thousands of kilometers long, is generally less than ten meters wide, far too narrow to be seen from that distance.</u> Even from low Earth orbit, astronauts report that it is difficult to spot without magnification.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the wall would be visible from the Moon if it were somewhat longer.","It provides evidence that the Great Wall is the longest structure ever built by humans.","It corrects a popular belief by explaining why the wall cannot be seen from the Moon.","It describes the experiences of astronauts who have tried to see the wall from orbit."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence refutes the belief by pointing to the wall's narrow width. Its length is mentioned only to concede a point, the astronauts appear in the next sentence, and narrowness, not length, is the issue.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ruth Park examined why some farmers in Malawi adopted a new drought-resistant maize variety while their neighbors did not. Price and availability, she found, explained little of the difference. <u>Instead, adoption spread through social networks: farmers were far more likely to plant the new variety if a friend or relative had already grown it successfully.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an example of a farmer who adopted the new maize variety because of its low price.","It explains how the drought-resistant maize variety was developed and distributed to farmers.","It suggests that farmers who adopted the new variety were wealthier than those who did not.","It presents the factor that Park found to explain adoption after ruling out other explanations."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After dismissing price and availability, Park identifies social networks as the explanation. The sentence gives a pattern, not an individual example. It doesn't discuss development or wealth, and price was ruled out.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1610, Galileo turned his telescope toward Jupiter and observed four small points of light near the planet that changed position from night to night. He concluded that they were moons orbiting Jupiter. <u>This was a significant finding, because many scholars of the time held that everything in the heavens orbited Earth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that Galileo's contemporaries, including the scholars who believed everything orbited Earth, quickly accepted his conclusion.","It describes the method Galileo used to determine that the points of light were moons.","It explains why Galileo's observation was important in the context of beliefs held at the time.","It presents evidence that the points of light Galileo observed were not actually moons."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence explains the finding's significance: objects orbiting Jupiter contradicted the view that everything orbited Earth. It doesn't describe method or reception, and it doesn't challenge the observation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Cognitive scientist Omar Haddad studies how people estimate quantities. In one experiment, participants were shown jars of marbles and asked to guess how many each contained. Individual guesses varied wildly, some off by more than 50 percent. <u>But the average of all the guesses was typically within 5 percent of the true number.</u> Haddad argues that this "wisdom of crowds" effect depends on people guessing independently, without hearing one another's answers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a result that contrasts with the inaccuracy of individual guesses and illustrates the effect Haddad discusses.","It explains why individual participants' guesses about the number of marbles varied so widely.","It provides evidence that participants heard one another's answers before making their guesses, which explains why the average was accurate.","It describes a limitation of Haddad's experiment that may have affected his results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"But" contrasts the accurate average with the wild individual guesses, illustrating the wisdom-of-crowds effect. The sentence doesn't explain the variation, and the text implies independence rather than shared answers.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians credit the printing press with sparking the Protestant Reformation. Before printing, they note, Martin Luther's arguments would have reached only a small audience; with it, his pamphlets spread across Europe within weeks, turning a local dispute into a continental movement.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ilse Berger cautions against treating technology as the cause of the Reformation. Printing presses, she observes, also operated in Spain and Italy, where Luther's ideas gained little ground. What mattered, Berger argues, was a combination of political conditions—especially German princes eager to assert independence from Rome—that made audiences receptive.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Berger (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that printing caused the Reformation but argue that its effects were limited to Germany, where the pamphlets first circulated widely.","She would reject the idea that printing played any role in spreading Luther's ideas.","She would argue that printing alone cannot explain the Reformation's success, since it did not produce the same result everywhere.","She would argue that Luther's pamphlets actually spread more slowly than historians have claimed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Berger points to printing in Spain and Italy without a comparable result, so she would argue that printing wasn't sufficient. She doesn't deny that printing played any role, which makes the choice beginning "She would reject the…" too strong, and she doesn't dispute the speed of the spread.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Jonas Reiter argues that reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 triggered a "trophic cascade": by hunting elk and changing where elk grazed, wolves allowed willows and aspens to recover along streams, which in turn stabilized riverbanks and brought back beavers and songbirds.<br><br>Text 2<br>Studies since 2010 have found that willow recovery in Yellowstone has been patchy and modest. Ecologist Meera Shah notes that elk numbers also fell because of human hunting outside the park and severe winters, and that decades of elk browsing had lowered water tables, making it hard for willows to regrow even where elk were scarce.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Shah (Text 2) most likely respond to Reiter's claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would argue that wolves had no measurable effect on the number or behavior of elk in the park.","She would argue that the reintroduction of beavers, rather than wolves, caused the willows to recover.","She would suggest that Reiter overstates the wolves' influence, since other factors affected elk and willows.","She would agree with Reiter and offer additional evidence that willows have fully recovered along streams."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Shah cites human hunting, winters, and lowered water tables, plus patchy recovery, so she would say Reiter overstates the wolves' role. She doesn't claim wolves had no effect, she describes recovery as modest, not full, and she doesn't credit beavers.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some educators argue that students learn best when they discover principles on their own through open-ended exploration. When students construct knowledge themselves, the argument goes, they understand it more deeply and remember it longer than when they are simply told.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Daniel Ferro reviewed decades of classroom studies and found that novice learners generally performed better with explicit instruction, in which teachers demonstrate procedures and explain concepts directly. Exploration, Ferro found, benefited mainly students who already had substantial background knowledge in a subject.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ferro (Text 2) most likely respond to the educators' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would argue that students remember information longer when they discover it themselves.","He would agree that open-ended exploration is the most effective approach for all students.","He would argue that it applies at best to experienced learners and not to most beginners.","He would claim that background knowledge has little effect on how students learn."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ferro found exploration helped mainly students with background knowledge, while novices did better with explicit instruction. He would limit the educators' claim to experienced learners. The other choices contradict his findings.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Art historian Paula Greer argues that the Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer used a camera obscura—a darkened box that projects an image through a lens—to compose his paintings. The soft, out-of-focus highlights in works like <i>The Milkmaid</i>, she notes, resemble the optical effects such a device produces.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Henrik Vos acknowledges that Vermeer's paintings contain optical effects consistent with a camera obscura. But Vos points out that no inventory of Vermeer's possessions lists such a device, and that painters of his era could have observed similar effects simply by studying light closely. The visual evidence, Vos argues, is suggestive but not decisive.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Vos (Text 2) would most likely respond to Greer's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that the inventory of Vermeer's possessions proves that he never used a camera obscura.","He would grant that her evidence is relevant but argue that it does not rule out other explanations.","He would fully agree with Greer and offer the inventory as further support for her view.","He would argue that Vermeer's paintings contain no optical effects of the kind Greer describes."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Vos accepts that the effects exist ("consistent with a camera obscura") but calls the evidence "not decisive," since close observation could explain them. The inventory doesn't "prove" anything; the absence of evidence is only one point, and he doesn't fully agree.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Because octopuses can solve puzzles, use tools, and recognize individual humans, many researchers describe them as highly intelligent. Their intelligence is especially remarkable because it evolved independently of that of mammals and birds, in animals that live only a year or two.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Ruth Akande warns against measuring octopuses by human standards. Calling an octopus "intelligent" because it solves puzzles designed by people, she argues, tells us more about our own expectations than about how octopuses actually experience and navigate their world, which may involve abilities we have not thought to test.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Akande (Text 2) most likely view the evidence for octopus intelligence described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["As irrelevant, because octopuses live too briefly to develop real intelligence","As reliable only because it was gathered in the octopuses' natural habitats","As convincing proof that octopuses are more intelligent than mammals and birds, since their intelligence evolved in such short-lived animals","As potentially incomplete, because it reflects the kinds of abilities humans value and may miss others"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Akande argues that human-designed tests reflect our expectations and may miss abilities we haven't tested. She doesn't rank intelligence, she doesn't dismiss octopus cognition on the grounds of lifespan, and nothing says the tests were done in the wild.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Urban economist Felix Aranda argues that building more housing in expensive cities will lower rents over time. When supply rises, he contends, landlords must compete for tenants, and even new luxury apartments free up older units as wealthier residents move into them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Grace Liu studied neighborhoods where large numbers of new apartments were built. She found that average rents in surrounding areas did rise more slowly than citywide, but that the effect took about five years to appear and was smallest for the lowest-cost units.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Liu (Text 2) most likely respond to Aranda's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["Her findings would contradict it, since rents near new construction rose faster than in the rest of the city.","Her findings would largely support it, while suggesting that the benefits are gradual and uneven.","Her findings would neither support nor contradict it, since she did not study rents.","Her findings would support it and show that the lowest-cost units benefit most from new construction."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slower rent growth near new construction supports Aranda, but Liu's findings add that the effect is delayed and weakest for cheap units. The choice beginning "Her findings would contradict…" reverses her finding, and the choice beginning "Her findings would support…" reverses the detail about low-cost units.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The novelist Jean Rhys's <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i> (1966) retells the story of the "madwoman in the attic" from Charlotte Brontë's <i>Jane Eyre</i>, giving her a history and a voice. Critic Anna Moll praises the novel as a corrective: it exposes how Brontë's book reduced a Caribbean woman to a frightening symbol.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Tobias Wren admires Rhys's novel but resists calling it a "corrective." The label, he argues, implies that <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i> matters chiefly as a response to Brontë. Rhys's novel, in Wren's view, stands on its own as a work of art whose power does not depend on readers knowing the earlier book.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Moll (Text 1) and Wren (Text 2) would most likely agree that <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i>$q$, $q$["is a novel worthy of admiration.","fails to give the character from <i>Jane Eyre</i> a convincing voice.","should be read only by those who are familiar with <i>Jane Eyre</i>.","is valuable mainly because of its relationship to <i>Jane Eyre</i>."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Moll praises the novel and Wren admires it, so both value it. They disagree about whether its importance lies in its response to Brontë. Wren explicitly rejects the choices beginning "is valuable mainly because…" and "should be read only…", and neither critic suggests the fourth.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Physicist Lena Holm argues that nuclear fusion will become a practical energy source within twenty years. Recent experiments, she notes, have for the first time produced more energy from fusion reactions than the lasers delivered to the fuel, a milestone long considered the key scientific hurdle.<br><br>Text 2<br>Engineer Victor Osei agrees that the recent results are a genuine scientific achievement. But he points out that the lasers themselves consumed far more electricity than the reaction produced, and that a working power plant would also need to repeat the reaction many times per second for years. These engineering challenges, Osei argues, are at least as daunting as the scientific ones.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Osei (Text 2) most likely respond to Holm's prediction (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would consider it overly optimistic because the achievement she cites leaves major practical obstacles unresolved.","He would argue that fusion will never be used as a practical energy source under any circumstances.","He would argue that the recent experiments did not actually produce more energy from fusion than the lasers delivered to the fuel.","He would agree with it, since the recent experiments have solved the main challenges to fusion power."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Osei accepts the scientific milestone but stresses the overall energy balance and the need for repeated reactions, so he would find Holm's timeline too optimistic. He doesn't dispute the milestone, and "never" goes beyond anything he says.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Linguists once assumed that the earliest human languages were simple, with few words and little grammar, and that complexity developed gradually. Some still argue that the languages of small, isolated societies preserve this early simplicity.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Ama Mensah has documented languages spoken by small communities in West Africa and the Amazon. Many, she reports, have grammars more intricate than those of widely spoken languages like English, with elaborate systems for marking how a speaker knows a piece of information—whether by seeing it, hearing it, or inferring it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mensah (Text 2) most likely respond to the view that small societies' languages preserve early simplicity (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would agree, noting that these languages have fewer words than widely spoken languages.","She would argue that these languages are simple in grammar but complex in vocabulary.","She would argue that English preserves more features of early human language than other languages.","She would point to evidence that such languages can be highly complex, undermining the view."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mensah's evidence of intricate grammars in small communities contradicts the claim that their languages are simple. She says nothing about vocabulary size or English preserving early features.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many nutrition researchers recommend that adults drink eight glasses of water a day. Staying well hydrated, they argue, improves concentration, supports kidney function, and helps regulate body temperature.<br><br>Text 2<br>Physiologist Henrik Larsen notes that there is little scientific basis for the specific figure of eight glasses. Much of the water people need, he points out, comes from food and from other beverages, and healthy adults can generally rely on thirst to tell them when to drink. Larsen does not dispute that adequate hydration is important.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Larsen (Text 2) most likely respond to the recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would agree that adults should drink exactly eight glasses of water a day in addition to other beverages.","He would argue that drinking water does not affect concentration or kidney function.","He would argue that most adults should drink considerably more than eight glasses of water a day.","He would accept that hydration matters but question the need for a specific number of glasses of water."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Larsen affirms that hydration is important but says the eight-glass figure lacks support. He doesn't deny hydration's benefits, and he never suggests drinking more.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Teresa Ruiz argues that the Maya city of Tikal declined in the ninth century primarily because of prolonged drought. Lake sediments from the region record several decades of unusually low rainfall at the time the city's population fell.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Kofi Danso agrees that drought struck the region but notes that some Maya cities only a few hundred kilometers from Tikal continued to thrive through the same period. He argues that warfare among rival cities and the collapse of trade networks made Tikal especially vulnerable when drought arrived.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Danso (Text 2) most likely respond to Ruiz's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that drought alone cannot explain Tikal's decline, since other factors made the city vulnerable.","He would agree that drought was the only significant cause of the decline of Maya cities.","He would argue that Tikal's population actually grew during the period of drought.","He would dispute the evidence from lake sediments that a prolonged drought occurred in the region during the ninth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Danso accepts the drought but argues that warfare and trade collapse explain why Tikal fell while nearby cities survived. He doesn't dispute the drought, doesn't call it the only cause, and doesn't claim Tikal grew.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Composer Mariana Duarte argues that classical orchestras should commission more new works. Programs dominated by eighteenth- and nineteenth-century music, she contends, turn orchestras into museums and discourage younger audiences who want to hear music that reflects their own time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Orchestra director Paul Stein notes that when his orchestra programmed a season heavy with new works, ticket sales fell by a third. Many longtime subscribers, he observes, attend specifically to hear familiar masterpieces. Stein now pairs each new piece with a well-known symphony, an approach that has kept attendance steady while still introducing contemporary music.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Stein (Text 2) most likely respond to Duarte's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that younger audiences prefer eighteenth- and nineteenth-century music to new works.","He would agree that programming more new works reliably increases ticket sales, especially among the younger audiences Duarte hopes to attract.","He would argue that orchestras should never perform new works, since audiences prefer older music.","He would support introducing new works but caution that doing so too heavily may alienate existing audiences."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Stein still introduces new works but found that a heavy dose cut sales, so he pairs new and familiar pieces. He doesn't reject new music, and his experience contradicts the claim that new works raise sales. He says nothing about younger audiences' preferences.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers who study remote work have found that employees working from home report higher job satisfaction and complete more tasks per hour than their office-based colleagues. These findings, the researchers argue, suggest that companies should allow most employees to work remotely.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Laila Haddad notes that most studies of remote work measure individual productivity over a few months. Her research on software teams, tracked over three years, found that remote workers were promoted less often and that teams working entirely remotely produced fewer innovative products, possibly because chance conversations among colleagues became rarer.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree with it and offer her findings as further evidence that remote work increases innovation.","She would suggest that it overlooks longer-term and team-level costs not captured by the studies it relies on.","She would argue that individual productivity is higher in offices than at home.","She would argue that remote workers are actually less satisfied with their jobs than office-based workers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Haddad's long-term, team-level research reveals costs (fewer promotions, less innovation) that short-term individual studies miss. She doesn't dispute the satisfaction or productivity findings, and her findings go against innovation benefits.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some biologists argue that the bright colors of poison dart frogs evolved as a warning to predators: the vivid patterns advertise the frogs' toxicity, and predators that have had an unpleasant experience learn to avoid them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Sofia Marín studied a species of poison dart frog whose populations vary in color, from bright red to dull brown. She found that the brightest populations were not the most toxic, and that in some regions, color seemed to play a larger role in attracting mates than in deterring predators.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Marín (Text 2) most likely respond to the biologists' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that poison dart frogs are not actually toxic to predators.","She would suggest that the warning explanation may not account fully for the frogs' coloration.","She would agree that the brightest frogs are always the most toxic, as the argument predicts.","She would argue that predators are unable to see the colors of poison dart frogs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Marín's findings, that brightness doesn't track toxicity and color attracts mates, suggest the warning explanation is incomplete. She doesn't deny toxicity, her findings contradict the choice beginning "She would agree that…", and she says nothing about predators' vision.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics have praised the documentary <i>River Voices</i> for letting residents of a flood-damaged town tell their own stories, without narration. The absence of an outside narrator, they argue, gives the film an unusual intimacy and authenticity.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film scholar Naomi Brandt points out that even without narration, the director shaped the story through editing: she selected which of more than two hundred hours of interviews to include and arranged them in a particular order. The film's apparent authenticity, Brandt argues, is itself a carefully constructed effect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brandt (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that the film presents the residents' stories without any influence from the director.","She would argue that the residents' stories in the film were scripted by the director.","She would argue that the film would have been more authentic with an outside narrator.","She would argue that the critics overlook how the director's choices shape what seems authentic."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Brandt says editing shapes the film, so its authenticity is constructed, which challenges the critics' view that the absence of a narrator makes it authentic. She doesn't claim the stories were scripted, and she doesn't recommend narration.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many scientists have argued that the dinosaurs went extinct about 66 million years ago because of an asteroid impact near what is now Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula. The impact would have thrown enough debris into the atmosphere to block sunlight and collapse food chains worldwide.<br><br>Text 2<br>Geologist Aisha Kamara notes that massive volcanic eruptions in India, known as the Deccan Traps, were releasing enormous amounts of gas in the same period. She argues that the eruptions had already stressed ecosystems before the asteroid struck, and that the extinction resulted from both events together.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kamara (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that the asteroid impact was the sole cause of the dinosaurs' extinction.","She would argue that the asteroid impact did not occur at the time of the dinosaurs' extinction.","She would argue that it is incomplete because it leaves out another major contributing factor.","She would argue that the volcanic eruptions in India occurred long after the dinosaurs went extinct."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Kamara accepts the asteroid but adds the Deccan Traps as a contributing cause, so she would call the explanation incomplete. She doesn't deny the impact or call it the sole cause, and she places the eruptions in the same period.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Educational researcher Marcus Lee argues that schools should ban smartphones during the school day. Phones, he contends, distract students from lessons and reduce face-to-face interaction among peers.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Priya Nair studied schools that adopted phone bans. Test scores rose modestly at schools where phone use had previously been widespread, but changed little at schools where most students already kept their phones put away. Nair suggests that the benefits of a ban depend on how much phones were disrupting a school in the first place.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Nair (Text 2) most likely respond to Lee's recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would suggest that a ban may help some schools considerably more than others.","She would argue that phones increase face-to-face interaction among students.","She would argue that phone bans lower test scores at most schools that adopt them.","She would agree that every school should adopt a ban, since bans raise scores everywhere."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Nair found benefits only where phone use had been widespread, so a ban's value varies by school. Her findings show no score declines, contradict "raise scores everywhere," and don't address face-to-face interaction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Anthropologist Elena Kovacs argues that the spread of agriculture about 10,000 years ago improved human well-being. Farming, she notes, produced food surpluses that supported larger populations, permanent settlements, and eventually cities, writing, and specialized crafts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Skeletal remains from early farming communities tell a more mixed story. Compared with earlier hunter-gatherers, early farmers were often shorter, had more tooth decay, and showed more signs of nutritional stress and infectious disease, likely because of diets dominated by a few crops and crowded living conditions.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Kovacs's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["By suggesting that the benefits she describes for societies did not necessarily translate into better health for individuals","By arguing that hunter-gatherers developed writing and cities before the spread of agriculture","By arguing that the spread of agriculture did not actually lead to larger populations, permanent settlements, or the eventual rise of cities","By agreeing that early farmers were healthier than the hunter-gatherers who preceded them"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2's skeletal evidence shows worse individual health among early farmers, complicating Kovacs's claim that well-being improved, without denying the societal developments she lists. The choice beginning "By arguing that the…" disputes what Text 2 doesn't address, and the choice beginning "By agreeing that early…" reverses it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many writing teachers advise students to avoid the passive voice, as in "The experiment was conducted," arguing that active constructions, such as "We conducted the experiment," are clearer and more direct.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Robert Halle notes that the passive voice has legitimate uses. It allows writers to focus on the recipient of an action, to omit an unknown or unimportant actor, and to keep the topic of a paragraph in a consistent position. In scientific writing, he argues, the passive often reflects a sensible emphasis on procedures rather than on the researchers.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Halle (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' advice in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would consider it too sweeping, since the passive voice is sometimes the better choice.","He would argue that active constructions are generally less clear than passive ones.","He would agree that the passive voice should always be avoided in scientific writing, where clarity matters most.","He would argue that the passive voice should be used in all forms of writing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Halle says the passive has legitimate uses, so a blanket rule to avoid it goes too far. He doesn't call active voice generally less clear or recommend the passive everywhere, and he defends it in scientific writing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine biologist Hana Sato argues that coral reefs can adapt to warming oceans. Some corals, she notes, survived recent heat waves that killed their neighbors, and these survivors may pass heat tolerance to their offspring.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Diego Ruiz agrees that some corals are more heat-tolerant than others. But he points out that corals reproduce slowly and that heat waves are now occurring every few years. Adaptation, he argues, is unlikely to keep pace unless warming slows.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Sato (Text 1) and Ruiz (Text 2) would most likely agree that$q$, $q$["coral reefs will adapt quickly enough to survive current rates of warming.","corals reproduce quickly enough to adapt to rising temperatures.","some corals are better able to withstand heat than others.","heat waves have become less frequent in recent years."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Sato cites survivors of heat waves, and Ruiz agrees that some corals are more heat-tolerant. They disagree about whether adaptation will keep pace. Ruiz contradicts the choices beginning "coral reefs will adapt…" and "corals reproduce quickly enough…", and the choice beginning "heat waves have become…" contradicts his point about frequent heat waves.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Tech analyst Mira Jensen argues that artificial intelligence tools will eliminate most jobs in translation within a decade. Machine translation, she notes, has improved so rapidly that it now produces acceptable results for most everyday texts at almost no cost.<br><br>Text 2<br>Translator Kemal Arslan notes that demand for human translators has actually grown in recent years. Clients increasingly use machine translation for routine material, he explains, but then hire people to revise the output and to handle legal, literary, and marketing texts where nuance and accountability matter.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Arslan (Text 2) most likely respond to Jensen's prediction (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that machine translation has not improved in quality in recent years, despite claims about its rapid progress.","He would agree that demand for human translators has fallen sharply in recent years.","He would argue that clients no longer use machine translation for routine material.","He would argue that machine translation is changing translators' work rather than simply eliminating it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Arslan describes translators revising machine output and handling specialized texts, a shift in the work rather than its disappearance. He doesn't deny machine translation's improvement, and he says demand has grown and clients do use it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For much of the twentieth century, scholars dismissed the Kingdom of Kush, located in what is now Sudan, as a mere imitator of ancient Egypt, pointing to its pyramids and its adoption of Egyptian gods.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Nadia Osman argues that Kush was an innovator in its own right. Its capital at Meroë was a major center of iron production, its writing system, Meroitic, was developed independently, and Kushite rulers even conquered Egypt in the eighth century BCE, ruling it for nearly a century.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Osman (Text 2) most likely respond to the view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would argue that it overlooks Kush's distinctive achievements and its power relative to Egypt.","She would argue that Kush did not build pyramids or worship any Egyptian gods.","She would argue that Egypt imitated Kush in building pyramids and worshipping its gods, reversing the direction of influence.","She would agree that Kush borrowed nearly all of its culture from Egypt."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Osman's evidence of independent writing, iron production, and conquest of Egypt counters the "mere imitator" view. She doesn't deny the pyramids or Egyptian gods, and she doesn't claim Egypt imitated Kush.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Advocates of four-day school weeks argue that the schedule saves districts money on transportation and utilities and that students, with a long weekend to rest, return to class more focused.<br><br>Text 2<br>A study of rural districts that adopted four-day weeks found that cost savings averaged only about 1 to 2 percent of budgets. Students' math scores grew slightly more slowly than in comparable five-day districts, though attendance improved modestly.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the advocates' claims in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By suggesting that the benefits they claim are smaller or less clear-cut than they imply","By arguing that four-day weeks reduce student attendance significantly","By confirming that students in four-day districts learn more quickly in math","By arguing that four-day weeks actually cost districts more money than five-day weeks and lower attendance"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 finds small savings and slower math growth, though attendance improved, so the claimed benefits are modest and mixed. Text 2 shows savings, not higher costs, and it reports slower math growth and better attendance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Anders Lund argues that boredom is purely harmful. Bored people, his surveys show, are more likely to make impulsive purchases, overeat, and take unnecessary risks.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Bea Moreno found that participants who first completed a dull task, copying numbers from a phone book, later generated more creative uses for everyday objects than participants who had not been bored. Moreno suggests that boredom may prompt the mind to seek novelty.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Moreno (Text 2) most likely respond to Lund's view (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that dull tasks reduce people's ability to think creatively.","She would agree that boredom has no positive effects on thinking.","She would argue that it overlooks a possible benefit of boredom.","She would argue that bored people are actually less likely to take unnecessary risks."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Moreno's finding that boredom boosted creativity points to a benefit that Lund's "purely harmful" view ignores. She doesn't dispute his risk findings, and the choices beginning "She would agree that…" and "She would argue that dull…" contradict her results.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Grace Obi argues that the 1863 Emancipation Proclamation was largely symbolic. It applied only to Confederate states, where the Union had no power to enforce it, and exempted slaveholding states loyal to the Union.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Samuel Price acknowledges the proclamation's legal limits but emphasizes its practical effects. It authorized the enlistment of Black soldiers, nearly 180,000 of whom served in the Union army, and it turned the advancing Union army into an army of liberation, freeing enslaved people in each territory it captured.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Obi (Text 1) and Price (Text 2) view the Emancipation Proclamation?$q$, $q$["Obi focuses on its effect on Black soldiers, while Price focuses on its effect on loyal states.","Obi argues that it freed most enslaved people immediately, while Price argues that it freed none.","Obi emphasizes its limited legal reach, while Price emphasizes its concrete consequences.","Obi believes it was unconstitutional, while Price believes it was fully enforceable."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Obi stresses that the proclamation couldn't be enforced where it applied, while Price stresses enlistment and liberation as the army advanced. Neither makes the claims in the choices beginning "Obi argues that it…" and "Obi believes it was…", and the choice beginning "Obi focuses on its…" reverses their focuses.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some nutrition experts recommend eating breakfast every day, citing studies showing that people who skip breakfast tend to weigh more than those who eat it.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Tomasz Wolski conducted a trial in which participants were randomly assigned either to eat breakfast or to skip it for sixteen weeks. Weight change did not differ significantly between the groups. Wolski notes that observational studies cannot rule out the possibility that people who eat breakfast differ in other ways, such as exercise or sleep habits.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Wolski (Text 2) most likely respond to the experts' recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that eating breakfast leads to lower body weight.","He would argue that skipping breakfast causes people to lose weight.","He would argue that the studies they cite do not show that eating breakfast itself affects weight.","He would argue that people who eat breakfast exercise less than those who skip it, which explains why they weigh more."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Wolski's randomized trial found no difference, and he notes that observational studies can't separate breakfast from other habits. He doesn't claim skipping breakfast causes weight loss, and he only speculates that other habits may differ, without specifying how.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Architect Nadia Rahim argues that cities should preserve historic buildings whenever possible. Older structures, she says, give neighborhoods character and a sense of continuity that new construction cannot replicate.<br><br>Text 2<br>Urban planner Leo Grant agrees that historic buildings have value, but he notes that strict preservation rules in some cities have blocked new housing in neighborhoods where it is badly needed, driving up rents. Grant proposes protecting the most significant buildings while allowing more flexibility elsewhere.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to Rahim's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that her approach, applied broadly, can have costs that justify a more selective policy.","He would argue that historic buildings contribute nothing to a neighborhood's character.","He would agree that every historic building should be preserved regardless of housing needs.","He would argue that new construction gives neighborhoods more character than old buildings do, so preservation rules should be eliminated."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Grant values historic buildings but warns that broad preservation can block housing, and he proposes selective protection. He doesn't dismiss their value, and he doesn't make the comparative claim in the choice beginning "He would argue that new…".$q$),
  ($q$cs-h430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Biologist Yuki Tanaka argues that the songs of humpback whales serve mainly to attract females. Only males sing, she notes, and singing peaks during the breeding season.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Omar Bakr observed that female humpbacks rarely approach singing males. Instead, singers are most often joined by other males, sometimes leading to brief contests. Bakr suggests that songs may function primarily in interactions among males.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bakr (Text 2) most likely respond to Tanaka's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that humpback songs have no connection to the breeding season.","He would argue that observed behavior around singers does not fit well with her proposed function.","He would argue that female humpbacks also sing during the breeding season.","He would agree that songs attract females, since females often approach singing males and remain with them through the breeding season."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Bakr observed that females rarely approach singers while males often do, which undercuts the mate-attraction idea. He doesn't claim females sing or deny the link to breeding season, and the choice beginning "He would agree that…" contradicts his observation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Poet and critic Lena Ward argues that poetry should be read aloud. Its music, she says—rhythm, rhyme, and the sounds of words—is essential to its meaning and is lost when poems are read silently.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Haruto Mori agrees that sound is important in much poetry, but he notes that many modern poets arrange words visually on the page, using line breaks, spacing, and even shapes that cannot be conveyed by speaking. For such poems, Mori argues, silent reading may reveal meanings that a performance would obscure.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mori (Text 2) most likely respond to Ward's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that it does not apply equally well to all poems.","He would argue that rhyme is more important than rhythm in modern poetry.","He would agree that all poetry should be read aloud to be understood.","He would argue that sound plays no role in the meaning of poetry."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mori accepts that sound matters in much poetry but says visually arranged poems may be better read silently, so Ward's claim doesn't hold for all poems. He doesn't deny sound's role or rank rhyme and rhythm.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>A 2019 study found that people who spent at least two hours per week in nature reported better health and well-being than those who spent less time outdoors. The authors recommend that doctors encourage patients to spend regular time in green spaces.<br><br>Text 2<br>Public health researcher Ama Boateng praises the study but notes that it relied on participants' own reports of their health and time outdoors. People who already feel healthy, she points out, may be more inclined to spend time in nature, so the direction of the relationship is uncertain.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boateng (Text 2) most likely respond to the authors' recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that the study shows conclusively that time in nature improves health, since it surveyed so many participants.","She would argue that participants spent much more time outdoors than they reported.","She would argue that spending time in nature harms people's health.","She would caution that the study's findings may not show that time in nature causes better health."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Boateng raises the possibility of reverse causation and notes the reliance on self-reports, which questions whether nature causes the health benefit. She doesn't call the evidence conclusive, claim harm, or claim underreporting.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economists often assume that people value a dollar saved the same as a dollar earned. On this view, a shopper should be equally willing to drive across town to save $10 on a $20 item or on a $1,000 item.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky found that people were much more willing to make a trip to save $5 on a $15 purchase than to save $5 on a $125 purchase. People, they concluded, evaluate savings relative to the price of an item rather than in absolute terms.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kahneman and Tversky (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would argue that people are more willing to travel to save money on expensive items.","They would argue that people value a dollar saved more than a dollar earned in all situations.","They would argue that their findings show people do not actually behave as the assumption predicts.","They would agree that people are equally willing to travel to save the same amount on any item."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Their finding shows people treat the same $5 savings differently depending on the price, contradicting the assumption. They don't make the second claim, and the choice beginning "They would argue that people are…" reverses their result.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Geologist Ruth Fischer argues that the giant stone circles at Nabta Playa in Egypt, built around 7,000 years ago, served as a calendar. Some stones, she notes, align with the position of the rising sun at the summer solstice, which coincided with the arrival of seasonal rains.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Kwasi Adu notes that only a few of the many stones at the site align with astronomical events, and that with so many stones, some alignments would occur by chance. Adu does not rule out an astronomical purpose but argues that the evidence is weaker than often claimed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Adu (Text 2) most likely respond to Fischer's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would consider it possible but insufficiently supported by the alignments she cites.","He would argue that the site was built long after the seasonal rains ended.","He would agree that the alignments prove the stones were used as a calendar.","He would argue that none of the stones at Nabta Playa align with astronomical events."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adu doesn't rule out Fischer's idea but argues that a few alignments among many stones could be chance. He accepts that some stones align, says nothing about timing, and doesn't think the alignments prove her claim.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some sports scientists recommend that young athletes specialize in a single sport early, arguing that thousands of hours of focused practice are needed to reach elite levels.<br><br>Text 2<br>A study of more than 1,500 elite athletes found that most played multiple sports in childhood and specialized only in their mid-teens. Early specialists were more likely to suffer overuse injuries and to quit their sport before adulthood.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By agreeing that early specialization is the most reliable path to elite performance","By presenting evidence that early specialization may be unnecessary for elite success and may carry risks","By arguing that practice has no effect on athletic performance","By arguing that athletes who play multiple sports as children are more likely to suffer overuse injuries than early specialists are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 shows that most elite athletes specialized late and that early specialists faced more injuries and dropouts. It doesn't claim practice is irrelevant, and the choice beginning "By arguing that athletes…" reverses its finding.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary scholar Inés Calderón reads Emily Dickinson's frequent dashes as signs of hesitation and uncertainty, moments where the speaker pauses, unable to finish a thought.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Paul Okonkwo argues that Dickinson's dashes are not hesitations but connections. By refusing to separate her phrases with periods or commas, Okonkwo contends, Dickinson links ideas that conventional punctuation would divide, inviting readers to see relationships among them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Calderón (Text 1) and Okonkwo (Text 2) interpret Dickinson's dashes?$q$, $q$["Calderón sees them as errors, while Okonkwo sees them as deliberate artistic choices.","Calderón sees them as interruptions in thought, while Okonkwo sees them as ways of joining ideas.","Calderón sees them as unimportant to the poems' meaning, while Okonkwo sees them as the single key to understanding Dickinson's work.","Calderón sees them as connections, while Okonkwo sees them as signs of uncertainty."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Calderón reads the dashes as pauses or hesitations, while Okonkwo reads them as links between ideas. Neither calls them errors or unimportant, and the choice beginning "Calderón sees them as connections…" reverses the two views.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some engineers argue that self-driving cars will dramatically reduce traffic deaths, since more than 90 percent of crashes involve human error.<br><br>Text 2<br>Safety researcher Mara Ellis notes that the statistic about human error includes crashes in which a human was only partly at fault, and that automated systems introduce new kinds of errors, such as failing to recognize unusual objects. Ellis expects safety gains from automation but argues that their size is still highly uncertain.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ellis (Text 2) most likely respond to the engineers' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would suggest that it overstates the likely benefit by relying on a statistic that is easily misread.","She would argue that self-driving cars will make roads more dangerous than they are now, because of the new kinds of errors they introduce.","She would argue that human error plays almost no role in traffic crashes.","She would agree that self-driving cars will eliminate more than 90 percent of crashes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ellis says the 90 percent figure is misleading and that automation brings new errors. She expects gains but of uncertain size, so she would see the engineers' claim as overstated. She doesn't predict more danger or deny human error.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Lucía Vega argues that the Silk Road's greatest impact was economic: it moved silk, spices, and precious metals across Eurasia, enriching merchants and the cities along its routes.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ibrahim Saleh contends that the routes' most lasting legacy was the movement of ideas. Buddhism traveled from India to China along them, as did technologies such as papermaking, which moved westward from China to the Islamic world and eventually to Europe.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Vega (Text 1) and Saleh (Text 2) would most likely agree that the Silk Road$q$, $q$["had little effect on the economies of the cities along its routes.","was used mainly for transporting religious texts.","had significant effects on the societies connected by its routes.","moved technologies only from west to east."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both historians describe major effects, economic for Vega and intellectual for Saleh, though they differ on which was greatest. The other choices contradict one or both texts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Freya Holm argues that planting trees on a massive scale is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change, since trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scientist Rajesh Menon notes that planting trees in grasslands and savannas, which naturally have few trees, can destroy ecosystems that store large amounts of carbon in their soils. He also points out that young trees absorb relatively little carbon for decades. Menon supports protecting existing forests as a higher priority.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Menon (Text 2) most likely respond to Holm's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would caution that large-scale planting can have drawbacks and may not be the best priority.","He would argue that existing forests release more carbon than they absorb, so planting new trees is the only effective strategy.","He would argue that trees do not absorb carbon dioxide as they grow.","He would agree that trees should be planted in grasslands and savannas."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Menon points to harm to grasslands and slow carbon uptake by young trees, and he prioritizes protecting forests. He doesn't deny absorption, opposes planting in grasslands, and says nothing about forests releasing carbon.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians describe the Middle Ages in Europe as a period of scientific stagnation, when learning was confined to copying ancient texts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Seb Falk points to medieval innovations such as mechanical clocks, eyeglasses, and the astrolabe-based instruments that monks used to track time and the stars. Medieval scholars, Falk argues, did not merely preserve ancient knowledge but extended it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Falk (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' characterization in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would argue that ancient scholars, rather than medieval ones, invented mechanical clocks, eyeglasses, and astrolabe-based instruments.","He would argue that medieval monks had no interest in astronomy.","He would argue that it ignores evidence of genuine medieval invention and scholarship.","He would agree that medieval scholars only copied ancient texts."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Falk cites medieval inventions to show scholars extended knowledge, which contradicts the stagnation view. The other choices contradict his examples or are unsupported.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many teachers believe that students have distinct "learning styles"—visual, auditory, or kinesthetic—and that instruction matched to a student's style improves learning.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Harold Pashler and colleagues reviewed the research on learning styles. While many people express preferences for one mode of instruction, they found almost no well-designed studies showing that matching instruction to those preferences improved learning outcomes.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Pashler and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' belief (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["They would say that while students may have preferences, matching instruction to them is not shown to help.","They would agree that matching instruction to students' learning styles reliably improves outcomes, as many teachers believe.","They would argue that students have no preferences about how they receive instruction.","They would argue that visual instruction is superior for all students."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The review acknowledges preferences but finds no good evidence that matching helps. It doesn't deny preferences, rejects the choice beginning "They would agree that…", and doesn't favor visual instruction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine scientist Elise Moreau argues that deep-sea mining for metals such as cobalt and nickel should be banned. The seafloor ecosystems where these metals occur, she notes, are poorly understood, and damage from mining could take thousands of years to recover.<br><br>Text 2<br>Engineer Tomas Lindqvist acknowledges the ecological risks but notes that metals for electric-vehicle batteries must come from somewhere, and that land-based mines have also caused serious environmental and human harm. He argues for strict regulation of deep-sea mining rather than an outright ban.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lindqvist (Text 2) most likely respond to Moreau's proposal (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would accept that deep-sea mining carries risks but argue that a ban ignores the costs of the alternatives.","He would argue that land-based mines cause no environmental harm.","He would agree that deep-sea mining should be banned completely.","He would argue that deep-sea mining poses no real risk to seafloor ecosystems, since land-based mines cause far more serious harm."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lindqvist grants the risks but notes that land mining also does harm, so he favors regulation over a ban. The other choices contradict his acknowledgment of risks or his comments about land mines.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>In her study of letters written by nineteenth-century immigrants to the United States, historian Anja Novak emphasizes their optimism. Writers frequently urged relatives to join them, describing high wages and plentiful land.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Carlos Mena cautions that surviving immigrant letters may not represent immigrants' experiences as a whole. Letters urging relatives to emigrate were often saved and even published by shipping companies as advertising, while letters describing hardship were less likely to be preserved.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mena (Text 2) most likely respond to Novak's emphasis on optimism (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that nineteenth-century immigrants never described high wages in their letters.","He would suggest that the optimism may partly reflect which letters happened to survive.","He would argue that shipping companies wrote most of the letters themselves.","He would agree that the surviving letters accurately represent all immigrants' experiences."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mena notes that hopeful letters were preferentially preserved, so the optimism in the record may be partly a product of selection. He doesn't deny that the letters describe wages, and he doesn't claim companies wrote the letters.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Neuroscientist Karin Holt argues that adults cannot grow new neurons in the hippocampus, a brain region involved in memory. Her team examined brain tissue from adults and found almost no young neurons.<br><br>Text 2<br>Neuroscientist Maura Boldrini's team, using different methods to preserve brain tissue, found thousands of young neurons in the hippocampi of adults, even in people in their seventies. Boldrini suggests that differences in how tissue is handled after death may explain the conflicting results.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boldrini (Text 2) most likely respond to Holt's conclusion (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would agree that adults cannot grow new neurons in the hippocampus.","She would argue that new neurons are found only in people under the age of seventy, which explains why Holt found almost none.","She would argue that the hippocampus plays no role in memory.","She would suggest that Holt's findings may reflect methodological factors rather than an absence of new neurons."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Boldrini found many young neurons and suggests that tissue handling explains the conflict, implying Holt's result may be an artifact. She found neurons even in people in their seventies, and she doesn't dispute the hippocampus's role.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics of video games often argue that they encourage social isolation, as players spend hours alone in front of screens instead of interacting with others.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Nicole Banks surveyed more than 2,000 teenagers and found that most of their gaming time was spent playing online with friends, often while talking over headsets. Many described games as a main way of staying in touch with friends who had moved away.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Banks (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that it doesn't reflect how many teenagers actually play video games.","She would argue that teenagers spend very little time playing video games.","She would argue that face-to-face interaction is less valuable than online interaction.","She would agree that video games lead most teenagers to become socially isolated."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Banks found gaming is largely social, which contradicts the isolation claim. She doesn't measure total time or rank types of interaction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Linguist Peter Lowe argues that texting and social media are degrading young people's writing skills, pointing to the abbreviations and missing punctuation common in online messages.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Gretchen McCulloch argues that internet writing is a distinct, informal register, much like casual speech. People who use abbreviations online, she notes, can still write formally when a situation calls for it, just as people who speak casually with friends can speak formally at a job interview.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would McCulloch (Text 2) most likely respond to Lowe's concern (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that formal writing should include abbreviations used online.","She would agree that online writing has made many young people unable to write formally, though she considers the change harmless.","She would argue that informal online writing does not necessarily indicate a loss of formal writing ability.","She would argue that young people rarely use abbreviations in online messages."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$McCulloch treats online writing as a separate register that coexists with formal skills, which counters Lowe's view of degradation. She doesn't agree with him, doesn't recommend abbreviations in formal writing, and doesn't deny their use.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some agricultural scientists argue that organic farming is better for the environment than conventional farming, since it avoids synthetic pesticides and fertilizers that can pollute soil and water.<br><br>Text 2<br>Agronomist Lars Peters notes that organic farms typically produce 20 to 25 percent less food per acre than conventional farms. Feeding the same population organically, he argues, could require clearing more land, which would destroy habitat and release stored carbon—possibly outweighing organic farming's local benefits.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Peters (Text 2) most likely respond to the scientists' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that organic farming is clearly better for the environment overall.","He would argue that it may overlook an environmental cost that arises from organic farming's lower yields.","He would argue that organic farms actually produce more food per acre than conventional farms, so no additional land would need to be cleared.","He would argue that synthetic pesticides cause no pollution of soil or water."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Peters points to lower yields leading to more land clearing, a cost the scientists' argument overlooks. He doesn't deny pesticide pollution, doesn't fully agree, and states the opposite of the choice beginning "He would argue that organic…".$q$),
  ($q$cs-h448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Art critic Renée Dubois argues that the value of a painting lies entirely in what viewers can see. Knowing who made it, when, or why, she contends, should not affect our judgment of its beauty.<br><br>Text 2<br>Philosopher Arthur Kemp points out that when a celebrated painting was revealed to be a forgery, the image itself did not change, yet critics' assessment of it collapsed. Kemp argues that our appreciation of art inevitably depends on knowing how it was made, including the skill and originality it represents.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kemp (Text 2) most likely respond to Dubois's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that viewers should judge paintings only on what they can see, since the forged image itself did not change.","He would argue that the forged painting was more beautiful than the original.","He would argue that critics are generally unable to recognize forgeries.","He would argue that the forgery case shows that knowledge of a work's origins does shape how it is judged."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Kemp uses the collapsed assessment of an unchanged image to show that origins matter to appreciation, which challenges Dubois. He doesn't rank the forgery's beauty or generalize about critics' ability to detect forgeries.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1950s, many doctors prescribed bed rest for patients recovering from heart attacks, sometimes for six weeks or more. <u>Cardiologist Samuel Levine challenged this practice, seating patients in armchairs within days and finding that they recovered as well as or better than those kept in bed.</u> Today, early movement is a standard part of cardiac care.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that patients kept in bed for six weeks recovered faster than others.","It describes a challenge to an established practice that foreshadows a later change in standard care.","It describes a practice that is still recommended for patients recovering from heart attacks.","It explains why doctors in the 1950s believed that long periods of bed rest helped heart patients heal fully."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Levine's armchair approach challenged bed rest, and the final sentence shows that early movement became standard. The sentence doesn't explain doctors' reasoning, and the choices beginning "It presents evidence that…" and "It describes a practice…" reverse its findings.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1911 novel. Mrs. Abbot is speaking to her son.<br><br>"Of course you must do as you think best," she said, folding her napkin into a smaller and smaller square. "I wouldn't dream of standing in your way. It's only that your father always said the city ruins young men. But you're grown now, and I'm sure you know better than your father did."$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of Mrs. Abbot's statement in the text?$q$, $q$["It expresses disapproval of her son's plans while appearing to accept them.","It shows that she has no strong opinion about where her son chooses to live.","It indicates that she agrees with her son that his father's views were mistaken.","It reveals that she plans to move to the city with her son."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$She claims she won't stand in his way but invokes his father's warning and adds a pointed remark, which signals disapproval behind apparent acceptance. Her fidgeting with the napkin reinforces the tension. The other choices take her words at face value or invent a plan.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have long debated whether Neanderthals created art. Paintings in three Spanish caves, including a ladder-like red design and hand stencils, have been dated using uranium in the mineral crusts that formed over them. <u>The dates indicate that the paintings are at least 64,000 years old, about 20,000 years before modern humans arrived in Europe.</u> If the dating is correct, Neanderthals must have made them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the subject matter of the paintings found in three Spanish caves.","It presents evidence that modern humans arrived in Europe much earlier than previously believed, about 64,000 years ago.","It explains the method that researchers used to date the paintings in the Spanish caves.","It presents a result that, if accurate, supports a particular answer to the question raised in the first sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The dates precede modern humans' arrival, which leads to the conclusion that Neanderthals made the art. The dating method is described in the previous sentence, and the sentence implies nothing about earlier arrival.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most ant species forage individually, with each worker searching on its own and recruiting others only after finding food. Army ants, by contrast, hunt in massive coordinated raids of up to several hundred thousand workers that sweep across the forest floor. <u>So effective are these raids that many other animals, including antbirds and certain flies, follow the swarms to catch the insects fleeing from them.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that army ants depend on antbirds to locate their prey.","It illustrates the impact of army ant raids by describing how other species exploit them.","It explains how army ant workers recruit hundreds of thousands of other workers to join a raid after one of them finds food.","It provides an example of an ant species that forages individually."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Other animals following the raids shows how powerful the raids are. The sentence doesn't describe recruitment or individual foraging, and the dependency runs the other way: birds rely on ants.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, many New Yorkers doubted that it was safe. A week after the opening, a rumor that the bridge was collapsing caused a stampede that killed twelve people. <u>The following year, showman P. T. Barnum led twenty-one elephants across the bridge, a spectacle widely reported as proof of its strength.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the bridge was later damaged by the weight of the elephants.","It explains why a rumor that the bridge was collapsing caused a deadly stampede a week after the bridge opened to the public.","It provides evidence that engineers had underestimated the bridge's strength.","It describes an event that addressed the public doubts mentioned earlier in the text."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Barnum's elephant parade was presented as proof of strength, answering the doubts and fears described earlier. It doesn't explain the stampede, imply damage, or concern engineers' estimates.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that photographs taken from airplanes or satellites are recent tools for archaeology. In fact, archaeologists began using aerial photography in the 1910s, when pilots flying over England noticed patterns in crop fields: <u>plants growing over buried walls were shorter and ripened earlier, revealing the outlines of ancient buildings invisible from the ground.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how early aerial observers were able to detect structures beneath the ground.","It describes the kinds of crops most commonly grown in England in the 1910s.","It suggests that buried walls cause crops to grow taller than surrounding plants.","It presents evidence that aerial photography is a recent development in archaeology, first used only after satellites became available."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The portion explains the crop patterns that revealed buried walls. It doesn't support the "recent" assumption the text rejects, isn't about crop types, and the choice beginning "It suggests that buried…" reverses "shorter."$q$),
  ($q$cs-h455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Kiri Ngata studies the kea, a parrot native to New Zealand's mountains. Keas are known for their curiosity, sometimes dismantling car parts in parking lots. <u>In experiments, Ngata found that keas could use probabilities to make choices, picking from a jar with more of their preferred tokens even when the jar was larger overall.</u> This ability was previously documented only in humans and great apes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an example of the kea's habit of dismantling car parts in parking lots.","It suggests that keas are more intelligent than humans and great apes.","It explains why keas prefer some kinds of tokens over others in experiments.","It presents a finding that the final sentence reveals to be unusual among animals."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The probabilistic reasoning finding is put in context by the final sentence: it was previously seen only in humans and apes. The sentence doesn't concern car parts or explain preferences, and the text makes no claim that keas are smarter than humans.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, the Great Pacific Garbage Patch was described as an island of plastic visible from space. In reality, much of the debris consists of tiny fragments spread through the water. <u>A 2018 survey using aircraft and trawling nets estimated that the patch contains about 80,000 metric tons of plastic, much of it in larger objects such as fishing nets, which accounted for nearly half of the total mass.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It adds quantitative detail that further complicates the popular image of the patch.","It suggests that the amount of plastic in the patch has decreased since 2018.","It explains why fishing nets break down more slowly than other kinds of plastic.","It confirms that the patch is a solid island of plastic visible from space."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The survey's estimate, including the large share made up of fishing nets, adds nuance beyond both the island image and the "tiny fragments" description. It doesn't confirm the island myth, explain breakdown rates, or describe a trend.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Painter Kerry James Marshall has said that when he visited museums as a young man, he saw almost no images of Black people in the paintings on display. <u>Since the 1980s, his large canvases have depicted Black figures in scenes of everyday life—barbecues, beauty salons, couples in parks—rendered in the grand scale traditionally reserved for history paintings.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why museums in the past collected few paintings depicting Black people and how that has changed since the 1980s.","It describes Marshall's work in a way that suggests a response to the absence he noticed in museums.","It suggests that Marshall's paintings have been rejected by major museums.","It provides examples of history paintings that Marshall saw as a young man."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Marshall's monumental paintings of Black life answer the absence he observed. The sentence doesn't explain museums' collecting, describe the paintings he saw, or discuss rejection.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most of the water on Earth's surface is salt water. Desalination plants can convert seawater into drinking water, but the process uses large amounts of energy. <u>Engineers in Chile are now testing plants powered entirely by solar panels in the Atacama Desert, one of the sunniest places on Earth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that most of Earth's surface water is salt water.","It explains how desalination plants convert seawater into drinking water and why the process requires so much energy.","It suggests that desalination requires less energy in sunny locations.","It describes an effort that may address a drawback mentioned in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solar-powered plants could address the energy cost of desalination. The sentence doesn't explain the process or support the saltwater fact, and it doesn't say desalination itself uses less energy in the sun.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Elena Rossi asked participants to estimate how long it would take them to complete various tasks, from writing a report to assembling furniture. Most underestimated, often by half. <u>Strikingly, participants underestimated even when they had completed similar tasks before and could recall how long those had taken.</u> Rossi suggests that people focus on the specific plan for a task and neglect their own past experience.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the kinds of tasks that participants were asked to estimate in the study.","It explains why participants overestimated the time needed for some tasks.","It presents a detail that makes the participants' errors more surprising and leads to Rossi's explanation.","It suggests that participants who had completed similar tasks before made more accurate estimates than participants who had not."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Underestimating despite relevant experience is surprising, and it supports Rossi's claim that people neglect past experience. The choice beginning "It suggests that participants…" reverses the finding, the tasks are described earlier, and the participants underestimated rather than overestimated.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1960s, Jane Goodall reported that chimpanzees at Gombe, Tanzania, stripped leaves from twigs and used them to fish termites from mounds. <u>The finding startled scientists, many of whom defined humans as the only toolmakers.</u> Goodall's mentor, Louis Leakey, famously replied that scientists would now have to redefine "tool," redefine "man," or accept chimpanzees as humans.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that other scientists had already observed chimpanzees making tools before Goodall reported her findings at Gombe.","It describes the method Goodall used to observe chimpanzees at Gombe.","It explains why Goodall's observation challenged a prevailing view of what distinguishes humans.","It presents evidence that chimpanzees use tools more skillfully than humans do."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence shows that the finding was startling because it contradicted the definition of humans as the only toolmakers, which sets up Leakey's remark. It doesn't describe methods, prior observations, or relative skill.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Hydrologist Priya Raman studies "atmospheric rivers," long, narrow bands of water vapor that carry moisture from the tropics. A single atmospheric river can transport more water than the Amazon River. <u>These storms supply up to half of California's annual precipitation, refilling reservoirs after droughts.</u> But the strongest ones can also cause catastrophic floods, a danger Raman expects to grow as the climate warms.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that atmospheric rivers have become less common in California as the climate has warmed over recent decades.","It compares the size of atmospheric rivers with that of the Amazon River.","It establishes a benefit of atmospheric rivers before the text turns to the danger they pose.","It explains how atmospheric rivers form over tropical oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence describes how atmospheric rivers help California, and "But" then introduces flooding risk. Formation and the Amazon comparison appear elsewhere, and nothing indicates declining frequency.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1929 novel.<br><br>The village had one street, and on it stood the post office, the church, the general store, and Mrs. Gale's house. It was generally agreed that of these four, Mrs. Gale's was the most reliable source of news.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that Mrs. Gale worked at the general store and spoke with all its customers.","It introduces a note of humor by suggesting that Mrs. Gale is the village's chief gossip.","It explains why the post office was the most important building in the village.","It indicates that the village church was rarely attended by the villagers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ranking Mrs. Gale's house above the post office and other institutions as a source of news humorously implies that she gossips. Nothing suggests the post office was most important, that she worked at the store, or that the church was unattended.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Hamid Rezaei studied why some neighborhoods recovered faster than others after a severe 1995 heat wave in Chicago. Neighborhoods with similar poverty rates had very different death rates. <u>Rezaei found that neighborhoods with busy sidewalks, local shops, and active community groups had far fewer deaths, since neighbors were more likely to check on elderly residents.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the weather conditions that caused the heat wave in Chicago.","It identifies a factor that helps explain the difference described in the previous sentence.","It provides evidence that poverty was the main cause of deaths during the heat wave.","It suggests that elderly residents were less affected by the heat wave than younger ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Social connection explains why neighborhoods with similar poverty had different death rates. The previous sentence rules out poverty as the explanation, and the sentence says nothing about weather or age differences in risk.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Classical music recordings once fit on a single side of a vinyl record, limited to about twenty-two minutes. When the compact disc was developed in the late 1970s, engineers debated how much music it should hold. <u>According to a widely repeated story, an executive insisted that a disc hold all of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, which runs about seventy minutes.</u> The final standard of seventy-four minutes accommodated even slow performances of the work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a popular account of how a particular requirement influenced a technical decision.","It explains how engineers developed the technology used in the compact disc.","It provides evidence that vinyl records could hold all of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony.","It suggests that the compact disc was designed mainly for recordings of popular music rather than for long classical works."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The story links the Ninth Symphony's length to the CD's capacity, which the final sentence confirms. Vinyl held only about twenty-two minutes per side, and the sentence doesn't explain the technology or claim the CD was designed for popular music.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h465$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have found that bilingual children often outperform monolingual children on tasks that require switching attention between rules. Some have concluded that speaking two languages strengthens general mental control. <u>However, large studies that carefully matched children by family income and education have often found the advantage shrinking or disappearing.</u> The question remains actively debated.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why bilingual children outperform monolingual children on tasks that require switching attention between rules.","It confirms that bilingualism strengthens general mental control in all children.","It describes the tasks that researchers used to measure children's attention.","It presents evidence that casts doubt on the conclusion described in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Matched studies weakening the advantage cast doubt on the conclusion that bilingualism strengthens control. The sentence doesn't confirm or explain the advantage, and the tasks are described earlier.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h466$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people picture the Sahara as an endless sea of sand dunes. Yet dunes cover only about a quarter of the desert. <u>Much of the rest consists of gravel plains, rocky plateaus, dry valleys, and mountains, some of which rise more than 3,000 meters and occasionally receive snow.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that the Sahara's mountains receive more snow than rain each year, unlike the rest of the desert.","It supports the correction of a common image by describing the desert's other landscapes.","It suggests that the Sahara's mountains are covered by sand dunes.","It explains why sand dunes cover only about a quarter of the Sahara."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Describing gravel plains, plateaus, and mountains supports the point that the Sahara isn't mostly dunes. The sentence doesn't explain why, "occasionally" doesn't support the snow claim, and the mountains are presented as separate from dunes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h467$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ines Moreau studies "food deserts," areas without nearby supermarkets. Policymakers have often assumed that opening a supermarket in such an area would improve residents' diets. <u>Moreau tracked purchases before and after new supermarkets opened in dozens of such neighborhoods and found that residents' diets changed very little.</u> She suggests that preferences and prices, not just distance, shape what people buy.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It confirms that opening supermarkets in food deserts substantially improves residents' diets.","It presents a finding that challenges an assumption and prompts Moreau's alternative explanation.","It describes how Moreau defined the term \"food desert\" in her research.","It explains why supermarkets have avoided opening stores in some neighborhoods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The finding of little dietary change challenges policymakers' assumption and leads to Moreau's suggestion about preferences and prices. It doesn't confirm the assumption, explain store locations, or define the term.$q$),
  ($q$cs-h468$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1932, the physicist James Chadwick discovered the neutron, a particle with no electric charge. <u>Because it carries no charge, a neutron is not repelled by the positively charged nucleus of an atom and can penetrate it easily.</u> This property made the neutron an ideal tool for splitting atoms, a process that within a decade would lead to nuclear reactors.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that neutrons are attracted to the negatively charged electrons of an atom.","It describes the experiment that Chadwick used to discover the neutron in 1932.","It explains a property of the neutron that accounts for the later use described in the final sentence.","It presents evidence that the first nuclear reactors were developed before the neutron was discovered, using other particles to split atoms."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The neutron's lack of charge lets it penetrate nuclei, which explains why it was useful for splitting atoms. The sentence doesn't describe Chadwick's experiment, the choice beginning "It suggests that neutrons…" is unsupported, and the choice beginning "It presents evidence that…" reverses the chronology.$q$),
  ($q$ei-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for nearly a year. ______ commuters were forced to take a lengthy detour through downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The detour was a consequence of the bridge closure, so a cause-and-effect transition like 'As a result' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals. ______ only two met every funding requirement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Additionally,","Likewise,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence states the final outcome of the review process, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$)
on conflict (id) do update set
  module = excluded.module,
  domain = excluded.domain,
  skill = excluded.skill,
  difficulty = excluded.difficulty,
  passage = excluded.passage,
  prompt = excluded.prompt,
  choices = excluded.choices,
  answer = excluded.answer,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;
